var e=JSON.parse('[[1701,"When an application is uninstalled, the process depends on the operating system. On iOS, uninstalling an application normally removes all its data and documents from its sandbox. On Android the behaviour can differ: the application\'s data in its dedicated directory is removed, but some data (for example, media files in shared storage) may remain. On computers, uninstalling often leaves configuration files and caches in the user\'s folders. It is always worth checking whether any important files remain.","When a user uninstalls an application, they expect it and all the data associated with it to disappear. The actual behaviour, however, depends on the platform (iOS, Android, desktop) and on how the application itself is implemented.\\nMobile platforms (iOS and Android)\\nOn mobile devices applications usually run in an isolated environment called a sandbox. That means the application\'s files are stored in an area of the file system dedicated to it.\\n\\n- iOS: Uninstalling an application from the Springboard (the home screen) removes its whole bundle (.app) and all the data in its sandbox (the Documents, Library and tmp directories). Data in iCloud or the Keychain may persist if it was synchronised.\\n- Android: A standard uninstall through the settings removes the APK file, the application\'s data in its internal storage (/data/data/[package_name]) and the cache. Files the application saved in external storage (for example, in the Pictures or Downloads folders), however, usually remain. Some applications may ask the user whether to clear that data on uninstall.\\nDesktop operating systems (Windows, macOS, Linux)\\nThe process here is less strict. An uninstaller or package manager may remove the main executable files but often leaves traces:\\n\\n- Configuration files and user settings in the home directory (for example, ~/.config or AppData).\\n- Caches, logs and temporary files.\\n- Libraries installed globally, if they are used by other programs.\\nFor a complete cleanup you often need specialised uninstall tools or to clean the system folders manually.\\nA practical example (Android)\\nConsider how an application can save files in different places, which affects their removal.\\n\\nThe first file is in the application\'s private area and will be removed by the system. The second file is saved in a publicly accessible location and will most likely remain on the device.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding this process is critically important for developers, in order to manage the life cycle of the user\'s data properly, provide confidentiality and not clutter the device. For users this knowledge helps them uninstall applications deliberately and clean up any remaining files manually when necessary.","// An example in Kotlin for Android\\n// Saving a file in internal storage (it will be removed)\\nval internalFile = File(context.filesDir, \\"secret.txt\\")\\ninternalFile.writeText(\\"This data will be removed along with the application.\\")\\n\\n// Saving a file in external, shared storage (it may remain)\\nval externalFile = File(Environment.getExternalStoragePublicDirectory(\\n    Environment.DIRECTORY_DOCUMENTS), \\"report.pdf\\")\\nexternalFile.writeText(\\"This file may survive the application being uninstalled.\\")"],[1702,"A class is a concrete implementation describing objects\' state (fields) and behaviour (methods). A protocol (or interface) is a contract that only declares methods without implementing them. A class can inherit from another class, acquiring its implementation, or it can conform to a protocol, committing to implement its methods. Protocols provide polymorphism without tying you to a particular inheritance hierarchy.","In object-oriented programming, classes and protocols (often called interfaces in languages such as Java or C#) serve different but complementary purposes in structuring code.\\nClasses: a concrete implementation\\nA class is a template or blueprint for creating objects. It defines their state (through fields or properties) and behaviour (through methods). A class can provide concrete implementations of methods, and it can also inherit from another class, taking over and extending its functionality.\\n\\nProtocols: a contract on behaviour\\nA protocol (interface) is an abstract type that defines only method signatures (their names, parameters and return types) and contains no implementations. A class that conforms to a protocol, or implements an interface, is obliged to provide an implementation for every method declared in it. That lets different classes, unconnected by a common ancestor, have the same behaviour.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- Implementation: A class can contain implementations of methods and hold state. A protocol contains only declarations.\\n- Inheritance: A class can inherit from only one parent class (in most languages). A class can implement many protocols.\\n- Purpose: Classes are used to create hierarchies of objects and to reuse code through inheritance. Protocols define roles or capabilities, providing polymorphism and loose coupling.\\nProtocols are widely used to create flexible architectures, such as dependency injection, design patterns (for example, Strategy, Observer) and when working with frameworks, where it matters to define a contract between the components.\\nConclusion: Use classes to describe concrete entities and their hierarchies. Use protocols (interfaces) to define contracts of behaviour that can be implemented by different, possibly unrelated classes, which makes the code more flexible and testable.","// An example class in Python\\nclass Animal:\\n    def __init__(self, name):\\n        self.name = name\\n\\n    def make_sound(self):\\n        print(\\"Some generic sound\\")\\n\\nclass Dog(Animal):  # Inheritance\\n    def make_sound(self):  # Overriding the method\\n        print(\\"Woof!\\")\\n\\n// An example protocol (interface) in TypeScript\\ninterface Drawable {\\n    draw(): void;\\n}\\n\\nclass Circle implements Drawable {\\n    draw() {\\n        console.log(\\"Drawing a circle\\");\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass Square implements Drawable {\\n    draw() {\\n        console.log(\\"Drawing a square\\");\\n    }\\n}\\n// The function can work with any object that implements Drawable\\nfunction renderShape(shape: Drawable) {\\n    shape.draw();\\n}"],[1703,"Alamofire uses URLSession\'s standard caching system, which is based on the response\'s HTTP headers. The cache is managed by a URLCache object, which stores responses in memory and on disk. Repeated requests for the same resource may return data from the cache if it is still valid, which saves traffic and speeds things up. The developer can configure the caching policy through URLSessionConfiguration.","Alamofire, the popular networking library for Swift, does not implement its own caching mechanism but relies on the built-in capabilities of the Foundation framework, namely URLSession and URLCache. That means request caching in Alamofire works in accordance with the HTTP standards (RFC 7234) and the session\'s settings.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nWhen a request is performed through Alamofire, a DataRequest instance is created that uses a URLSessionTask. URLSession checks whether the request can be served from the cache before sending it over the network. The decision is based on:\\n\\n- The response\'s HTTP headers (Cache-Control, Expires, ETag, Last-Modified).\\n\\n- The caching policy (URLRequest.CachePolicy) set in URLSessionConfiguration.\\n\\n- The settings of the shared URLCache.shared.\\n\\nIf the response is cached and considered fresh (for example, the max-age lifetime has not expired), URLSession returns the data from the cache and no network request is performed.\\n\\nConfiguring caching\\n\\nYou can configure the behaviour by creating your own session configuration. Here is an example:\\n\\nThe available caching policies (URLRequest.CachePolicy) include .useProtocolCachePolicy (the default, which follows the HTTP headers), .reloadIgnoringLocalCacheData (ignore the cache) and .returnCacheDataDontLoad (cache only, no network).\\n\\nPractical use and limitations\\n\\nCaching is especially useful for static resources (images, configuration files, rarely changing API data). For dynamic data (for example, a news feed), however, finer control is often required: you can use the Cache-Control: no-cache header or invalidate the cache forcibly by adding unique parameters to the URL (for example, a timestamp). Alamofire also makes it easy to add response validation, which can interact with the cache.\\n\\nConclusion: Use Alamofire\'s built-in caching to reduce network traffic and speed up loading repeated, rarely changing data. For full control, configure URLSessionConfiguration and set the HTTP headers correctly on the server side.","import Alamofire\\n\\n// Creating a configuration with a particular cache policy\\nlet configuration = URLSessionConfiguration.default\\nconfiguration.requestCachePolicy = .returnCacheDataElseLoad\\n// Setting the cache size (in bytes)\\nconfiguration.urlCache = URLCache(\\n    memoryCapacity: 20 * 1024 * 1024, // 20 MB in memory\\n    diskCapacity: 100 * 1024 * 1024   // 100 MB on disk\\n)\\n\\nlet session = Session(configuration: configuration)\\n\\n// Using the custom session for a request\\nsession.request(\\"https://api.example.com/data\\").response { response in\\n    // Handling the response, which may come from the cache\\n    debugPrint(response)\\n}"],[1704,"The main principles of OOP are encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction. Encapsulation hides an object\'s internal state and provides a public interface. Inheritance lets you create new classes from existing ones, reusing their functionality. Polymorphism makes it possible for objects of different classes to be handled through a common interface. Abstraction simplifies complex systems by picking out only the essential characteristics. Together these principles make code more modular, reusable, flexible and easy to understand.","Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a paradigm that organises code around objects combining data and behaviour. Its main principles are the foundation for creating scalable, maintainable applications.\\n\\nEncapsulation\\n\\nEncapsulation is the mechanism of hiding an object\'s internal implementation details and providing controlled access through public methods (getters/setters). That protects the data from unauthorised modification and reduces the coupling between the system\'s components.\\n\\nInheritance\\n\\nInheritance lets you create a new class (a child) from an existing one (the parent), taking over its properties and methods. That promotes code reuse and the creation of hierarchies.\\n\\nPolymorphism\\n\\nPolymorphism means \\"many forms\\" and lets objects of different classes be handled through a common interface. It is achieved by overriding methods or by using interfaces/abstract classes.\\n\\nAbstraction\\n\\nAbstraction is the process of picking out an object\'s essential characteristics while ignoring irrelevant details. It lets you work with high-level concepts, hiding the complexity.\\n\\nConclusion: The principles of OOP should be applied when designing complex systems, where modularity, code reuse and ease of maintenance matter. They are especially useful in long-term projects with a large team of developers.","class BankAccount {\\n    private balance: number;\\n\\n    constructor(initialBalance: number) {\\n        this.balance = initialBalance;\\n    }\\n\\n    public deposit(amount: number): void {\\n        if (amount > 0) {\\n            this.balance += amount;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    public getBalance(): number {\\n        return this.balance;\\n    }\\n}\\n// A user cannot change balance directly, only through the methods.\\n\\nclass Vehicle {\\n    protected speed: number;\\n    move() { console.log(\'Moving...\'); }\\n}\\n\\nclass Car extends Vehicle {\\n    private wheels = 4;\\n    honk() { console.log(\'Beep beep!\'); }\\n}\\n// Car automatically receives the speed field and the move() method.\\n\\nclass Shape {\\n    draw() {}\\n}\\nclass Circle extends Shape {\\n    draw() { console.log(\'Drawing a circle\'); }\\n}\\nclass Square extends Shape {\\n    draw() { console.log(\'Drawing a square\'); }\\n}\\n\\nconst shapes: Shape[] = [new Circle(), new Square()];\\nshapes.forEach(shape => shape.draw()); // Each type\'s own implementation is called.\\n\\n// An abstract class sets the common contract.\\nabstract class DatabaseConnector {\\n    abstract connect(): void;\\n    abstract query(sql: string): any[];\\n}\\n// The concrete implementation hides the details of connecting to PostgreSQL.\\nclass PostgresConnector extends DatabaseConnector {\\n    connect() { /* ...the connection logic... */ }\\n    query(sql: string) { /* ...running the query... */ return []; }\\n}\\n// The programmer uses only the connect and query methods, without going into the driver\'s details."],[1705,"To handle JSON with non-standard field names, the mapping mechanisms provided by serialisation libraries are used. For example, in Python with the `json` module you can pass the `object_hook` parameter, while in libraries such as Pydantic or dataclasses_json you use attributes like `alias` or `field`. In JavaScript, when deserialising with `JSON.parse()`, you can rename the keys manually in a loop or use libraries such as class-transformer for TypeScript. The key idea is to separate the data\'s internal representation in the code from the external JSON format.","When working with external APIs or legacy systems you often have to deal with JSON whose keys contain spaces, hyphens or dots, or begin with a digit — such names cannot be used directly as variable identifiers in most programming languages. Handling such data requires mapping between the keys in the JSON and the fields in your code\'s objects.\\n\\nApproaches to the solution\\n\\nThe main strategies include:\\n\\n- Manual mapping after parsing: Parse the JSON into a dictionary/object and then copy the values manually into the appropriate fields with the correct names.\\n\\n- Using annotations in serialisation libraries: Many modern libraries let you state how a JSON key corresponds to a class field.\\n\\n- Writing custom parsers/serialisers: For complex cases you can create your own transformation logic.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nHere is an example in Python using the Pydantic library, which handles such tasks excellently:\\n\\nIn JavaScript/TypeScript you can use the class-transformer library\'s decorators:\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nSuch handling is critically important when integrating with external services (payment systems, social networks, government APIs), where the data format is controlled by a third party. It is also useful when migrating legacy systems, where the naming conventions may have been different.\\n\\nConclusion: Using field-mapping mechanisms is essential for a clean separation between the external data format and the application\'s internal domain model, which improves the code\'s maintainability and reduces coupling with external APIs.","from pydantic import BaseModel, Field\\nfrom typing import Optional\\n\\n# The JSON may have keys like \\"user-name\\" or \\"2FA_enabled\\"\\njson_data = \'\'\'\\n{\\n    \\"user-name\\": \\"john_doe\\",\\n    \\"2FA_enabled\\": true,\\n    \\"registration date\\": \\"2023-01-15\\"\\n}\\n\'\'\'\\n\\nclass User(BaseModel):\\n    # We use Field with an alias for the mapping\\n    username: str = Field(alias=\\"user-name\\")\\n    two_fa_enabled: bool = Field(alias=\\"2FA_enabled\\")\\n    registration_date: Optional[str] = Field(alias=\\"registration date\\")\\n\\n# Deserialising, stating that the aliases must be taken into account\\nuser = User.parse_raw(json_data)\\nprint(user.username)  # john_doe\\nprint(user.two_fa_enabled)  # True\\n# In the code we now use normal field names.\\n\\nimport { plainToClass, Type, Expose } from \\"class-transformer\\";\\n\\nclass User {\\n    @Expose({ name: \\"user-name\\" })\\n    username: string;\\n\\n    @Expose({ name: \\"2FA_enabled\\" })\\n    twoFaEnabled: boolean;\\n}\\n\\nconst jsonData = `{\\n    \\"user-name\\": \\"john_doe\\",\\n    \\"2FA_enabled\\": true\\n}`;\\n\\nconst parsedJson = JSON.parse(jsonData);\\nconst user = plainToClass(User, parsedJson);\\nconsole.log(user.username); // john_doe"],[1706,"If the key for an array is missing from the JSON, it is neither nil nor an empty array — the key simply is not there. When parsing, in most languages an attempt to get the value for a missing key returns null/nil/undefined, not an empty array. To guarantee an array you have to check explicitly: if the value is null or absent, use an empty array as the default. That prevents errors like \\"Cannot read property \'length\' of null\\".","When working with JSON obtained from external sources (APIs, files), uncertainty often arises: what do you do if the expected key with an array is missing from the data? It is important to understand the difference between three states: the key is absent, the key is present with the value null, the key is present with an empty array `[]`. These states are handled differently when parsing.\\n\\nThe differences between the states\\n\\n- The key is absent: There is no such field in the JSON object. Accessing it via `obj.key` in JavaScript returns `undefined`; in Python you get a `KeyError`, or `None` depending on the method (`.get()`); in Java, `null`.\\n\\n- The key with the value null: The field is there, but its value is explicitly `null`. Accessing it returns null/nil/None.\\n\\n- An empty array: The field is there and its value is `[]`. That is a valid array of zero length.\\n\\nHandling it in practice\\n\\nTo avoid errors you need to set a default value explicitly. An example in JavaScript:\\n\\nAn example in Python using the `.get()` method:\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nSuch a check is critical when developing API clients, processing configuration files or reading data from databases. It provides resilience to changes in the data schema and prevents failures in processing chains (for example, when calling `.map()` or `.filter()`).\\n\\nConclusion: Always handle missing keys or null values explicitly, setting an empty array as the default, so that the code is predictable and resilient to incomplete data.","const jsonString = \'{\\"name\\": \\"test\\"}\'; // there is no \\"items\\" key\\nconst data = JSON.parse(jsonString);\\n\\n// Unsafe: may be undefined or null\\nconst unsafeItems = data.items; // undefined\\n// unsafeItems.length // TypeError\\n\\n// The safe approach with logical OR (||) or the nullish coalescing operator (??)\\nconst items1 = data.items || []; // if data.items is falsy (undefined, null, []), then []\\nconst items2 = data.items ?? []; // if data.items is null/undefined, then [] (but an empty array is kept)\\n\\n// An explicit check\\nlet items3;\\nif (Array.isArray(data.items)) {\\n  items3 = data.items;\\n} else {\\n  items3 = [];\\n}\\nconsole.log(items3); // []\\n\\nimport json\\n\\njson_str = \'{\\"name\\": \\"test\\"}\'\\ndata = json.loads(json_str)\\n\\n# .get() returns None if the key is absent, but you can supply a default\\nitems = data.get(\'items\', [])  # if the key is absent, it returns []\\n# If the value may be null, however, an additional check is needed\\nif items is None:\\n    items = []\\n# Or we use a ternary operator\\nitems = data[\'items\'] if \'items\' in data and data[\'items\'] is not None else []\\nprint(items)  # []"],[1707,"Yes, you can call JavaScript from Swift, and it is often needed in hybrid applications. The main way is to use the WKWebView component, which lets you load web content and run JavaScript code in it. You can also use the JavaScriptCore framework to execute JS code directly without a web view. That lets native applications interact with web components, for example to update the interface or process data.","Interaction between Swift and JavaScript is a key capability for building hybrid applications, where part of the logic or the interface is implemented with web technologies. It lets you reuse code and speed up development.\\nThe main approaches\\nThere are two main ways of calling JavaScript from Swift:\\n\\n- WKWebView: A component from the WebKit framework that displays web content. Through it you can run JS code in the context of the loaded page.\\n- JavaScriptCore: A framework providing a direct JavaScript execution environment. It lets you evaluate JS code, create objects and call functions without needing a web view.\\nAn example with WKWebView\\nWKWebView is usually used when you have an embedded web page or are loading remote content. Here is how you can run a simple script:\\n\\nThe `evaluateJavaScript` method runs a string of JS and returns the result to Swift through a closure. That is useful for getting data from a web interface or calling functions defined in the loaded script.\\nAn example with JavaScriptCore\\nJavaScriptCore suits cases where you need to run pure JavaScript logic without displaying a web page. For example, for computations or data processing:\\n\\nThis approach gives full control over the JS execution environment but is not tied to the UI.\\nWhere this is used\\nSuch interaction is often used in:\\n\\n- Hybrid applications (for example, using Cordova or Capacitor).\\n- Embedding web widgets or advertising banners.\\n- Running dynamic scripts configured on the server side.\\n- Testing or prototyping, when the logic can be changed quickly in JS.\\nConclusion: Calling JavaScript from Swift makes sense when you need to integrate web technologies into a native application for flexibility, code reuse or cross-platform support. Use WKWebView to interact with a web interface, and JavaScriptCore for isolated computations.","import WebKit\\n\\nclass ViewController: UIViewController {\\n    let webView = WKWebView()\\n    \\n    override func viewDidLoad() {\\n        super.viewDidLoad()\\n        view.addSubview(webView)\\n        // Load HTML or a URL\\n        let html = \\"<html><head><title>Hello</title></head><body><h1>Hello</h1></body></html>\\"\\n        webView.loadHTMLString(html, baseURL: nil)\\n        \\n        // Call JavaScript once it has loaded\\n        webView.evaluateJavaScript(\\"document.title\\") { result, error in\\n            if let title = result as? String {\\n                print(\\"Title from JS:\\", title)\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimport JavaScriptCore\\n\\nlet context = JSContext()\\n// Run the JS code\\ncontext?.evaluateScript(\\"\\"\\"\\n    function add(a, b) {\\n        return a + b;\\n    }\\n\\"\\"\\")\\n// Call the function from Swift\\nlet addFunction = context?.objectForKeyedSubscript(\\"add\\")\\nlet result = addFunction?.call(withArguments: [5, 3])\\nprint(\\"Result:\\", result?.toString() ?? \\"none\\") // Output: 8"],[1708,"JSON has no dedicated type for dates, so dates are usually transmitted as strings. To convert a string into a Date object in JavaScript the `new Date()` constructor is used. The most reliable format is a string in the ISO 8601 standard (for example, \\"2024-12-31T23:59:59.999Z\\"), which the Date constructor understands directly. If the date arrives in another format, manual parsing of the string or libraries such as date-fns or moment.js may be needed.","When working with JSON in JavaScript it is important to understand that the JSON format supports a limited set of data types: strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays and objects. There is no dedicated type for dates. So when a server sends a date, it is serialised into a string (or, more rarely, into a number representing a timestamp). The client\'s task is to convert that string correctly back into a Date object, which is convenient to work with.\\n\\nThe standard approach: the Date constructor\\n\\nThe simplest way is to pass the string directly into the `new Date()` constructor. JavaScript can parse strings in the ISO 8601 format, which is the international standard for representing dates and times. That format is often the default in many APIs and databases.\\n\\nWorking with a numeric timestamp\\n\\nSometimes a date is transmitted as a number — the count of milliseconds elapsed since 1 January 1970 (a Unix timestamp in milliseconds). In that case the Date constructor also accepts a number.\\n\\nPotential problems and their solution\\n\\nNot every string date format is parsed equally well in different browsers. Formats like \\"15/05/2024\\" may be interpreted incorrectly. It is recommended to:\\n\\n- Agree with the back end on using ISO 8601.\\n\\n- Use libraries for complex formats (for example, date-fns, moment.js).\\n\\n- Parse the string manually if the format is known and fixed.\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\nConverting a date from JSON is needed in practically any web application that receives data from a server: displaying when a post was published, filtering events in a calendar, building charts with time series. Handling it correctly guarantees that dates are displayed properly in the user\'s local time zone.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `new Date()` for strings in ISO 8601 format or for numeric timestamps. For non-standard formats it is preferable to use proven libraries or explicit parsing, to avoid problems with cross-browser behaviour and time zones.","// Example: a date from JSON as an ISO string\\nconst jsonString = \'{\\"eventDate\\":\\"2024-05-15T14:30:00.000Z\\"}\';\\nconst data = JSON.parse(jsonString);\\n\\n// Converting the string into a Date object\\nconst dateObj = new Date(data.eventDate);\\nconsole.log(dateObj); // Output: Wed May 15 2024 17:30:00 GMT+0300 (Moscow Standard Time)\\nconsole.log(dateObj.getFullYear()); // 2024\\n\\n// Example: a timestamp from JSON\\nconst jsonWithTimestamp = \'{\\"createdAt\\": 1715779800000}\';\\nconst data2 = JSON.parse(jsonWithTimestamp);\\nconst dateFromTimestamp = new Date(data2.createdAt);\\nconsole.log(dateFromTimestamp.toISOString()); // \\"2024-05-15T14:30:00.000Z\\"\\n\\n// Manual parsing for the \\"DD/MM/YYYY\\" format\\nfunction parseCustomDate(dateStr) {\\n  const [day, month, year] = dateStr.split(\'/\').map(Number);\\n  // Months in Date start from 0\\n  return new Date(year, month - 1, day);\\n}\\nconst customDate = parseCustomDate(\\"15/05/2024\\");\\nconsole.log(customDate.toDateString()); // Wed May 15 2024"],[1709,"A memory leak is a situation where a program does not release memory it no longer needs. That leads to a gradual growth in RAM consumption, which can slow the application down or even make it crash. In languages with automatic memory management, such as JavaScript, leaks often arise from keeping unnecessary references to objects. Understanding and preventing leaks is critically important for building responsive, reliable applications.","A memory leak is a widespread problem in software development where an application keeps holding memory allocated for objects that are no longer used in the program. Since that memory is not released, the application\'s overall RAM consumption grows over time, which can lead to reduced performance, stuttering and ultimately to the program crashing because the available memory is exhausted.\\n\\nHow do memory leaks arise?\\n\\nIn languages with manual memory management (C, C++) a leak happens when the developer allocates memory (for example, with `malloc` or `new`) but forgets to free it (with `free` or `delete`). In languages with automatic garbage collection (JavaScript, Java, C#, Python) memory is freed automatically once there are no more references to an object. Leaks are still possible, however, if references to unneeded objects are inadvertently retained in the code.\\n\\nTypical causes in JavaScript\\n\\n- Event handlers that are not removed: Adding listeners to DOM elements that are not removed when the element is destroyed.\\n\\n- Closures: Keeping references to large objects or DOM elements inside closures that go on existing.\\n\\n- Global variables: Unintentionally creating global variables that are never cleared.\\n\\n- Caches and collections: Storing objects in arrays or Maps with no mechanism for clearing them.\\n\\n- Detached DOM elements: References to DOM nodes removed from the page but still held in JavaScript\'s memory.\\n\\nAn example of a leak in JavaScript\\n\\nHow do you detect and prevent leaks?\\n\\nTo detect leaks, the browsers\' developer tools are used (for example, the Memory Profiler in Chrome DevTools) or memory profilers for server applications. Profiling lets you take heap snapshots and compare them to find objects that are not being freed. To prevent leaks it is important to:\\n\\n- Remove event handlers when they are no longer needed.\\n\\n- Avoid holding large data in the global scope.\\n\\n- Clear references to objects in caches and collections (for example, use WeakMap or WeakSet).\\n\\n- Work carefully with closures and timers.\\n\\nConclusion: Understanding memory leaks is essential for building stable, performant applications, especially long-lived ones such as SPAs (single-page applications) or server processes. Auditing memory regularly and using the right coding patterns help you avoid accumulating garbage and keep a program running smoothly.","// Example: a leak caused by keeping a reference in a closure\\nfunction createHeavyObject() {\\n  const largeArray = new Array(1000000).fill(\'data\'); // A large object\\n  return function() {\\n    // The inner function keeps a reference to largeArray,\\n    // even though the outer function has already finished.\\n    console.log(\'Closure references array of size:\', largeArray.length);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nlet leakyFunction = createHeavyObject();\\n// largeArray can now not be removed by the garbage collector\\n// as long as leakyFunction exists.\\n\\n// One more example: a global variable\\nwindow.cache = {};\\nfunction processData(data) {\\n  // The data accumulates in the global object and is never cleared\\n  window.cache[Date.now()] = data;\\n}"],[1710,"To preserve the order of asynchronous tasks\' results you need to use Promise.all with an array of promises. The promises start simultaneously, but Promise.all returns an array of results in the same order the promises were passed in. That works because Promise.all does not change the order of the resolved values. If the order is critical, do not use methods that return results as they become ready, for example Promise.race or loops with await inside.","When working with several asynchronous tasks (for example, API requests or reading files) you often need to start them in parallel for performance but receive the results in the same order the tasks were initiated. That matters when the order of the data is meaningful, for example when processing a list of items where each item corresponds to a particular position.\\n\\nThe solution with Promise.all\\n\\nThe standard solution is the `Promise.all` method. It takes an iterable collection of promises (for example, an array) and returns a new promise that resolves when all the promises passed in have completed. The key property: the array of results preserves the order of the original promises, even if some of them finish later than others.\\n\\nAlternative approaches\\n\\nIf you need to limit the number of tasks running at once (for example, so as not to overload the server), you can use a pool pattern that preserves the order. One way is to process the tasks in parallel in groups and then collect the results.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\n- Loading data for a list of items while preserving the display order.\\n\\n- Processing files in parallel, when the file names have to correspond to the results.\\n\\n- Running a chain of asynchronous operations where each operation depends on the previous one but some steps can be parallelised.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `Promise.all` to run asynchronous tasks in parallel while preserving the order of the results. If you need control over the number of simultaneous operations, implement a task pool that processes groups with `Promise.all` and combines the results in the right sequence.","async function fetchData(url) {\\n  const response = await fetch(url);\\n  return response.json();\\n}\\n\\nconst urls = [\'/api/user/1\', \'/api/user/2\', \'/api/user/3\'];\\n// Create an array of promises\\nconst promises = urls.map(url => fetchData(url));\\n\\n// Start them all and wait for the results\\nconst results = await Promise.all(promises);\\n// results[0] corresponds to urls[0], results[1] to urls[1] and so on\\nconsole.log(results);\\n\\nasync function runInOrder(tasks, concurrency) {\\n  const results = [];\\n  for (let i = 0; i < tasks.length; i += concurrency) {\\n    const chunk = tasks.slice(i, i + concurrency);\\n    const chunkResults = await Promise.all(chunk.map(task => task()));\\n    results.push(...chunkResults);\\n  }\\n  return results;\\n}\\n\\n// tasks is an array of functions returning promises\\nconst tasks = [\\n  () => fetch(\'/api/1\'),\\n  () => fetch(\'/api/2\'),\\n  // ...\\n];\\nconst orderedResults = await runInOrder(tasks, 2);"],[1711,"Designing an API starts with identifying the resources that represent your application\'s entities, for example users or orders. For each resource you create endpoints that use the standard HTTP methods: GET to retrieve data, POST to create, PUT/PATCH to update and DELETE to remove. The data is usually transferred in JSON format, and the API should return comprehensible HTTP statuses, such as 200 for success, 404 if the resource is not found, or 400 for an error in the request. It is important to document the API so that front-end developers understand how to work with it.","Designing an API means creating a contract between the front end and the back end that defines how they will exchange data. A well-designed API makes integration predictable, scalable and easy to maintain.\\nThe key principles of REST\\nFollow the REST architectural style. Resources (for example, `/users`, `/orders`) are the key abstraction. Every resource is identified by a unique URL (an endpoint).\\n\\n- Use nouns, not verbs, in the paths: Instead of `/getUser` use `GET /users/{id}`.\\n- Use the HTTP methods for their intended purpose: GET (read), POST (create), PUT (full update), PATCH (partial update), DELETE (remove).\\n- Use the correct HTTP status codes: 200 (OK), 201 (Created), 400 (Bad Request), 401 (Unauthorized), 404 (Not Found), 500 (Internal Server Error).\\nThe structure of requests and responses\\nData is usually transferred in JSON format. The responses should be consistent. For example, wrap a list of resources in an object to allow for pagination.\\n\\nVersioning and documentation\\nInclude the API\'s version in the path (for example, `/api/v1/`) or in the headers. That lets you make breaking changes without breaking old clients. Document the API with tools such as Swagger (OpenAPI), which generate interactive documentation from the code automatically.\\nSecurity and performance\\nUse HTTPS. Authenticate requests, often with tokens (JWT). Implement rate limiting. For complex queries use filtering, sorting and pagination parameters right in the endpoints: `GET /api/v1/products?category=books&sort=-price&page=2&limit=10`.\\nConclusion: A well-designed RESTful API that follows the conventions, uses the correct HTTP methods and statuses and has clear documentation significantly speeds up front-end development, reduces the number of errors and makes the whole application easier to maintain in the long term.","// An example response for GET /api/v1/users\\n{\\n  \\"data\\": [\\n    { \\"id\\": 1, \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\" },\\n    { \\"id\\": 2, \\"name\\": \\"Bob\\" }\\n  ],\\n  \\"meta\\": {\\n    \\"total\\": 2,\\n    \\"page\\": 1,\\n    \\"limit\\": 20\\n  }\\n}"],[1712,"A synchronous request blocks the execution of the code until a response is received from the server, freezing the user interface. An asynchronous request is sent and runs in the background, letting the main thread of code carry on working and handle other events. Once a response arrives from the server, a special handler function (a callback) is invoked, which keeps the application responsive. Synchronous requests are considered obsolete and are almost never used in modern front ends.","HTTP requests are the foundation of a web application\'s interaction with a server. By the way they are performed they divide into synchronous and asynchronous, and this difference directly affects performance and the user experience.\\n\\nSynchronous requests\\n\\nA synchronous (blocking) request halts the execution of the script at the place it was called. The browser \\"freezes\\" — the user cannot scroll the page, press buttons or interact with the interface until the server sends a response. This approach was widespread in the early days of the web, but is now considered an anti-pattern because of its catastrophic effect on UX.\\n\\nAsynchronous requests\\n\\nAn asynchronous (non-blocking) request is sent, and control returns to the main thread of execution immediately. The browser carries on handling user events, animations and other code. When the server responds, a previously specified handler function (a callback) fires, a Promise resolves, or the code after the `await` keyword runs. This is the foundation of AJAX and of modern SPA frameworks.\\n\\nWhere they are used\\n\\n- Asynchronous requests are used everywhere: loading data for a news feed, submitting forms without reloading the page, search autocomplete, infinite scroll.\\n\\n- Synchronous requests have been all but banished from front-end development. In rare cases you may encounter them in utility scripts or in specific server environments where blocking is acceptable.\\n\\nSummary: Always use asynchronous HTTP requests in client-side JavaScript. It is a mandatory condition for building smooth, responsive web applications that do not make the user wait. The synchronous approach is an archaic method that leads to the interface hanging.","// An example of a synchronous request (DO NOT USE IN PRACTICE)\\nconst request = new XMLHttpRequest();\\nrequest.open(\'GET\', \'/api/data\', false); // The third parameter \'false\' makes the request synchronous\\nrequest.send();\\n// The code here will not run until the response arrives\\nconsole.log(request.responseText);\\n\\n// An example of an asynchronous request using the Fetch API and async/await\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  try {\\n    // The code is not blocked while the request is in flight\\n    const response = await fetch(\'/api/data\');\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    console.log(\'The data has arrived:\', data);\\n    // Update the interface with the new data\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(\'Request error:\', error);\\n  }\\n}\\n// Call the function. The interface stays responsive.\\nfetchData();\\nconsole.log(\\"This line runs immediately, without waiting for the response from the server.\\");"],[1713,"Synchronous operations inside an asynchronous application block the Event Loop, which halts the handling of all other requests and tasks. That leads to a sharp drop in performance, increased response times, and can cause a denial of service under heavy load. For example, reading a large file synchronously in a web server freezes the server for the duration of the operation. It is therefore critically important to avoid blocking code in asynchronous handlers.","Asynchronous applications, such as Node.js servers, are built on a single-threaded Event Loop model. That means one thread handles all the incoming requests, switching between tasks while they wait for I/O operations to complete (for example, reading a file, a network request). Synchronous (blocking) operations break that principle.\\n\\nThe problems that arise from using synchronous operations\\n\\n- Blocking the Event Loop: While a synchronous task runs (for example, a complex computation or synchronous file access), the event loop cannot handle other requests, timers or callbacks. The whole server \\"freezes\\".\\n\\n- A drop in performance and scalability: The application\'s throughput falls sharply. Instead of handling thousands of simultaneous connections, the server will process requests one after another.\\n\\n- Increased latency: Even simple requests will wait in the queue until the current blocking operation finishes.\\n\\n- The risk of denial of service (DoS): A few heavy synchronous requests can exhaust the Event Loop\'s resources completely, making the server unavailable to the other users.\\n\\nA code example demonstrating the problem\\n\\nConsider a simple HTTP server in Node.js. The route handler uses a synchronous function to read a large file.\\n\\nWhile `readFileSync` runs, the server cannot answer other requests. If the file is very large, that can take seconds.\\n\\nWhere this applies and how to avoid the problems\\n\\nThe problem is relevant to every environment based on an Event Loop (Node.js, Python asyncio). The solutions:\\n\\n- Always use the asynchronous versions of the APIs (with callbacks, Promises or async/await).\\n\\n- Move CPU-intensive tasks (sorting, cryptography) into separate threads (Worker Threads in Node.js) or processes.\\n\\n- Use streaming data processing (Streams) to work with large files or network data, so as not to load everything into memory at once.\\n\\nConclusion: Synchronous operations destroy the main advantage of asynchronous applications — high concurrency with low resource consumption. They should be avoided in the Event Loop\'s main thread, leaving it free to dispatch events quickly. Use asynchronous patterns for I/O operations and move heavy computation into background processes.","const http = require(\'http\');\\nconst fs = require(\'fs\');\\n\\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\\n    // BAD: Reading the file synchronously blocks the Event Loop\\n    const data = fs.readFileSync(\'./veryLargeFile.json\'); // Blocking!\\n    res.end(data);\\n\\n    // GOOD: The asynchronous, non-blocking version\\n    // fs.readFile(\'./veryLargeFile.json\', (err, data) => {\\n    //     res.end(data);\\n    // });\\n});\\n\\nserver.listen(3000);"],[1714,"The event loop is blocked when a synchronous operation takes too long, leaving the loop unable to process other tasks. To avoid that, use the asynchronous versions of functions (for example, fs.readFile instead of fs.readFileSync) for I/O operations. For heavy computations, move them into separate threads with Worker Threads or break them into pieces using setImmediate/setTimeout, to let the loop \\"catch its breath\\". Also watch the complexity of synchronous code in request handlers.","The Event Loop in Node.js is the mechanism that handles asynchronous operations, letting an application stay responsive. It is blocked when a long synchronous task runs on the main thread (for example, complex computations, synchronous file I/O or an infinite loop), because the event loop cannot move to the next phase until the current synchronous code finishes.\\n\\nThe main causes of blocking and their solutions\\n\\n- Synchronous I/O: Use the asynchronous APIs (callback-based, Promises, async/await). For example, `fs.readFile` instead of `fs.readFileSync`.\\n\\n- Heavy computation (CPU-bound tasks): Move them into separate threads with the `worker_threads` module, or break them into pieces using `setImmediate()` or `process.nextTick()` to free the event loop between iterations.\\n\\n- Bad regular expressions: Avoid catastrophic backtracking in complex regexes.\\n\\n- Large JSON operations: Parsing or serialising large objects can block the thread; use streaming parsers or move the work into workers.\\n\\nExample: splitting a CPU task\\n\\nInstead of running a long loop all at once, you can break it into pieces:\\n\\nWhen to use Worker Threads\\n\\nFor genuinely heavy computations that cannot be split up effectively, create separate threads:\\n\\nConclusion: Avoid blocking the event loop by using asynchronous operations for I/O and by moving CPU tasks into separate threads or breaking them into pieces. This is critically important for maintaining high throughput and responsiveness in Node.js applications, especially servers handling many simultaneous connections.","function processChunk(data, start, end, callback) {\\n  // Heavy computation over part of the data\\n  let result = 0;\\n  for (let i = start; i < end; i++) {\\n    result += data[i] * Math.sqrt(i);\\n  }\\n  callback(result);\\n}\\n\\nfunction processHeavyTask(data, chunkSize) {\\n  let index = 0;\\n  let finalResult = 0;\\n  function nextChunk() {\\n    if (index >= data.length) {\\n      console.log(\'Total:\', finalResult);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const end = Math.min(index + chunkSize, data.length);\\n    processChunk(data, index, end, (chunkResult) => {\\n      finalResult += chunkResult;\\n      index = end;\\n      // Free the event loop before the next portion\\n      setImmediate(nextChunk);\\n    });\\n  }\\n  nextChunk();\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst bigArray = new Array(1000000).fill(1);\\nprocessHeavyTask(bigArray, 10000);\\n\\nconst { Worker } = require(\'worker_threads\');\\nfunction runInWorker(scriptPath, data) {\\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    const worker = new Worker(scriptPath, { workerData: data });\\n    worker.on(\'message\', resolve);\\n    worker.on(\'error\', reject);\\n    worker.on(\'exit\', (code) => {\\n      if (code !== 0) reject(new Error(`Worker stopped with exit code ${code}`));\\n    });\\n  });\\n}\\n// worker.js will perform the computation in the background"],[1715,"When you access an attribute the object itself does not have, JavaScript automatically looks for it along the prototype chain, starting from the `__proto__` (or `[[Prototype]]`) object. If the property is found on the parent object (the prototype), it is used. If the property is not found on any prototype in the chain, `undefined` is returned. This is the basis of inheritance in JavaScript.","In JavaScript objects can inherit properties and methods from other objects through the prototype mechanism. Every object has an internal reference to another object, called its prototype. When you try to access an object\'s property, the engine first looks for that property on the object itself. If the property is not found, the search continues on the object\'s prototype, then on the prototype\'s prototype and so on, until the end of the chain is reached (usually `null`).\\n\\nHow the lookup works\\n\\nConsider an example with a constructor and a prototype:\\n\\nPractical application\\n\\nThe prototype chain is used to implement inheritance in JavaScript, allowing code to be reused and hierarchies of objects to be built. For example, the built-in objects such as arrays and strings have methods (for example, `Array.prototype.map`) available through their prototypes. It is also the foundation for ES6 classes, which are syntactic sugar over prototypal inheritance.\\n\\nIt is important to understand that if a property is found in the chain, it is used as though it belonged to the object itself, with `this` set to the original object. If the property is only read, however, that is transparent to the code. When a property is written (for example, `dog.speak = \'bark\'`) it is created on the object itself without touching the prototype — this is called shadowing.\\n\\nConclusion: The prototype chain is a powerful JavaScript mechanism for inheritance and code reuse; it automatically lets objects access the properties of parent objects, which simplifies building complex hierarchies and saves memory.","function Animal(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n}\\n\\nAnimal.prototype.speak = function() {\\n  console.log(this.name + \' makes a sound.\');\\n};\\n\\nconst dog = new Animal(\'Rex\');\\n\\n// Accessing a property dog does not have but the prototype does\\nconsole.log(dog.speak); // The function will be found on Animal.prototype\\ndog.speak(); // Prints: \\"Rex makes a sound.\\"\\n\\n// Accessing a non-existent property\\nconsole.log(dog.color); // undefined, since it is neither on dog nor in the chain"],[1716,"A linear search checks the elements one after another until it finds the one it needs, which in the worst case takes O(n) time. An index lookup uses a previously built structure (for example, a hash table or a B-tree), letting you find an element in O(1) or O(log n). An index speeds up searching but requires additional memory and time to maintain the structure. The choice of method depends on how often you search versus update the data.","Searching for data is one of the fundamental operations in programming, and understanding the differences between the approaches is critical for writing efficient code. The two main methods — a linear search and an index lookup — differ radically in how they work and in their performance.\\n\\nA linear search\\n\\nThis is the simplest algorithm. It checks every element in a collection (for example, an array or a list) one after another, starting from the first, until it finds the value sought or reaches the end. Its time complexity in the worst case is O(n), where n is the number of elements. That means the search time grows linearly with the size of the data. A linear search requires no additional memory and is simple to implement, but is slow on large volumes.\\n\\nAn index lookup\\n\\nThis method uses an auxiliary data structure — an index — to speed up the search. An index, like a table of contents in a book, holds keys and references to the corresponding data. Common types of index include hash tables (a lookup in O(1) on average) and balanced trees, such as the B-tree in databases (a lookup in O(log n)). An index lookup is significantly faster than a linear search, especially on large data sets, but there is a price to pay:\\n\\n- The additional memory to hold the index.\\n\\n- The overhead of keeping the index up to date when data is inserted, updated or deleted (which can slow down modification operations).\\n\\nWhere are these methods used?\\n\\nA linear search suits small or unordered collections, where simplicity of implementation matters more than speed, or when the data is rarely searched. An index lookup is the foundation of high-performance systems: databases use indexes to speed up queries, caching systems (for example, Redis) store data by key, and applications often create indexes for frequently queried fields.\\n\\nConclusion: Use a linear search for simple tasks with a small volume of data. When working with large data sets where search speed is critical, be sure to use an index lookup, despite the additional memory and maintenance costs.","function linearSearch(arr, target) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] === target) {\\n      return i; // Return the index of the element found\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return -1; // The element was not found\\n}\\n\\n// Example usage\\nconst numbers = [5, 3, 8, 1, 9];\\nconsole.log(linearSearch(numbers, 8)); // Output: 2\\n\\n// An example using an object as a simple hash table (an index) in JavaScript\\nconst data = [\\n  { id: 101, name: \'Alice\' },\\n  { id: 102, name: \'Bob\' },\\n  { id: 103, name: \'Charlie\' }\\n];\\n\\n// Build an index on the id field\\nconst indexById = {};\\ndata.forEach((item, idx) => {\\n  indexById[item.id] = idx;\\n});\\n\\n// A fast lookup by id through the index\\nfunction searchById(id) {\\n  const position = indexById[id];\\n  if (position !== undefined) {\\n    return data[position];\\n  }\\n  return null;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(searchById(102)); // Output: { id: 102, name: \'Bob\' }"],[1717,"The event loop in Node.js processes tasks on a single thread. If tasks take a long time (CPU-intensive ones), they block the event loop. That leads to delays in handling incoming requests, timers and I/O operations. The application becomes slow and unresponsive. To solve it you need to move heavy computation into separate threads or processes.","The event loop in Node.js is the mechanism that handles asynchronous operations on a single thread. It is efficient for I/O tasks but has limits when a large number of synchronous or computationally heavy tasks have to run.\\n\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Event loop blocking: Long synchronous operations (for example, complex computations, synchronous file I/O) occupy the thread, leaving the loop unable to process other tasks.\\n\\n- Delayed event handling: While the loop is busy, new incoming HTTP requests, timers (setTimeout, setInterval) and callbacks from completed I/O operations are queued and wait.\\n\\n- A drop in performance and responsiveness: The application starts answering requests slowly and response times grow, which is especially critical for web servers and real-time applications.\\n\\n- The risk of exhausting memory: If tasks arrive faster than they are processed, the event queue can grow indefinitely, consuming ever more memory.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider a server that performs a heavy computation synchronously in a request handler:\\n\\nWhile the server handles the `/compute` request, every other request (even to `/`) will wait 3 seconds. That makes the server unfit for production.\\n\\nHow to solve these problems\\n\\n- Separating the tasks: Move CPU-intensive operations into separate threads with Worker Threads, or into separate processes (via child_process).\\n\\n- Asynchronous patterns: Always use the asynchronous versions of the APIs for I/O (fs.promises, not fs.readFileSync).\\n\\n- Splitting tasks up: Long synchronous tasks can be broken into pieces with `setImmediate()` or `process.nextTick()`, to give the event loop a chance to handle other tasks between the pieces.\\n\\n- Monitoring: Use tools such as `node --inspect` or libraries (for example, clinic.js) to identify blockages.\\n\\nConclusion: Problems with a large number of tasks in the event loop arise from its single-threaded nature. The key solution is not to block the loop with long operations, moving them outside it (into threads/processes) and following asynchronous patterns strictly. This is critically important for building responsive, scalable Node.js applications.","const http = require(\'http\');\\n\\n// A function that blocks the event loop (simulating a heavy task)\\nfunction blockingTask(durationMs) {\\n    const start = Date.now();\\n    while (Date.now() - start < durationMs) { /* busy wait */ }\\n}\\n\\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\\n    if (req.url === \'/compute\') {\\n        // This operation will block the loop for 3 seconds!\\n        blockingTask(3000);\\n        res.end(\'Computation done\');\\n    } else {\\n        res.end(\'Hello\');\\n    }\\n});\\n\\nserver.listen(3000);\\nconsole.log(\'Server running on port 3000\');"],[1718,"Automatic context switching is the process of saving the current thread\'s state and loading another\'s, performed by the operating system\'s scheduler. It can be inefficient because of the overhead of the switch itself (saving/restoring registers, updating kernel structures), which consumes processor time. Frequent switches can mean the system spends more time managing threads than doing useful work (the \\"thrashing\\" effect). The switch also flushes the processor\'s caches (for example, the TLB, the instruction cache), which reduces performance.","Automatic context switching is a fundamental mechanism of multitasking operating systems, letting several processes or threads share time on one or more processor cores. The OS scheduler periodically interrupts the current thread\'s execution, saves its state (the context — processor registers, the stack pointer, the memory state and so on) and loads the context of the next thread ready to run. Although this mechanism creates the illusion of parallel execution, it introduces unavoidable overhead.\\n\\nThe main causes of inefficiency\\n\\n- Direct overhead: The very operation of saving and restoring a context requires processor time. That includes saving dozens of registers into memory, updating the kernel\'s internal data structures (for example, process descriptors) and switching the address space (if these are different processes).\\n\\n- Loss of data locality and caches: Modern processors depend heavily on caches for speed. When a thread is pre-empted, the data it was actively using, which was held in the fast L1/L2 caches, will most likely be replaced by the new thread\'s data. When the original thread resumes, the caches will be \\"cold\\", which leads to cache misses and slower execution. The flushing of the TLB (the translation lookaside buffer) is especially painful, since it leads to additional memory accesses.\\n\\n- Switching too often (thrashing): If the system tries to be \\"fair\\" and give time slices to many active threads, or if the threads frequently block on I/O operations, switches can happen excessively often. In the extreme case most of the processor\'s time goes on the context switching itself rather than on the applications\' useful work.\\n\\n- Synchronisation problems: Frequent context switching increases contention for locks. A thread holding a lock may be pre-empted, forcing other threads to wait longer, which reduces overall throughput.\\n\\nAn example at the level of code\\n\\nConsider simplified pseudocode illustrating how two threads doing simple work can suffer from frequent switches.\\n\\nIf the scheduler\'s time slice is small and the loops run quickly, the system will constantly spend resources switching between A and B instead of letting one thread finish its work in a large chunk.\\n\\nWhere this matters and how to optimise it\\n\\nThe problem is relevant in high-load servers, real-time systems and latency-sensitive applications (for example, audio/video processing). To reduce the cost the following are used:\\n\\n- Increasing the scheduler\'s time slice (where that is acceptable).\\n\\n- Pinning threads to particular processor cores (CPU affinity), to make better use of the caches.\\n\\n- Asynchronous/non-blocking programming (for example, using an event loop), which lets many tasks be handled on one thread, minimising switches.\\n\\n- Using user-space (green) threads (coroutines, goroutines), where the switch happens in user space and is far cheaper.\\n\\nConclusion: Automatic context switching is a necessary compromise for multitasking, but it should be minimised in performance-critical sections of code. It is at its most inefficient when switches are frequent and in tasks that make intensive use of the processor\'s cache. Understanding this helps you choose the right concurrency models (multithreading, asynchrony, processes) for a particular task.","// Thread A\\nvoid workerA() {\\n    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {\\n        // A short computational task\\n        resultA += someCalculation(i);\\n        // Here the scheduler may pre-empt thread A\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Thread B\\nvoid workerB() {\\n    for (int j = 0; j < 1000000; j++) {\\n        // A similar short task\\n        resultB += anotherCalculation(j);\\n        // And here a switch back to A may happen\\n    }\\n}"],[1719,"If you do not use await when calling an asynchronous function, then instead of waiting for the operation\'s result the function returns a Promise object in the pending state. That means the code carries on executing synchronously without waiting for the asynchronous task to finish, which can lead to an unresolved value being used. For example, if the fetchData function returns a Promise, then without await the variable receives the Promise itself rather than the data inside it, which causes errors when you try to work with it as an ordinary object.","Asynchronous functions in JavaScript, declared with the `async` keyword, always return a Promise object. The `await` keyword is used inside such functions to suspend execution until the Promise is fulfilled or rejected. If you call an asynchronous function without `await`, you receive that Promise directly rather than its result.\\nWhat happens without await\\nWhen an asynchronous function is called without `await`, it starts running, but control returns to the calling code immediately. That means subsequent operations may run before the asynchronous task finishes, which leads to a race condition or to data that is not ready being used.\\nA code example\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nSometimes calling without `await` deliberately is useful, when you want to start several asynchronous operations in parallel to improve performance. For example, you can start several requests at once and then use `Promise.all` to wait for all the results.\\nConclusion\\nUse `await` to wait for an asynchronous function\'s result when the subsequent code depends on that result. If you need to run operations in parallel or manage the Promise manually, you can omit `await`, but then you have to handle the Promise explicitly with the `.then()` or `.catch()` methods.","async function fetchData() {\\n    // Simulating an asynchronous operation, for example an API request\\n    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(\'The data has arrived\'), 1000));\\n}\\n\\nasync function process() {\\n    // A call without await\\n    const dataPromise = fetchData();\\n    console.log(dataPromise); // Prints: Promise { <pending> }\\n    // Trying to use the Promise as a string raises an error\\n    // console.log(dataPromise.toUpperCase()); // Error!\\n    \\n    // The correct call, with await\\n    const data = await fetchData();\\n    console.log(data); // Prints: \'The data has arrived\' (after one second)\\n}\\n\\nprocess();"],[1720,"A delay between attempts to run a function can be added with setTimeout in callback style, or using a Promise with async/await. The simplest way is to create a `delay` function that returns a promise resolving after a given time. Then, in the loop or recursive function implementing the retries, call `await delay(ms)` before the next attempt. That avoids blocking the main thread and keeps the code readable.","Adding a delay between attempts to run a function is a common task when implementing retry logic to handle transient failures, for example in network requests or interaction with external APIs. The main goal is not to spam the server with requests and to give it time to recover.\\nThe main approaches\\nIn JavaScript, which runs on a single thread, you cannot use synchronous pauses (for example, a while loop with Date.now()), since that would block the whole interface. Asynchronous mechanisms are used instead:\\n\\n- setTimeout/callback: the classic way, but it can lead to \\"callback hell\\".\\n- Promise + async/await: the modern, preferable approach, which makes the code linear and easy to read.\\nA practical implementation\\nFirst let\'s create a helper `delay` function that returns a promise resolving after the specified number of milliseconds.\\n\\nIt can then be used inside an asynchronous function that performs the main operation with retries.\\n\\nIn this example there will be a one-second (1000 ms) pause between failed attempts. The delay can be made exponential (exponential backoff) by multiplying `delayMs` by a factor after every failure, which is best practice for distributed systems.\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is used when:\\n\\n- Working with unstable network connections.\\n- Calling external APIs that may return 429 (Too Many Requests) or 503 (Service Unavailable) errors.\\n- Performing database operations that may fail temporarily under heavy load.\\n- Any background tasks that need resilience to transient failures.\\nSummary: Use a Promise-based asynchronous delay function inside a retry loop or recursion. That keeps the code clean and non-blocking, and is the standard for handling transient failures in modern JavaScript development.","function delay(ms) {\\n  return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));\\n}\\n\\nasync function fetchWithRetry(url, maxAttempts = 3, delayMs = 1000) {\\n  for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {\\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch(url);\\n      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\\n      return await response.json();\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      console.log(`Attempt ${attempt} failed: ${error.message}`);\\n      if (attempt === maxAttempts) throw error;\\n      // Add a delay before the next attempt\\n      await delay(delayMs);\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\n// fetchWithRetry(\'https://api.example.com/data\');"],[1721,"The main approaches include using providers\' APIs directly, such as OpenAI\'s or Anthropic\'s, to send prompts and receive responses. To work with private data, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is used, which retrieves relevant information from a knowledge base before generating an answer. More complex systems are built on agents, which can plan actions and use tools (for example, searching the internet or running code). Managing prompts through templates and managing context through chains of calls (chaining) also matter.","Integrating with large language models (LLMs) has become a key task for developers who want to add \\"intelligent\\" capabilities to their applications, such as generating text, analysis or dialogue. The choice of approach depends on the data requirements, the complexity of the logic and the need to interact with external systems.\\n1. Direct API calls\\nThe simplest method is sending HTTP requests to providers\' APIs (OpenAI GPT, Claude, Gemini). You build a prompt with instructions and context, receive a response and process it in the application. This approach suits tasks that do not require access to current or private data.\\n\\n2. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\\nRAG solves the problem of the model\'s knowledge being limited by its training date. On a query, the system first looks for relevant documents in a vector database (for example, using embeddings) and then passes the fragments found into the prompt to the LLM. That makes it possible to answer questions about internal documentation, news or any other current information.\\n3. Agents and tool use\\nAn agent is a system where the LLM acts as the \\"brain\\" that can solve a task step by step. The agent is given access to tools, for example a calculator, internet search or a database. The model decides which tool to call, passes it arguments, receives the result and carries on reasoning. This is the foundation for complex autonomous systems.\\n\\n4. Managing prompts and chaining\\nFor complex workflows, prompts are broken into stages (chains). For example, the LLM first analyses the query and classifies its intent, then extracts the entities, and then generates the answer. Libraries such as LangChain or LlamaIndex provide abstractions for building such chains and for managing context and prompt templates.\\nConclusion: Direct API calls suit simple generation tasks. RAG is necessary when you need to work with current or private data. The agent approach is used to create autonomous systems capable of acting in the outside world. Managing chains helps structure complex logic for interacting with the model.","import openai\\n\\nresponse = openai.ChatCompletion.create(\\n    model=\\"gpt-4\\",\\n    messages=[{\\"role\\": \\"user\\", \\"content\\": \\"Explain quantum entanglement simply\\"}]\\n)\\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)\\n\\n# A simplified concept of an agent loop\\ncontext = \\"The user is asking about the weather in Moscow\\"\\n# The LLM decides: the get_weather(city) tool must be called\\ntool_result = call_weather_api(\\"Moscow\\")\\n# The LLM receives the result and composes the final answer for the user\\nfinal_answer = llm(f\\"The weather in Moscow: {tool_result}. Answer politely.\\")"],[1722,"The await operator suspends the execution of an asynchronous function until the Promise passed to it is fulfilled or rejected. It does not block the main thread of execution, because control returns to the event loop, which can handle other tasks. When the Promise settles, the function\'s execution resumes from the point where it was suspended. That lets you write asynchronous code that looks synchronous, making it easier to read and maintain.","The `await` keyword is used inside functions declared with `async`. Its main job is to make the JavaScript interpreter \\"wait\\" for a Promise to settle, but to do so in a non-blocking way.\\n\\nHow the suspension works\\n\\nWhen the engine encounters `await somePromise`, it immediately suspends the execution of the current asynchronous function. The function returns control to whoever called it (usually a higher level of code, or the event loop itself). The code after `await` does not run until the Promise moves into the `fulfilled` (success) or `rejected` (error) state.\\n\\nInteraction with the Event Loop\\n\\nThe Event Loop is the mechanism that manages the execution of code, processing the task queue and the microtask queue. When an asynchronous function\'s execution is suspended at `await`, the engine places a microtask in the microtask queue. That microtask contains the code that must resume once the Promise settles.\\n\\n- The Event Loop carries on running the synchronous code that follows the call to the asynchronous function.\\n\\n- When the call stack becomes empty, the Event Loop checks the microtask queue.\\n\\n- When the Promise that `await` is waiting on settles, the corresponding microtask is taken from the queue and run, resuming the suspended function with the Promise\'s result.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider a simple example with a delay and a data request.\\n\\nIn this example, when execution reaches the `await`, the `fetchData` function is suspended and control returns, letting `console.log(\'After the function call\')` run. A second later the Promise settles, a microtask to resume `fetchData` lands in the queue, and when the call stack is empty the Event Loop runs it, printing \\"The data has arrived\\".\\n\\nError handling\\n\\nIf the Promise passed to `await` is rejected, that results in an exception being thrown at the `await` point. That exception can be caught with `try...catch` inside the asynchronous function.\\n\\nSummary: The `await` operator is syntactic sugar for working with Promises that makes asynchronous code more linear and readable. It is worth using wherever you need to perform asynchronous operations in sequence without blocking the main thread, for example when working with APIs, reading files or performing any other I/O tasks.","async function fetchData() {\\n    console.log(\'Starting the request...\');\\n    // Simulate an asynchronous request with a delay\\n    const data = await new Promise(resolve => {\\n        setTimeout(() => resolve(\'The data has arrived\'), 1000);\\n    });\\n    console.log(data); // Runs once the Promise settles\\n    return data;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(\'Before the function call\');\\nfetchData();\\nconsole.log(\'After the function call\');\\n\\n// The console output:\\n// Before the function call\\n// Starting the request...\\n// After the function call\\n// (after 1 second) The data has arrived\\n\\nasync function riskyOperation() {\\n    try {\\n        const result = await Promise.reject(\'Something went wrong!\');\\n    } catch (error) {\\n        console.error(\'The error was caught:\', error);\\n    }\\n}"],[1723,"Re-running a function on error is implemented with a retry mechanism, which restarts the operation if it fails. The simplest way is to wrap the call to the function in a loop with a limited number of attempts and a check for success. For network requests an exponential backoff strategy is often used, where the pause between attempts grows exponentially to reduce the load on the server. That lets an application survive transient problems such as network timeouts or an overloaded service.","A retry mechanism is a design pattern that improves an application\'s resilience to transient failures. Instead of reporting an error immediately, the system makes several attempts to perform the operation before declaring it failed. This is especially useful for operations that depend on external resources: network requests, database connections or calls to third-party APIs, where failures are often transient.\\n\\nThe basic implementation principle\\n\\nThe main idea is to wrap the call to the function in a loop that runs until the operation succeeds or the attempt limit is exhausted. Inside the loop a try/catch block is used to catch exceptions. When an error occurs, execution is interrupted, the attempt counter is incremented, and after a pause the loop repeats.\\n\\nStrategies for the delay between attempts\\n\\nA simple retry with no delay can make the problem worse, for example by overloading a server that has fallen over. Delay strategies are therefore used:\\n\\n- A fixed delay: the same interval between every attempt.\\n\\n- Exponential backoff: the waiting time doubles with every new attempt (for example, 1 s, 2 s, 4 s). That gives the system time to recover.\\n\\n- Jitter: adding a random component to the delay, to prevent many clients synchronising.\\n\\nAn implementation example in JavaScript\\n\\nBelow is an example wrapper function with exponential backoff and a limit on attempts.\\n\\nWhere and how to apply it\\n\\nRetry logic is used in a wide range of scenarios:\\n\\n- HTTP clients and API calls: to handle 5xx statuses (server errors) or timeouts.\\n\\n- Database connections: when the database is temporarily unavailable.\\n\\n- Message queues: when processing messages from Kafka or RabbitMQ.\\n\\n- Background tasks and cron jobs: to ensure they complete even in the face of transient failures.\\n\\nIt is important not to apply retries to errors that are not transient (for example, 404 Not Found or 4xx validation errors). You should also set sensible limits on attempts and delays, so as not to block the system for a long time.\\n\\nConclusion: A retry mechanism is a key tool for building fault-tolerant applications. It is worth applying to operations where failures are likely but transient, such as network requests or interaction with external services. Using an exponential backoff strategy with jitter makes the implementation efficient and safe for distributed systems.","async function retryWithBackoff(operation, maxRetries = 3, baseDelay = 1000) {\\n  let lastError;\\n  \\n  for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {\\n    try {\\n      // Try to perform the asynchronous operation passed in\\n      return await operation();\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      lastError = error;\\n      console.warn(`Attempt ${attempt} failed:`, error.message);\\n      \\n      // If this was the last attempt, leave the loop\\n      if (attempt === maxRetries) break;\\n      \\n      // Compute the delay: baseDelay * 2^(attempt-1)\\n      const delay = baseDelay * Math.pow(2, attempt - 1);\\n      // Add a little randomness (jitter)\\n      const jitter = delay * 0.1 * Math.random();\\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay + jitter));\\n    }\\n  }\\n  \\n  // If every attempt is exhausted, rethrow the last error\\n  throw lastError;\\n}\\n\\n// An example of use: a function simulating an unreliable network request\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  const shouldFail = Math.random() > 0.5;\\n  if (shouldFail) throw new Error(\'Network timeout\');\\n  return { data: \'Success!\' };\\n}\\n\\n// Calling it with retries\\nretryWithBackoff(fetchData, 3, 1000)\\n  .then(result => console.log(\'Result:\', result))\\n  .catch(error => console.error(\'All attempts failed:\', error));"],[1724,"In most programming languages, such as JavaScript, Python or Java, the default scope depends on the context of the declaration. For example, in JavaScript a variable declared outside functions or blocks has global scope. In Python a variable assigned inside a function without any keyword becomes local. Understanding this helps you avoid accidentally polluting the global namespace and causing name conflicts.","Scope determines where in the code a variable or function is available for use. The default concept varies depending on the language and on the context of the declaration.\\n\\nScope in different languages\\n\\nHere is how it works in some popular languages:\\n\\n- JavaScript (before ES6): Variables declared with `var` outside functions have global scope. If `var` is used inside a function, the scope becomes function scope (local to the function).\\n\\n- JavaScript (ES6+): The `let` and `const` keywords introduce block scope. A variable declared with them inside a `{}` block (for example, in a loop or a condition) is available only inside that block.\\n\\n- Python: Variables assigned a value inside a function are considered local to that function by default, unless stated otherwise (for example, with `global` or `nonlocal`).\\n\\n- Java/C#: The default scope for variables declared inside a method is local to that method. For class fields you can specify access modifiers (`public`, `private` and so on).\\n\\nA practical example in JavaScript\\n\\nConsider the difference between `var` and `let`:\\n\\nThis code shows how `var` \\"floats\\" within the function while `let` is confined to the block. Understanding this behaviour is critical for writing clean, predictable code, especially in large projects.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nKnowing the default scope helps you:\\n\\n- Avoid accidentally redefining global variables, which can lead to elusive bugs.\\n\\n- Encapsulate logic properly inside functions or blocks.\\n\\n- Choose appropriate keywords for declaring variables depending on the lifetime and availability required.\\n\\nConclusion: The default scope is a fundamental concept that affects the availability of variables. In modern languages the use of block scope (`let`/`const` in JS) is often encouraged to make code more reliable. Apply this knowledge to create modular, safe programs, minimising the use of global scope.","// An example with var (function scope)\\nfunction exampleVar() {\\n  if (true) {\\n    var x = 10; // x is available throughout the function\\n  }\\n  console.log(x); // Prints: 10\\n}\\nexampleVar();\\n// console.log(x); // Error: x is not defined globally\\n\\n// An example with let (block scope)\\nfunction exampleLet() {\\n  if (true) {\\n    let y = 20; // y is available only inside this block\\n    console.log(y); // Prints: 20\\n  }\\n  // console.log(y); // Error: y is not defined\\n}\\nexampleLet();"],[1725,"An Iterable is an object that implements the Symbol.iterator method, which returns an Iterator. An Iterator is an object with a `next()` method that returns an object `{ value, done }`. An Iterable can be used in `for...of`, while an Iterator is the mechanism for stepping through. For example, an array is an Iterable, and calling `array[Symbol.iterator]()` returns an Iterator for that array.","In JavaScript the iteration protocol lets objects define or customise their behaviour during iteration, for example in a `for...of` loop. The key components of that protocol are the Iterable and Iterator interfaces.\\n\\nIterable\\n\\nAn object is iterable if it implements a method keyed by `Symbol.iterator`. That method must return an object conforming to the Iterator interface. The standard built-in types, such as Array, String, Map and Set, are iterable by default.\\n\\nIterator\\n\\nAn iterator is an object that implements a `next()` method. Every call to `next()` returns an object with two properties: `value` (the current value) and `done` (a boolean flag indicating whether the iteration has finished). The iterator manages the iteration\'s state (for example, the current position).\\n\\nHow they work together\\n\\nWhen a `for...of` loop runs over an iterable object, it first calls the `Symbol.iterator` method to obtain an iterator, and then calls `next()` on that iterator repeatedly until `done` becomes `true`. This separation makes it possible to have several independent iterators for the same iterable object.\\n\\nPractical application\\n\\n- Creating custom iterable data structures (for example, linked lists, trees).\\n\\n- Lazily generating sequences (infinite sequences, reading large files in pieces).\\n\\n- Use with the spread operator (`...`) and destructuring, which also work with iterable objects.\\n\\nConclusion: Use an Iterable when you need to define an object that can be iterated over (for example, in `for...of`). An Iterator is the low-level mechanism for managing the iteration process; it is useful when you need fine control over traversing the data or when implementing lazy evaluation.","const arr = [1, 2, 3];\\n// Obtain an iterator from the iterable object\\nconst iterator = arr[Symbol.iterator]();\\nconsole.log(iterator.next()); // { value: 1, done: false }\\n\\nfunction createSimpleIterator(max) {\\n  let count = 0;\\n  return {\\n    next() {\\n      if (count < max) {\\n        return { value: count++, done: false };\\n      }\\n      return { value: undefined, done: true };\\n    }\\n  };\\n}\\nconst myIterator = createSimpleIterator(3);\\nconsole.log(myIterator.next()); // { value: 0, done: false }\\n\\n// An example of a custom Iterable\\nconst range = {\\n  from: 1,\\n  to: 3,\\n  [Symbol.iterator]() {\\n    let current = this.from;\\n    let last = this.to;\\n    return {\\n      next() {\\n        if (current <= last) {\\n          return { value: current++, done: false };\\n        }\\n        return { done: true };\\n      }\\n    };\\n  }\\n};\\nfor (let num of range) {\\n  console.log(num); // 1, 2, 3\\n}"],[1726,"The finally block is for code that must always run, regardless of whether an exception occurred in the try block. It is the ideal place to release resources, such as closing files or network connections. The code in finally runs after try, and also after catch if an exception was caught. That provides reliability and prevents resource leaks.","The `try...catch...finally` construct in programming languages such as JavaScript, Java or C# is intended for handling exceptions. The `finally` block is an optional but extremely important part of it. Its key feature is that the code inside it is guaranteed to run after the attempt to execute the `try` block and, if necessary, the `catch` block.\\n\\nHow the finally block works\\n\\nThe order of execution is as follows:\\n\\n- The code inside the `try` block runs.\\n\\n- If an exception occurred in `try`, execution moves to the corresponding `catch` block.\\n\\n- If there was no exception, the `catch` block is skipped.\\n\\n- Regardless of the scenarios above (an exception occurred and was caught, an exception occurred and was not caught, there was no exception), the `finally` block always runs.\\n\\nThat makes it the ideal place for so-called cleanup operations.\\n\\nPractical application\\n\\nTypical scenarios for using `finally`:\\n\\n- Closing resources: Files, network connections or database handles opened in the `try` block must be closed to avoid leaks.\\n\\n- Resetting state: For example, clearing a loading flag or unlocking a user interface that was locked at the start of the operation.\\n\\n- Logging: Recording the fact that an operation finished (successfully or not) in a log.\\n\\nA code example in JavaScript\\n\\nEven if the `try` block contains a `return`, `break` or `continue` statement, the `finally` block runs before actually leaving the function or the loop. That behaviour is critically important for maintaining the integrity of the program\'s state.\\n\\nConclusion: The `finally` block should be used whenever the `try` block performs operations on external resources or changes state that has to be returned to its original condition regardless of whether the operation succeeded or failed. It is the foundation of writing reliable, error-resilient code.","function readFileSafely() {\\n  let fileHandle = null;\\n  try {\\n    console.log(\'Opening the file...\');\\n    // Simulating opening a resource\\n    fileHandle = { name: \'data.txt\', close: function() { console.log(\'The file has been closed.\'); } };\\n    // An error may occur\\n    throw new Error(\'Error reading the data!\');\\n    console.log(\'The data has been read.\'); // This line will not run\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(\'An error occurred:\', error.message);\\n    // The error can be handled, but the resource still has to be closed\\n  } finally {\\n    // This code ALWAYS runs\\n    if (fileHandle) {\\n      fileHandle.close(); // The file is guaranteed to be closed\\n    }\\n    console.log(\'The operation has finished (in finally).\');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nreadFileSafely();\\n// Output:\\n// Opening the file...\\n// An error occurred: Error reading the data!\\n// The file has been closed.\\n// The operation has finished (in finally)."],[1727,"A non-static synchronized method in Java synchronises on the object it is called on (the class instance, `this`). That means two threads cannot execute synchronized methods of the same object instance at the same time. Synchronisation provides thread safety by preventing different threads from changing the object\'s state simultaneously.","In Java the `synchronized` keyword is used to control several threads\' access to shared resources. When a method is declared `synchronized`, it acquires an intrinsic lock (a monitor) before it starts executing.\\n\\nWhat does the synchronisation happen on?\\n\\nFor a non-static synchronized method the monitor is the object the method is called on (that is, `this`). If two threads try to call any synchronized method of the same object instance, one of them will be blocked until the first releases the monitor by finishing the method.\\n\\nAn example and its use\\n\\nThis mechanism is used to protect an object\'s data from a race condition. Consider a simple counter class:\\n\\nIf several threads work with a single `Counter` instance, the calls to `increment()` will run one after another, guaranteeing a correct final count. Synchronisation at the method level is equivalent to synchronising on a block of code with `synchronized(this) { ... }`.\\n\\nImportant nuances\\n\\n- The synchronisation happens on the instance, not on the class. Two different objects of the same class do not block each other.\\n\\n- Static synchronized methods synchronise on the `Class` object (that is, `Counter.class`), which is a different monitor.\\n\\n- Using synchronized too broadly can reduce performance because of the locking.\\n\\nConclusion: A non-static synchronized method should be used when you need to provide thread safety for operations on a particular object instance\'s state and several threads may work with that instance at the same time.","public class Counter {\\n    private int value = 0;\\n\\n    // A non-static synchronized method\\n    public synchronized void increment() {\\n        value++; // This operation is now atomic\\n    }\\n\\n    public synchronized int getValue() {\\n        return value;\\n    }\\n}"],[1728,"Changes to the data during pagination can cause problems such as skipped or duplicated records. For example, if offset/limit pagination is used and a record on page 1 is deleted, then when moving to page 2 the user will skip one record. To solve this problem, cursor pagination is used, which anchors to a specific value in the data (for example, an ID or a timestamp), which makes it resilient to changes. Cursor pagination is harder to implement but keeps the data consistent when it is actively changing.","Pagination is a mechanism for splitting a large data set into separate pages so that it can be loaded and displayed efficiently. If the data changes (records are added, deleted or updated) while the user is moving between pages, however, that can break the expected order and the integrity of the information displayed.\\nThe problems of offset/limit pagination\\nThe classic approach uses the `offset` and `limit` parameters (the number of records per page). For example, the query `SELECT * FROM items ORDER BY id LIMIT 10 OFFSET 10` returns records 11 to 20. The main problems when the data changes:\\n\\n- Duplicated records: If a new record is added on the first page, the offset for the second page becomes stale and the user may see a record that was already on the previous page.\\n- Skipped records: If a record from the first page is deleted, the offset for the second page means one record will be skipped.\\nThe solution: cursor pagination\\nCursor (or keyset) pagination uses a unique, stable record identifier (the cursor), for example `id` or `created_at`, for navigation. Instead of an offset, the client passes the cursor value of the last record on the current page, and the next request fetches the records that follow that cursor.\\n\\nThis approach is resilient to records being added and deleted, since the navigation is anchored to specific data rather than to its position in the overall list.\\nWhere it is used\\nCursor pagination is widely used in social networks\' APIs (for example, Twitter, Facebook) and in any system with a frequently updated feed. Offset pagination suits static or rarely changing data, for example in administrative panels with filtering.\\nConclusion: Cursor pagination is worth using in dynamic interfaces where the data is frequently updated in real time, to avoid problems with duplicated and skipped records. Offset pagination is simpler to implement and suits reports or data that does not change during the user\'s session.","-- An example SQL query for cursor pagination by ID\\nSELECT * FROM items\\nWHERE id > last_seen_id  -- last_seen_id is the cursor passed by the client\\nORDER BY id\\nLIMIT 10;"],[1729,"Incrementing a primitive int (for example, in Java) is not an atomic operation at the processor level. It consists of three steps: reading the value from memory, increasing it by one and writing the result back. If two threads perform those steps at the same time, they may read the same initial value, increase it independently and write the same result, which means one increment is lost. To provide thread safety you need to use atomic types (AtomicInteger), synchronisation (synchronized) or locks.","Incrementing a variable of type `int` seems like a simple operation, but in a multithreaded context it breaks down into several machine instructions. That creates a window for a race condition, when several threads try to change the same variable at once.\\n\\nWhy is the operation not atomic?\\n\\nOn most architectures the operation `i++` (where `i` is a primitive `int`) is performed in three stages:\\n\\n- Reading the variable\'s current value from memory into a processor register.\\n\\n- Increasing that value by one (the computation in the register).\\n\\n- Writing the new value back into memory.\\n\\nThese stages are not performed as a single indivisible action (they are not atomic). A thread can be interrupted by the operating system after any of the steps.\\n\\nAn example of a race condition\\n\\nImagine two threads (A and B) incrementing the same variable `counter` at once, with an initial value of 0.\\n\\nHow do you provide thread safety?\\n\\nFor a safe increment in a multithreaded environment the following are used:\\n\\n- Synchronisation (the `synchronized` keyword in Java, or mutexes in other languages), which guarantees that only one thread can execute the critical section of code at any moment.\\n\\n- Atomic classes (for example, `AtomicInteger` in Java), which provide methods such as `incrementAndGet()` implemented with the processor\'s low-level atomic instructions (CAS — Compare-And-Swap).\\n\\n- Volatile variables (volatile) do not in themselves solve the increment problem, since they provide only the visibility of changes between threads, not the atomicity of compound operations.\\n\\nA code example with AtomicInteger\\n\\nConclusion: Incrementing a primitive type is not thread-safe because the operation is not atomic. This knowledge is critically important when developing multithreaded applications where shared data is changed by several threads. For counters and similar constructs you should use dedicated atomic types or explicit synchronisation.","// Pseudocode for the execution\\nThread A: reads counter (the value 0)\\nThread B: reads counter (the value 0) // The read happens before A wrote its result\\nThread A: increases the value read to 1\\nThread B: increases the value read to 1\\nThread A: writes 1 into counter\\nThread B: writes 1 into counter // One increment has been lost!\\n// The final value of counter = 1, although 2 was expected.\\n\\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;\\n\\npublic class SafeCounter {\\n    private AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);\\n\\n    public void increment() {\\n        counter.incrementAndGet(); // An atomic operation\\n    }\\n\\n    public int getValue() {\\n        return counter.get();\\n    }\\n}"],[1730,"Loggers provide a centralised, structured way of recording events in an application. They let you track errors, warnings and informational messages both in real time and historically. That simplifies debugging problems, analysing performance and auditing users\' actions. Without loggers, developers would have to rely on primitive methods like console.log, which are hard to scale and maintain.","Loggers are specialised libraries or components intended to record messages about events happening while a program runs. Unlike one-off `console.log` calls, they offer a systematic approach to managing an application\'s output.\\n\\nThe key advantages of using loggers\\n\\n- Structure and context: Loggers let you add metadata to messages: timestamps, a severity level (ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG), a request identifier, the module\'s name. That turns raw text into structured data that is easy to filter and analyse.\\n\\n- Flexible output (appenders/handlers): Messages can be directed to several places at once: the console, files, databases, monitoring systems (for example, the ELK stack or Grafana Loki), or sent as notifications to Slack/email on critical errors.\\n\\n- Logging levels: The ability to change the level of detail dynamically. In production usually only ERROR and WARN are written, while during debugging DEBUG is enabled to get exhaustive information, without rewriting the code.\\n\\n- Performance and control: Good loggers are asynchronous and buffer their writes, minimising the impact on the main application. They also help manage the rotation of log files so they do not fill the whole disk.\\n\\nPractical use and an example\\n\\nLoggers are critically important in server applications, microservice architectures and any system where state has to be tracked in real time. For example, when a payment fails in an online shop, the logger records the full context: the user\'s ID, the amount, the payment gateway\'s response and the error\'s stack trace.\\n\\nConclusion: Using loggers rather than ad-hoc output to the console is a mandatory practice for building maintainable, fault-tolerant, debuggable applications, especially in a production environment. They are the foundation of observability and save a great deal of time investigating incidents.","// An example in Node.js using the popular Winston library\\nconst winston = require(\'winston\');\\n\\n// Create a logger with a configuration\\nconst logger = winston.createLogger({\\n  level: \'info\',\\n  format: winston.format.combine(\\n    winston.format.timestamp(),\\n    winston.format.json() // Structured JSON on output\\n  ),\\n  transports: [\\n    // Writing errors into a separate file\\n    new winston.transports.File({ filename: \'error.log\', level: \'error\' }),\\n    // Writing all the logs into a combined file\\n    new winston.transports.File({ filename: \'combined.log\' }),\\n    // Output to the console in development\\n    new winston.transports.Console({\\n      format: winston.format.simple()\\n    })\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\n// Use in the application\'s code\\nlogger.info(\'The user has logged in\', { userId: 123 });\\nlogger.warn(\'A slow response from the API\', { endpoint: \'/api/data\', duration: 1200 });\\ntry {\\n  // ... code that may break\\n} catch (error) {\\n  logger.error(\'Error processing the payment\', {\\n    error: error.message,\\n    stack: error.stack,\\n    orderId: 456\\n  });\\n}"],[1731,"A retry mechanism is a strategy of repeating failed operations, usually network requests, to handle transient failures. The main approaches include a simple retry with a fixed delay, exponential backoff, which increases the pauses between attempts, and a combination with a Circuit Breaker, which prevents an avalanche of requests to a broken service. It is also important to consider the idempotency of the operations and to limit the maximum number of attempts, so as not to overload the system.","A retry mechanism is a key technique for improving the resilience of distributed systems. It lets you automatically repeat an operation that failed, in the hope that the failure was transient (for example, a network timeout, a service being temporarily unavailable or heavy load). Without such a mechanism an application can fail frequently because of fleeting problems, which degrades the user experience and reliability.\\nThe main implementation approaches\\n\\n- A simple retry with a fixed delay: After a failure the operation is repeated after a constant interval (for example, 1 second). Simple to implement, but it can make the load on a struggling service worse if the failure is prolonged.\\n- Exponential backoff: The delay between attempts grows exponentially (for example, 1 s, 2 s, 4 s, 8 s...). It is often combined with random jitter to spread out the requests from many clients in time. That reduces the load on a recovering service.\\n- Circuit Breaker: This pattern complements retries. When errors are frequent the \\"circuit\\" opens and subsequent requests are rejected immediately without a real call, giving the service time to recover. After a timeout a probe attempt is made, and if it succeeds the circuit closes.\\n- Limiting attempts and giving up: A maximum number of attempts (maxRetries) is always set. Once it is exhausted the error is propagated upwards, so as not to loop indefinitely.\\nA practical example in JavaScript\\nBelow is an example function with exponential backoff and jitter that repeats an asynchronous call.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nRetry logic is critically important in microservice architectures, when calling external APIs (payment gateways, weather services), in database operations during transient connection problems, and also in background tasks and message queues (for example, RabbitMQ, Kafka). Many libraries (for example, Polly for .NET, resilience4j for Java, axios-retry for JavaScript) provide ready-made, configurable implementations.\\nConclusion: A retry mechanism with exponential backoff and a Circuit Breaker should be applied to improve the fault tolerance of any operation subject to transient failures, especially in distributed systems, where it is impossible to guarantee that every component is always available.","async function fetchWithRetry(url, maxRetries = 3) {\\n  let lastError;\\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {\\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch(url);\\n      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\\n      return await response.json();\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      lastError = error;\\n      if (attempt === maxRetries) break;\\n      // Exponential backoff with jitter\\n      const delay = Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2, attempt) + Math.random() * 100, 10000);\\n      console.log(`Attempt ${attempt+1} failed. Retrying in ${delay.toFixed(0)}ms...`);\\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay));\\n    }\\n  }\\n  throw lastError; // After every attempt, propagate the error\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\n// fetchWithRetry(\'https://api.example.com/data\').then(data => console.log(data));"],[1732,"A retry can be implemented by wrapping an asynchronous operation in a loop with a limit on the number of attempts and a delay between them. Create a function that takes another function (the operation), the maximum number of attempts and the delay parameters. Inside, use try/catch: on success return the result; on error increment the counter, wait (for example, via setTimeout or a promise with a delay) and repeat. It is important to handle only retryable errors (for example, network timeouts) rather than fatal ones (for example, a 404).","A retry mechanism is used when an operation may fail temporarily because of network instability, an overloaded server or other transient problems. Instead of reporting an error to the user straight away, the system makes several attempts to perform the action, which improves the application\'s reliability.\\n\\nThe main components of an implementation\\n\\nTo create retry logic without libraries you need to decide on several key parameters:\\n\\n- The maximum number of attempts: so as not to loop indefinitely.\\n\\n- The delay strategy: for example, a constant pause, exponential backoff or random jitter, to avoid a thundering herd of requests.\\n\\n- The conditions for retrying: not every error is worth repeating (for example, a 400 \\"Bad Request\\" error will not be fixed by a retry, while a 503 \\"Service Unavailable\\" may be).\\n\\nAn implementation example in JavaScript\\n\\nBelow is a `retry` function for asynchronous operations that return a Promise. It uses exponential backoff and a check of the error\'s type.\\n\\nWhere to apply it and its limitations\\n\\nRetry logic is useful when calling external APIs, querying databases or in any I/O operation where transient failures are possible. It is important not to apply it blindly, however:\\n\\n- Avoid retrying idempotent operations (for example, GET requests), so as not to create unnecessary load.\\n\\n- For non-idempotent operations (POST, DELETE), make sure a retry will not lead to duplication or unwanted side effects.\\n\\n- Always configure sensible limits on attempts and delays, so as not to block the user for a long time.\\n\\nConclusion: Implementing a retry without libraries lets you control the retry strategy flexibly and understand the error-handling mechanism better. Use it to improve the resilience of applications that work with unreliable external services, but always account for idempotency and the nature of the errors.","async function retry(operation, maxAttempts = 3, delayMs = 1000) {\\n  let lastError;\\n  \\n  for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {\\n    try {\\n      // Try to perform the operation\\n      return await operation();\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      lastError = error;\\n      // Check whether it is worth retrying (for example, only network errors)\\n      if (!isRetryableError(error) || attempt === maxAttempts) {\\n        throw new Error(`Failed after ${attempt} attempts: ${error.message}`);\\n      }\\n      \\n      // Exponential backoff: delayMs * 2^(attempt-1)\\n      const waitTime = delayMs * Math.pow(2, attempt - 1);\\n      console.log(`Attempt ${attempt} failed. Retrying in ${waitTime}ms...`);\\n      await sleep(waitTime);\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// A helper function for the pause\\nfunction sleep(ms) {\\n  return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));\\n}\\n\\n// An example check of whether an error can be retried\\nfunction isRetryableError(error) {\\n  // Network errors or 5xx statuses are usually retryable\\n  return error.code === \'ECONNRESET\' || \\n         error.code === \'ETIMEDOUT\' || \\n         (error.response && error.response.status >= 500);\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  const response = await fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\');\\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\\n  return response.json();\\n}\\n\\n// Calling it with a retry\\nretry(fetchData, 4, 1000)\\n  .then(data => console.log(\'Data:\', data))\\n  .catch(err => console.error(\'Final error:\', err));"],[1733,"A closure is a function that remembers the variables from its outer scope even when that outer function has already finished running. That happens because in JavaScript every function has a reference to its lexical environment. Closures are useful for creating private variables, for currying and for handling events. For example, a counter that keeps its state between calls is often implemented with a closure.","A closure is a fundamental concept in JavaScript that arises from the combination of a function and the lexical environment in which that function was declared. The lexical environment contains all the local variables available at the moment the function was created. When a function returns another function, the inner function keeps a reference to that environment, even though the outer function has already finished executing.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nThe JavaScript engine creates a lexical environment for every function that runs. That environment includes the local variables, the function\'s arguments and a reference to the outer lexical environment (the scope chain). When a function finishes, its environment is normally removed by the garbage collector. If an inner function declared inside it goes on existing (for example, is returned or assigned to a variable), however, the reference to that environment is kept, preventing its removal. That is a closure.\\n\\nWhere closures are used\\n\\n- Private variables: Creating variables that are inaccessible from outside but available to the inner functions.\\n\\n- Currying and partial application: Fixing some of a function\'s arguments to create a new function.\\n\\n- Event handlers and callbacks: Preserving the context (for example, a loop variable) for asynchronous operations.\\n\\n- Modules and the \\"module\\" pattern: Encapsulating logic and state.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nIn this example the `createCounter` function creates a local variable `count` and returns an inner function that increments and returns its value. Every call to `counter()` changes the same lexical environment, so the value of `count` is preserved between calls.\\n\\nImportant aspects\\n\\n- Closures can lead to memory leaks if you carelessly hold references to large objects.\\n\\n- Creating functions inside a loop requires care, so that each function captures the right value of the variable (often solved with `let` or an additional wrapper function).\\n\\nConclusion: Closures are a powerful tool for managing scope and state in JavaScript. They are worth using when you need to encapsulate data, create functions with memory, or implement patterns such as modules and factories.","function createCounter() {\\n  let count = 0; // A private variable, accessible only through the closure\\n\\n  return function() {\\n    count += 1;\\n    return count;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst counter = createCounter();\\nconsole.log(counter()); // 1\\nconsole.log(counter()); // 2\\nconsole.log(counter()); // 3\\n// The count variable is not accessible directly from outside"],[1734,"A process is an instance of a running program with its own address space in memory, its own file descriptors and other resources. A thread is a unit of execution inside a process that shares the memory and resources with the other threads of that process but has its own stack and processor registers. Processes are isolated from each other, while threads inside a single process can communicate through shared memory, which makes them more lightweight but also more prone to synchronisation errors.","In operating systems, processes and threads are the main units of execution, but they serve different purposes and have a different architecture.\\nWhat is a process?\\nA process is an instance of a program being executed. The operating system allocates it its own virtual address space, a table of file descriptors, environment variables and other resources. Processes are strongly isolated: one process cannot read or write another process\'s memory directly without special inter-process communication (IPC) mechanisms, such as pipes, shared memory or message queues. Creating a process (fork) is usually more expensive than creating a thread, because the address space and resources have to be duplicated.\\nWhat is a thread?\\nA thread (sometimes called a lightweight process) exists inside a process. All the threads of one process share its address space, global variables, open files and other resources. Every thread has its own call stack and processor registers, which lets it execute code independently. Since threads share memory, exchanging data between them is simple (through shared variables), but it requires careful synchronisation (with mutexes, semaphores) to avoid race conditions and data corruption.\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- Memory isolation: Processes are isolated; threads share memory.\\n- The cost of creation: Creating a process is resource-intensive; threads are created faster.\\n- Communication: Inter-process communication is more complex and slower; threads communicate through shared memory.\\n- Fault tolerance: A failure in one thread can bring down the whole process; a failed process usually does not affect other processes.\\n- Scheduling: The OS scheduler works with threads as the units of execution, but processes are containers for threads.\\nAn example in Python\\nThe following code demonstrates creating several threads that work with a shared variable, and the need for synchronisation.\\n\\nIn this example two threads increment a shared counter. Without the `lock`, the final value will most likely be less than 200000 because of a race condition. Using a mutex guarantees the operation is atomic.\\nWhere are they used?\\n\\n- Processes are used when maximum isolation and fault tolerance are needed (for example, microservices, web browsers with tabs as separate processes).\\n- Threads are effective for I/O-bound tasks inside a single application, where concurrency with shared state is required (for example, a web server handling many connections).\\nConclusion: Use processes when isolation and stability matter, and threads when you need lightweight concurrency inside a single application with shared access to data and are prepared to manage the synchronisation.","import threading\\n\\n# A shared variable available to every thread\\ncounter = 0\\nlock = threading.Lock()\\n\\ndef increment():\\n    global counter\\n    for _ in range(100000):\\n        # Without the lock a race condition would cause an error\\n        with lock:\\n            counter += 1\\n\\n# Create two threads\\nthread1 = threading.Thread(target=increment)\\nthread2 = threading.Thread(target=increment)\\n\\nthread1.start()\\nthread2.start()\\nthread1.join()\\nthread2.join()\\n\\nprint(f\\"The final value of the counter: {counter}\\")  # Should be 200000"],[1735,"The increment operation (for example, `i++`) in most programming languages is NOT atomic at the level of machine instructions. It usually consists of three steps: reading the value from memory, increasing it by one and writing the result back. In a multithreaded environment another thread may read a stale value between those steps, which leads to a race condition and lost updates. To provide atomicity you have to use special synchronisation mechanisms, such as mutexes, atomic types or the atomic operations the language or a library provides.","The concept of atomicity means that an operation is performed as a single, indivisible action: either it completes in full or it does not happen at all, and while it is being performed the system cannot switch to another task that would see an intermediate state. This is extremely important when working with shared resources in multithreaded programs.\\n\\nWhy is an increment not atomic?\\n\\nConsider the simple operation `counter++` in C or a similar language. At the level of machine code it may be broken into several instructions:\\n\\nIf two threads perform this operation at the same time, their instructions can interleave. For example, both threads may read the same initial value (step 1), increase it locally (step 2) and write the same result, which means one of the increments is lost.\\n\\nHow do you provide atomicity?\\n\\nFor a safe increment of a shared counter you have to use synchronisation mechanisms:\\n\\n- Mutexes: they block other threads\' access to the variable for the duration of the operation.\\n\\n- Atomic types: many modern languages provide special types (for example, `std::atomic<int>` in C++ or `AtomicInteger` in Java) whose operations are guaranteed atomic at the processor level.\\n\\n- Atomic operations: some languages or libraries offer functions for an atomic increment (for example, `InterlockedIncrement` in the Windows API or `__sync_fetch_and_add` in GCC).\\n\\nAn example in C++ using std::atomic\\n\\nIn this example using `std::atomic<int>` guarantees that every increment is performed atomically and the final value is predictable.\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\nUnderstanding atomicity is critical in:\\n\\n- Developing multithreaded servers (request counters, statistics).\\n\\n- Creating parallel algorithms (for example, map-reduce).\\n\\n- Working with shared data structures (queues, caches).\\n\\n- Real-time systems and embedded systems.\\n\\nConclusion: The standard increment operation is not atomic, which can lead to errors in a multithreaded environment. To change shared variables safely you have to use synchronisation or the atomic types the language or platform provides explicitly.","// Pseudocode of the machine instructions for counter++\\n1. Load the value of counter from memory into a register.\\n2. Increase the value in the register by 1.\\n3. Store the updated value from the register back into memory.\\n\\n#include <iostream>\\n#include <thread>\\n#include <atomic>\\n#include <vector>\\n\\nstd::atomic<int> counter{0}; // An atomic counter\\n\\nvoid increment(int times) {\\n    for (int i = 0; i < times; ++i) {\\n        counter++; // This operation is now atomic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    std::vector<std::thread> threads;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {\\n        threads.emplace_back(increment, 1000);\\n    }\\n    for (auto& t : threads) {\\n        t.join();\\n    }\\n    std::cout << \\"Final counter value: \\" << counter << std::endl; // Always 10000\\n    return 0;\\n}"],[1736,"The order in which hooks are called matters because React relies on it to manage state and side effects correctly. Hooks must be called at the top level of a component, not inside conditions, loops or nested functions. That guarantees the hooks are called in the same sequence on every render, which lets React map state and effects correctly between renders. Breaking this rule leads to errors, for example mismatched state or missed updates.","Hooks in React are functions that let you use state and other React features in functional components. For them to work correctly, React internally tracks the order in which hooks are called on every render of a component.\\nWhy the order matters\\nReact stores hooks\' state as a list associated with the component. On the first render React remembers the order in which the hooks were called (for example, `useState` first, then `useEffect`). On subsequent renders React expects that order to stay unchanged, so it can map the previous state to the new hook call correctly. If the order changes (for example, because of a conditional statement), React \\"loses\\" the connection, which leads to errors.\\nAn example of breaking the order\\nConsider code where a hook call depends on a condition:\\n\\nIf `isVisible` changes from `true` to `false`, then on the next render there will be no `useState` hook. React expected the second call (for `useEffect`) but receives the first, which causes confusion in the state and, most likely, an error.\\nCorrect use\\nHooks must always be called at the top level of a component, guaranteeing the same sequence on every render:\\n\\nThis rule applies to every hook, including custom ones. It makes a component\'s behaviour predictable and stable.\\nConclusion: Observing the order in which hooks are called is critical for managing state and the life cycle correctly in React components; always call hooks at the top level, avoiding conditions and loops.","function MyComponent({ isVisible }) {\\n  if (isVisible) {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // The hook is called only when isVisible = true\\n  }\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log(\'Effect\');\\n  });\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent({ isVisible }) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // Always called first\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    if (isVisible) {\\n      console.log(count); // The logic inside the effect can be conditional\\n    }\\n  }, [isVisible, count]);\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}"],[1737,"useCallback is not needed when the function is not passed down the component tree as a prop. If the function is used only inside the component and does not depend on changing dependencies, memoising it is superfluous. There is also no point in using useCallback for simple components that rarely re-render, since the overhead of memoisation may exceed the benefit. Inline event handlers that do not cause unnecessary re-renders of child components likewise do not need useCallback.","The `useCallback` hook in React is intended for memoising functions so that they are not recreated on every render of a component. That is useful when a function is passed down as a prop to child components, especially ones wrapped in `React.memo`, to avoid unnecessary re-renders. Using it is not always justified, however, and can even make performance worse.\\n\\nWhen useCallback is not needed\\n\\n- The function is only used locally: If the function is not passed to child components, memoising it will not prevent unnecessary re-renders of other parts of the application.\\n\\n- The component is simple and rarely re-renders: For small, isolated components the overhead of calling `useCallback` (comparing dependencies, holding it in memory) may be greater than the benefit of not recreating the function.\\n\\n- The function has no dependencies, or they are stable: If the dependency array is empty or contains primitives that never change, the function will be stable anyway, but memoising it still adds a small cost.\\n\\n- The child components are not optimised: If the child components are not wrapped in `React.memo` or `useMemo` and are not pure class components, they will re-render regardless of how stable the props are.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider a component where `useCallback` is redundant:\\n\\nHere `handleClick` is not passed as a prop, so recreating it on every render has no negative consequences. Adding `useCallback` would only complicate the code to no benefit.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nUse `useCallback` deliberately: only when the function is passed to optimised child components and its instability causes excessive re-renders. In every other case, especially in simple components, forgo it in favour of readability and simplicity.","function SimpleForm() {\\n  // A local handler, not passed down\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    console.log(\'Button clicked\');\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[1738,"In React, timers are created with JavaScript\'s native setTimeout and setInterval functions. They should be started inside the useEffect hook, to tie them to the component\'s life cycle. The key point is clearing the timers in the cleanup function returned from useEffect. That prevents callbacks running after the component has unmounted, which could cause errors and memory leaks. The useRef hook can be used to manage the timer\'s state.","Working with timers in React requires particular attention because of the component model and the life cycle. If you simply call setTimeout or setInterval in the body of a functional component, a timer will be created on every render, which leads to an uncontrolled accumulation of timers and to memory leaks. The right approach is to use the useEffect hook to manage side effects, which timers are.\\nA basic example with setTimeout\\nHere is how you can implement a one-off timer:\\n\\nAn example with setInterval\\nFor periodic actions the logic is the same, but the cleanup matters even more:\\n\\nUsing useRef to hold the timer\'s identifier lets you access it from the component\'s other functions (for example, a click handler to stop it).\\nImportant aspects\\n\\n- Cleanup: Always return a cleanup function from useEffect that calls clearTimeout or clearInterval. That guarantees the timer will not fire on an unmounted component.\\n- Dependencies: If the timer has to restart when some prop or piece of state changes, list them in useEffect\'s dependency array. The cleanup function will then run before every new run of the effect.\\n- useRef vs useState: To hold the timer\'s ID it is better to use useRef, since changing it does not trigger a re-render of the component, unlike useState.\\nTimers are used for deferred actions (for example, a debounced search), animations, periodic polling of a server or building countdowns.\\nConclusion: Use the useEffect hook to create and — critically — to clear timers. This pattern prevents common errors and makes working with asynchronous operations in React safe and predictable.","import { useEffect, useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction TimerComponent() {\\n  const [message, setMessage] = useState(\'\');\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // Set the timer\\n    const timerId = setTimeout(() => {\\n      setMessage(\'The timer fired!\');\\n    }, 2000);\\n\\n    // The cleanup function\\n    return () => {\\n      clearTimeout(timerId);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty dependency array = the effect runs once, on mount\\n\\n  return <div>{message}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useEffect, useState, useRef } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const intervalRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n    };\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const stopTimer = () => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={stopTimer}>Stop</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[1739,"The criteria for choosing a state manager include the application\'s complexity, the performance requirements, the team\'s preferences and the ecosystem. For small projects, lightweight solutions such as Zustand or the Context API are suitable. In large applications with frequent state updates, predictability and developer tools matter, as in Redux. It is also worth assessing the learning curve and the integration with your framework.","Choosing a state-management library is an architectural decision that affects an application\'s maintainability, performance and development speed. There is no single \\"best\\" solution; the choice depends on the project\'s and the team\'s specific requirements.\\nThe key selection criteria\\n\\n- The application\'s complexity: For simple SPAs with a small amount of state, React\'s Context API or lightweight libraries (Zustand, Valtio) are often enough. For large enterprise applications with many interrelated pieces of data and complex business logic, Redux, MobX or XState are suitable.\\n- Performance and optimisation: How the library handles updates matters. Some (like Redux) require immutable updates and can cause unnecessary re-renders without optimisations. Others (MobX, Valtio) use proxies and track changes automatically, minimising re-renders.\\n- Predictability and debugging: Libraries such as Redux offer a strict unidirectional data flow and powerful developer tools (Redux DevTools), which simplifies debugging and reproducing states. That is critical in large teams.\\n- The learning curve and the team\'s preferences: Redux requires an understanding of reducers, actions and middleware. MobX uses reactive programming. Zustand offers a simpler API. The choice should match the team\'s experience.\\n- Integration with the ecosystem: Check the compatibility with your framework (React, Vue, Angular) and the availability of middleware (for example, Redux Thunk/Saga for asynchronous operations). The bundle size and TypeScript support also matter.\\nA practical comparison\\nConsider a simple counter implemented with Zustand and with Redux Toolkit (a simplified version of Redux).\\n\\nZustand offers more concise code out of the box, while Redux Toolkit requires more boilerplate but provides a better structure for scaling.\\nConclusion: Choose lightweight libraries (Zustand, Jotai) for startups and small projects where development speed matters. For large, long-term projects with a big team, solutions with a strict architecture and debugging tools, such as Redux or MobX, are preferable. Always weigh the trade-off between simplicity and control.","// An example with Zustand (simplicity)\\nimport create from \'zustand\';\\n\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  count: 0,\\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\\n  decrement: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count - 1 })),\\n}));\\n\\n// In a component:\\n// const { count, increment } = useStore();\\n\\n// An example with Redux Toolkit (structure)\\nimport { createSlice, configureStore } from \'@reduxjs/toolkit\';\\n\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: \'counter\',\\n  initialState: { value: 0 },\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment: (state) => { state.value += 1; },\\n    decrement: (state) => { state.value -= 1; },\\n  },\\n});\\n\\nconst store = configureStore({ reducer: counterSlice.reducer });\\n// Then it is wired up through a Provider and used with the useSelector/useDispatch hooks"],[1740,"Business logic and UI components are separated in order to isolate the domain\'s rules from the interface-rendering code. That makes the logic independent of the framework and easy to test. The UI components become \\"dumb\\" and are responsible only for display and for passing on user events. This approach simplifies refactoring and lets the logic be reused on different platforms.","Separating business logic and UI components is a key design principle that improves the quality of the code. Business logic contains the rules, computations and data operations specific to the application\'s domain. UI components are responsible for rendering the interface and handling user input. Mixing these layers leads to \\"fat\\" components that are hard to test and maintain.\\n\\nThe main approaches to separation\\n\\n- The container/presenter pattern: Container (\\"smart\\") components manage the data and logic, while presentational (\\"dumb\\") ones only display it.\\n\\n- Custom hooks (React): The logic is moved into reusable hooks that components use to obtain state and functions.\\n\\n- Services/stores (for example, Redux): State and business logic are held and managed centrally, outside the components.\\n\\n- MVVM/MVP: Architectural patterns where the ViewModel/Presenter acts as an intermediary between the View and the Model.\\n\\nA practical example in React with hooks\\n\\nInstead of putting the counting logic and the state directly in the component, we move them into a custom hook.\\n\\nIn this example the `useCounter` hook encapsulates all the logic for working with the counter. It can be tested separately from React. The `Counter` component becomes simple and predictable: it only displays the data and calls the functions passed to it.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nThis approach is critically important in large applications, where the logic is complex and changes often. It is used in every modern framework (React, Vue, Angular) and is the foundation of patterns such as Flux/Redux. The separation lets teams work in parallel: some develop the services and hooks, others the interfaces.\\n\\nConclusion: Separate business logic and the UI in order to create flexible, testable, maintainable applications. This is especially useful when requirements change often or when the same logic has to be used in different parts of the interface (for example, on the web and in a mobile application).","// useCounter.js - the business logic\\nimport { useState, useCallback } from \'react\';\\n\\nexport function useCounter(initialValue = 0) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(initialValue);\\n\\n  const increment = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const decrement = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev - 1);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const reset = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(initialValue);\\n  }, [initialValue]);\\n\\n  // Return the state and the API for changing it\\n  return { count, increment, decrement, reset };\\n}\\n\\n// Counter.js - the \\"dumb\\" UI component\\nimport React from \'react\';\\nimport { useCounter } from \'./useCounter\';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const { count, increment, decrement, reset } = useCounter();\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>+</button>\\n      <button onClick={decrement}>-</button>\\n      <button onClick={reset}>Reset</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nexport default Counter;"],[1741,"Integrating UI libraries with SSR requires solving the problems of hydration, the absence of the DOM API on the server, and state synchronisation. On the server the components are rendered into static HTML, but on the client the library has to bring that HTML to life by attaching event handlers. The key difficulties are working with browser APIs (window, document), which are unavailable on the server, and making the component tree identical between the server and the client so that hydration succeeds.","Server-Side Rendering (SSR) is a technique where the HTML for a page is generated on the server and sent to the client ready-made. That improves SEO and the time to first paint. When modern UI libraries such as React, Vue or Svelte are used, however, the integration with SSR has a number of particular features that must be accounted for if the application is to work correctly.\\n\\nThe key features and problems\\n\\n- Hydration: Once the server has sent the static HTML, the UI library on the client has to bring it to life — attach event handlers and restore interactivity. That process is called hydration. The library compares the HTML generated by the server with what it would itself have rendered on the client. If the trees do not match, a hydration error occurs.\\n\\n- The absence of browser APIs: On the server there are no `window`, `document` or `localStorage` objects. Code that accesses them must run only on the client, otherwise the application will crash.\\n\\n- State synchronisation: The application\'s state (for example, data from an API) used during the server render has to be passed to the client, to avoid repeated requests and to ensure an identical initial render.\\n\\n- Styles (CSS-in-JS): Many styling libraries generate classes dynamically. On the server you have to extract the generated CSS rules and insert them into the response, otherwise the styles will not be applied.\\n\\nPractical examples and solutions\\n\\nConsider a typical problem with using the `window` object in a React component.\\n\\nThe solution is to use a check for running in a browser, or hooks that fire only on the client (for example, `useEffect`).\\n\\nTo synchronise state in React applications, Next.js is often used, which automatically serialises the state obtained in `getServerSideProps` functions and passes it to the client.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nSSR with UI libraries is critically important for public web applications where search engine optimisation (SEO) and the speed of the first load matter. That is typical of marketplaces, news portals, blogs and any site with dynamic content that has to be indexed by search engines.\\n\\nConclusion: Integrating UI libraries with SSR requires attention to hydration, to running browser code conditionally and to synchronising state. Use frameworks (Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit) that provide built-in solutions to these problems, or organise the code carefully, separating the logic for the server and the client.","// A problematic component that will break under SSR\\nfunction UserLocation() {\\n  // window is available only in the browser\\n  const [city, setCity] = useState(window.location.pathname);\\n  return <div>Your city: {city}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// The fixed component\\nfunction UserLocation() {\\n  const [city, setCity] = useState(\'\');\\n  // useEffect runs only on the client\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setCity(window.location.pathname);\\n  }, []);\\n  // Show a placeholder during SSR\\n  return <div>Your city: {city || \'Loading...\'}</div>;\\n}"],[1742,"State management in micro frontends can be organised in several ways. The simplest is isolation: every micro frontend manages its own state independently. To exchange data between them you can use the Event Bus pattern, publishing and subscribing to events via window or a library. In more complex scenarios a shared global state is used — a common store available to every micro frontend, for example through Redux or the host application\'s context. The key task is to minimise coupling, so that changes in one module do not break the others.","Micro frontends are an architectural style in which a web application is broken into independent, autonomous parts developed and deployed separately. Managing state in such a system is a non-trivial task, since you have to balance the modules\' independence against the need to exchange data.\\n\\nThe main approaches to state management\\n\\n- Isolated state: Every micro frontend (MFE) manages its own state inside its own bundle. That is ideal for maximum independence and reuse. If MFEs need to display the same data (for example, the user\'s details), however, it will have to be loaded several times.\\n\\n- Shared state through an Event Bus: The MFEs communicate with each other by publishing and listening for events. That can be implemented with the browser\'s native CustomEvent API or libraries such as `mitt`. For example, when the basket is updated in one MFE you can emit an event that other modules displaying the number of items subscribe to.\\n\\n- Shared global state: A single store is created (for example, a Redux or MobX instance, or even a simple object on window) available to every MFE. Initialising and providing it is usually the host application\'s (the shell\'s) responsibility. This approach requires clear contracts and can increase coupling.\\n\\n- State at the URL and browser level: Part of the state (for example, the ID of the selected item, filter parameters) can be held in the URL\'s query parameters or in localStorage/sessionStorage. That is a simple way of synchronising between MFEs mounted on the same page.\\n\\nA practical example with an Event Bus\\n\\nConsider a simple example where two independent micro frontends exchange information about the user.\\n\\nRecommendations and conclusion\\n\\nThe choice of approach depends on the level of integration between the micro frontends. Start from maximum isolation, adding shared-state mechanisms (an Event Bus, URL parameters) only as a real need arises. Avoid direct dependencies between the MFEs\' bundles. A shared global store is worth using for data that is genuinely critical to the whole application, such as authentication or the colour theme, and its schema should be managed carefully.\\n\\nSummary: Isolated state is the foundation of micro frontends\' independence. An Event Bus and URL parameters are good tools for loose coupling. Use a shared global store judiciously for cross-cutting functionality, so as not to turn the micro frontends into a monolith at the level of the data.","// In the host application or a shared module we create a simple Event Bus\\nconst eventBus = {\\n  events: {},\\n  on(event, callback) {\\n    if (!this.events[event]) this.events[event] = [];\\n    this.events[event].push(callback);\\n  },\\n  emit(event, data) {\\n    (this.events[event] || []).forEach(cb => cb(data));\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// Micro frontend A (for example, the header) subscribes to updates of the user\'s name\\neventBus.on(\'user-updated\', (userData) => {\\n  document.getElementById(\'user-name\').textContent = userData.name;\\n});\\n\\n// Micro frontend B (the login form) emits an event after a successful login\\nfunction handleLoginSuccess(user) {\\n  // ... MFE B\'s logic\\n  eventBus.emit(\'user-updated\', { name: user.name, id: user.id });\\n}"],[1743,"Validating data on the client with schemas is the process of checking the user\'s input against previously defined rules (schemas) before sending it to the server. Popular libraries such as Zod or Yup let you describe those rules declaratively. That gives the user instant feedback, reduces the load on the server and prevents incorrect data being sent. This approach is especially important in registration or payment forms, or any input fields.","Validating data on the client is a critically important stage in web development that ensures the information a user enters is correct before it is sent to the server. Using validation schemas lets you structure that process, making it more reliable and easier to maintain. A schema is a set of rules describing the expected structure of, and constraints on, the data (for example, a string of a particular length, a number in a range, a mandatory field).\\n\\nPopular libraries for schema validation\\n\\nThe most widespread tools are:\\n\\n- Zod: A library for TypeScript with an emphasis on type safety and on inferring types from schemas.\\n\\n- Yup: A lightweight library, often used with Formik, with a simple, comprehensible syntax.\\n\\n- Joi (more often on the server, but it can be used on the client too): A powerful library with a rich set of validators.\\n\\nHow it is used\\n\\nValidation schemas are integrated into the form-handling process. When a field changes or the form is submitted, the data is checked against the schema. If the validation fails, the user sees an error message next to the field. That gives instant feedback and prevents an incorrect request being sent.\\n\\nA code example using Zod\\n\\nIn the example above, `safeParse` returns an object that safely indicates success or failure, which is convenient to handle. The errors contain detailed information that can be displayed to the user.\\n\\nThe advantages of the approach\\n\\n- A single source of truth: The validation rules are described in one place (the schema) rather than scattered through the code.\\n\\n- Type safety (TypeScript): Libraries such as Zod generate TypeScript types from the schemas automatically, which rules out type mismatches.\\n\\n- Reuse: The same schema can be used for validation on the client and, if needed, on the server.\\n\\n- A better UX: The user sees the errors immediately, without waiting for the server\'s response.\\n\\nConclusion: Using validation schemas on the client is a modern, effective approach worth applying in every form where the correctness of the data entered matters. It is especially useful in complex forms with many fields and interrelated rules, since it makes the validation code predictable and easy to test.","import { z } from \'zod\';\\n\\n// 1. Define the schema for the registration form\'s data\\nconst userSchema = z.object({\\n  email: z.string().email(\'Invalid email\'),\\n  password: z.string().min(8, \'The password must be at least 8 characters\'),\\n  age: z.number().int().min(18, \'You must be at least 18\').optional(),\\n});\\n\\n// 2. The data the user entered\\nconst userInput = {\\n  email: \'user@example\', // Error: an invalid email\\n  password: \'123\',        // Error: the password is too short\\n};\\n\\n// 3. The validation attempt\\nconst result = userSchema.safeParse(userInput);\\n\\nif (!result.success) {\\n  // 4. Handling the errors\\n  console.log(result.error.errors);\\n  // Prints an array of objects describing the error for each field\\n  // This can be displayed in the interface\\n} else {\\n  // The data is valid and can be sent to the server\\n  const validData = result.data;\\n  console.log(\'The data passed validation:\', validData);\\n}"],[1744,"Code review is the process of other developers inspecting source code before it is merged into the main branch. The main goal is to improve the code\'s quality, find errors, ensure conformance with the standards and share knowledge. Reviewers pay attention to the correctness of the logic, readability, performance, security and test coverage. It is an important practice for team work and for preventing bugs in production.","Code review is the systematic inspection of source code written by one developer by other members of the team, before the changes are integrated into the main code base. This process is a cornerstone of modern development practices such as Git Flow and CI/CD, and serves several critically important purposes: raising the quality of the code, finding defects at an early stage, spreading knowledge about the project within the team, and maintaining shared coding standards.\\n\\nThe key aspects reviewers pay attention to in a code review\\n\\nReviewers assess changes against many criteria, which can be divided into several categories:\\n\\n- Correctness and functionality: Does the code solve the task set? Are there any logical errors or bugs? Have all the edge cases been accounted for?\\n\\n- Readability and clarity: Are the names of the variables and functions comprehensible? Is the code\'s structure logical? Are there clear comments where the logic is not obvious?\\n\\n- Architecture and design: Does the change fit the application\'s overall architecture? Does it break the SOLID principles or other patterns adopted in the project? Has any code duplication appeared?\\n\\n- Performance: Are there any suboptimal algorithms (for example, nested loops over large arrays)? Are resources (memory, network requests) used properly?\\n\\n- Security: Are there any vulnerabilities, such as SQL injection, XSS or incorrect handling of user input?\\n\\n- Testing: Are the new features covered by tests? Are the existing tests not broken?\\n\\n- Conformance with conventions: Does the code follow the code style adopted by the team (indentation, naming)?\\n\\nA practical example and the process\\n\\nConsider a typical scenario in Git. A developer creates a branch, makes changes, commits them and creates a Pull Request (PR) or Merge Request (MR). In the PR\'s description they state which task it solves and what was changed. Other developers then study the diff and leave comments right in the interface of GitHub, GitLab or a similar system.\\n\\nAn effective code-review process is built on constructive feedback. Comments should be specific, polite and aimed at improving the code rather than criticising the author. It is good practice to use a previously agreed checklist for reviews.\\n\\nConclusion: Code review is a mandatory practice for teams aiming to build reliable, maintainable software. It is worth applying whenever changes are made to the main branch, since it helps not only to catch errors but also to build a shared understanding of the code base, to train new staff and to raise the team\'s quality standards continuously.","// An example of code that may raise questions in a review\\nfunction processUsers(users) {\\n    // A potential problem: a nested loop, O(n^2) for large data\\n    for (let i = 0; i < users.length; i++) {\\n        for (let j = 0; j < users.length; j++) {\\n            if (users[i].id === users[j].friendId) {\\n                // ... some logic\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    // A question from the reviewer: \\"Could this be optimised with a Map?\\"\\n}\\n\\n// The improved version after the review\\nfunction processUsersOptimized(users) {\\n    const userMap = new Map(users.map(user => [user.id, user]));\\n    for (const user of users) {\\n        const friend = userMap.get(user.friendId);\\n        if (friend) {\\n            // ... the same logic, but in O(n)\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],[1745,"Centralised state is a single global source of data for the whole application, accessible from any component. Modular state is local state broken into independent parts, each managed by a separate component or module. The centralised approach simplifies synchronising data between distant components, but can lead to excessive complexity in small applications. The modular approach makes components more independent and reusable, but complicates exchanging data between them.","State management is a key part of any modern application\'s architecture. It determines how data is stored, updated and passed between components. The two main approaches — centralised and modular — offer different trade-offs between simplicity, predictability and flexibility.\\n\\nCentralised state\\n\\nIn this approach all the application\'s state is held in one place — a single global store. Components do not own the data directly but subscribe to its changes and dispatch actions to update it. That makes the data flow unidirectional and predictable.\\n\\n- Advantages: It is easy to track and debug changes to the state. Synchronising data between many components that are not related hierarchically is simplified. It lets complex business logic be implemented in one place.\\n\\n- Disadvantages: It can create excessive complexity (boilerplate) for simple tasks. The global store can become a \\"black box\\" where all the data is dumped, which makes it harder to understand where it came from.\\n\\n- Where it is used: Large applications with complex business logic, many interrelated components and a need for time-travel debugging. Example tools: Redux, Zustand, Vuex.\\n\\nModular state\\n\\nHere the state is decentralised and encapsulated in individual components or modules. Every component manages its own data, passing it down the hierarchy through props (in React) or similar mechanisms.\\n\\n- Advantages: Components become more independent and reusable. The architecture is easier to understand in small projects. There is less boilerplate.\\n\\n- Disadvantages: Exchanging data between components that are not directly related (for example, siblings) becomes difficult and can lead to prop drilling. It is harder to see the overall picture of the application\'s state.\\n\\n- Where it is used: Small and medium applications, widgets, components with isolated logic. The frameworks\' native approaches: local state in React (useState), Vue components, services in Angular.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider a simple counter. In the modular approach the state lives inside the component:\\n\\nIn the centralised approach the state is moved into a global store (here with Redux Toolkit):\\n\\nConclusion: Centralised state is worth using in large applications where many components have to react to the same data, or where strict predictability of changes matters. Modular state is ideal for small projects, isolated components, or when you want to avoid the overhead of global state management.","// Modular state (a React component)\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // Local state\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Centralised state (a Redux slice)\\nimport { createSlice } from \'@reduxjs/toolkit\';\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: \'counter\',\\n  initialState: { value: 0 },\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment(state) { state.value += 1; },\\n  },\\n});\\n// The component connects to the store\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useSelector(state => state.counter.value);\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch(counterSlice.actions.increment())}>\\n        Increment\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[1746,"When a component uses many useState calls, that can lead to frequent re-renders and reduced performance. There are several ways to optimise it: combine related pieces of state into one object with useReducer, so they can be updated in a single action. Use memoisation via React.memo, useMemo and useCallback to prevent unnecessary computations and re-renders of child components. It is also worth splitting a large component into smaller ones, isolating state that changes independently. That reduces the area being re-rendered.","When a React component contains many `useState` calls, every state update triggers a re-render of the whole component. That can become a performance problem, especially if some pieces of state change often while others change rarely, or if the render involves heavy computation.\\n\\nThe main optimisation strategies\\n\\n- Combining state: Instead of many separate `useState` calls for related data, use `useReducer` or a single `useState` with an object. That lets you update several fields in one action, reducing the number of re-renders.\\n\\n- Memoisation: Apply `React.memo` to the component to prevent a re-render when the props have not changed. Use `useMemo` to cache expensive computations and `useCallback` for stable references to functions passed to child components.\\n\\n- Splitting components: Break a large component into smaller ones, each with its own state. That isolates the re-renders: only the subcomponent whose state changed is updated.\\n\\n- Lazy initialisation: For initial state that requires complex computation, use lazy initialisation by passing a function into `useState`.\\n\\nAn example using useReducer\\n\\nInstead of many `useState` calls, combine the logic in a reducer:\\n\\nAn example of memoisation with React.memo and useCallback\\n\\nIn this example `ExpensiveChild` will not re-render when `count` is updated, because its `text` and `handleTextChange` props stay unchanged (thanks to `useCallback`).\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nSuch optimisations are critical in complex interfaces: dashboards, forms with many fields, interactive tables or charts. They help keep the interface smooth when state is updated frequently.\\n\\nConclusion: Optimising many `useState` calls is worth doing when a component starts to lag because of frequent re-renders. Start by combining state with `useReducer` and splitting components, then add memoisation for fine-grained control over the rendering.","const initialState = { count: 0, text: \'\', flag: false };\\n\\nfunction reducer(state, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case \'increment\':\\n      return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };\\n    case \'updateText\':\\n      return { ...state, text: action.payload };\\n    case \'toggleFlag\':\\n      return { ...state, flag: !state.flag };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);\\n  // Now there is one state update instead of three separate ones\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: \'increment\' })}>\\n        Count: {state.count}\\n      </button>\\n      <input\\n        value={state.text}\\n        onChange={(e) => dispatch({ type: \'updateText\', payload: e.target.value })}\\n      />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst ExpensiveChild = React.memo(({ value, onChange }) => {\\n  console.log(\'Child rendered\');\\n  return <input value={value} onChange={onChange} />;\\n});\\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const [text, setText] = useState(\'\');\\n  \\n  // useCallback keeps the reference to the function stable between renders\\n  const handleTextChange = useCallback((e) => setText(e.target.value), []);\\n  \\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment: {count}</button>\\n      <ExpensiveChild value={text} onChange={handleTextChange} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[1747,"A default export creates one \\"main\\" entity for a module, which can lead to problems with renaming on import and makes automatic refactoring worse. The importing side can give it any name, which reduces the clarity and consistency of the code. It also complicates re-exporting and the analysis of dependencies by tools.","A default export is ES6 module syntax that lets you export one \\"main\\" entity from a module. Although that seems convenient, the approach has several substantial drawbacks affecting the code\'s maintainability and clarity.\\nProblems with renaming and clarity\\nWhen importing a default export, a developer can give it any name. That leads to inconsistency in the code base, where the same component may be called differently in different files, which makes reading and searching harder.\\n\\nDifficulties with refactoring and analysis\\nStatic analysis tools (for example, an IDE\'s rename refactoring) work less well with a default export, because the link between the export and the import is less explicit. Re-exporting a default export through barrel files (index.js) also requires special syntax and is less intuitive.\\n\\nProblems with the dependency tree\\nSome tools, such as Webpack, can apply more aggressive removal of unused code (tree shaking) to named exports, since they are listed explicitly. With a default export, which is often one large object, that can work less effectively.\\nConclusion\\nUsing a default export is justified when a module really does represent one logical entity, for example a class or a React component in its own file, and you want to emphasise that. For libraries, utilities and in large projects, however, named exports are preferable, since they improve clarity, simplify refactoring and promote better tree shaking.","// module.js\\nexport default function MyComponent() {}\\n\\n// fileA.js\\nimport Component from \'./module\'; // The name \'Component\'\\n\\n// fileB.js\\nimport MySuperComponent from \'./module\'; // The name \'MySuperComponent\'\\n\\n// The re-export problem\\nexport { default as MyComponent } from \'./MyComponent\'; // Special syntax\\n// With a named export it would be simpler:\\n// export { MyComponent } from \'./MyComponent\';"],[1748,"Copying an object with JSON.stringify and JSON.parse creates a deep copy but loses all the TypeScript type information. Every property becomes `any` or `unknown` unless stated explicitly. Class methods, symbols, undefined values and circular references are also lost. It is a simple way of deep copying simple data, but a dangerous one for complex objects with methods.","In TypeScript types exist only at compile time and are \\"erased\\" when the code runs. The `JSON.stringify()` and `JSON.parse()` methods work with values at runtime, taking no account of TypeScript\'s type system.\\n\\nWhat happens to the types?\\n\\nAfter deserialisation TypeScript sees the object as an ordinary object literal. If you do not supply an explicit type annotation or a type assertion, the compiler infers a broad type based on the JSON\'s structure, which can lead to typing errors.\\n\\nA practical example and what is lost\\n\\nConsider a class and copying it:\\n\\nHow do you preserve the typing?\\n\\nFor TypeScript to understand the type correctly after copying, you have to state it explicitly:\\n\\n- Use a type assertion: `const copy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(orig)) as User;` (careful — this can be unsafe).\\n\\n- Create a constructor or factory method that takes an object and returns a class instance.\\n\\n- Use serialisation/deserialisation libraries with type support (for example, class-transformer).\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nJSON copying is a quick way to deep-copy simple data objects (POJOs), but it completely ignores TypeScript\'s type system and loses the metadata (methods, prototypes). Use it for data without behaviour, and for complex objects with methods apply other cloning methods or restore the types explicitly after deserialisation.","class User {\\n  constructor(public name: string, public age: number) {}\\n  greet() { return `Hello, ${this.name}`; }\\n}\\n\\nconst original = new User(\'Alice\', 30);\\n// TypeScript knows that original is an instance of User\\n\\nconst jsonCopy: any = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\\n// jsonCopy is now a plain object { name: \'Alice\', age: 30 }\\n// Type: any (we stated it explicitly, though unknown could have been used)\\n// The greet() method is lost!\\n// console.log(jsonCopy.greet()); // Runtime error: greet is not a function\\n\\n// Without an annotation TypeScript infers the type { name: string; age: number; }\\nconst inferredCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\\n// inferredCopy has the type { name: string; age: number; }, not User."],[1749,"In FSD components within a single layer can and should be reused. That is one of the architecture\'s main goals — to avoid duplicating code. For example, a button from shared/ui can be used in several widgets. The reuse should be deliberate, however, and must not break the boundaries between layers. Components should not know about other layers\' implementation details.","Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for front-end applications that organises code by business capability (features) and by technical layer. One of its key principles is managing dependencies and the reuse of code.\\nReuse within a layer\\nWithin a single layer (for example, `shared`, `entities`, `features`) components, functions and other modules are actively reused. That is not only permitted but encouraged, since it reduces duplication and improves consistency. The `shared` layer exists specifically to hold reusable primitives, such as UI components, utilities and helpers.\\nAn example of reuse\\nImagine you have a button component in the `shared/ui` layer. It can be used in dozens of places throughout the application, including inside other components of that same `shared` layer (for example, in a modal window component) or in components of the layers above.\\n\\nImportant limitations\\nDespite the freedom within a layer, it is important to observe the direction of dependencies between layers. Dependencies may go only from the top down (from higher layers, such as `pages` or `features`, to lower ones, such as `entities` and `shared`). A component inside the `features` layer should not import and use a component from another slice of that same `features` layer unless the shared business logic justifies it — that can lead to strong coupling.\\nSummary: Reusing components within a single layer in FSD is a standard, recommended practice for building a consistent, maintainable code base, but it must be done with the rules about slice isolation and the direction of dependencies in mind.","// shared/ui/Button/Button.tsx\\nexport const Button = ({ children, onClick }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={onClick}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// shared/ui/Modal/Modal.tsx\\nimport { Button } from \'../Button/Button\';\\nexport const Modal = ({ onClose }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Modal Content</p>\\n      <Button onClick={onClose}>Close</Button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};"],[1750,"Renaming in destructuring lets you extract an object\'s property into a variable with a different name. The syntax is: `{ oldName: newName }`. It is useful when a property\'s name is inconvenient or conflicts with other variables. For example, extracting `id` into a variable `userId`. It makes the code cleaner and clearer.","Object destructuring in JavaScript is powerful ES6 syntax that lets you extract values from objects and assign them to variables. Sometimes an object property\'s name is unsuitable for use in the current context (for example, it is too generic or it conflicts with an existing variable). Renaming exists for such cases.\\n\\nThe renaming syntax\\n\\nRenaming is done with a colon: on the left you state the property\'s name in the object, on the right the variable name you want. The general form is: `{ originalPropertyName: newVariableName }`.\\n\\nPractical application\\n\\nRenaming is often used in the following scenarios:\\n\\n- Avoiding a name conflict, especially when destructuring several objects in the same scope.\\n\\n- Creating more semantic variable names that fit the function\'s or module\'s context.\\n\\n- Working with APIs that return data with poor or overly short keys.\\n\\nAn example with a name conflict:\\n\\nCombining it with other features\\n\\nRenaming can be combined with setting default values. That lets you rename a property and supply a value if it is absent from the object at the same time.\\n\\nConclusion: Renaming in destructuring is a convenient tool for improving the readability and maintainability of code, letting you adapt property names from external data sources to your code base\'s internal conventions without additional intermediate variables.","const user = { id: 42, name: \'Alice\' };\\n// Extract the \'id\' property into a variable named \'userId\'\\nconst { id: userId, name } = user;\\nconsole.log(userId); // 42\\nconsole.log(name);   // \'Alice\'\\n// No \'id\' variable is created; only \'userId\' is available\\n\\nfunction processData({ id: externalId, data }) {\\n    // Inside the function there is already an \'id\' variable\\n    const id = generateInternalId();\\n    console.log(`External ID: ${externalId}, Internal ID: ${id}`);\\n    // We use \'externalId\' for clarity\\n}\\nprocessData({ id: \'abc123\', data: {} });\\n\\nconst options = { timeout: 5000 };\\n// Rename \'timeout\' to \'requestTimeout\' and supply a default value\\nconst { timeout: requestTimeout = 3000, retries = 3 } = options;\\nconsole.log(requestTimeout); // 5000 (taken from the object)\\nconsole.log(retries);        // 3 (the default value)"],[1751,"Duplicating components in React leads to maintenance difficulties and a larger code size. The main approach is to follow the DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself) principle. You can create reusable components, extracting the shared logic and UI. Use component composition, higher-order components (HOCs) and custom hooks to factor out shared functionality. That keeps the code cleaner and makes changes easier to apply.","Duplicated code, especially in React components, is a frequent problem that complicates maintenance and increases the risk of errors. To avoid it you need to apply the principles of reuse and composition.\\nThe main strategies\\n\\n- Creating reusable components: Extract the common parts of the UI (buttons, input fields, cards) into separate components that can be configured through props.\\n- Component composition: Instead of inheritance, use composition, passing components as children or through special props (for example, render props).\\n- Higher-order components (HOCs): Functions that take a component and return a new one with added logic (for example, authentication or data loading).\\n- Custom hooks: Let you factor shared state and side-effect logic out of components.\\nAn example with a custom hook\\nSuppose several components use a counter. Instead of duplicating the useState logic and the handlers, let\'s create a `useCounter` hook:\\n\\nAn example with a wrapper component\\nIf several components have to share a style or behaviour, create a wrapper component:\\n\\nConclusion: Avoid duplication by factoring shared logic into hooks and shared UI into reusable components. That is especially useful in large projects, where it matters to keep things consistent and to make refactoring simpler.","import { useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction useCounter(initialValue = 0) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(initialValue);\\n  const increment = () => setCount(count + 1);\\n  const decrement = () => setCount(count - 1);\\n  const reset = () => setCount(initialValue);\\n  return { count, increment, decrement, reset };\\n}\\n\\n// Use in a component\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const { count, increment } = useCounter();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Card({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"card\\">\\n      {children}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nfunction UserProfile() {\\n  return (\\n    <Card>\\n      <h2>User Name</h2>\\n      <p>Some description</p>\\n    </Card>\\n  );\\n}"],[1752,"KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid) urges you to make solutions as simple as possible, avoiding unnecessary complexity. DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself) means that every piece of knowledge should have a single representation in the system, which removes duplication. YAGNI (You Ain\'t Gonna Need It) advises you not to add functionality until it is needed, to avoid over-complication. These principles help you create code that is easier to understand, test and change.","The main development principles\\nKISS, DRY and YAGNI are fundamental heuristics aimed at improving the quality of code and of the development process. They are not strict rules but rather guiding ideas that help you make decisions.\\nThe KISS principle (Keep It Simple, Stupid)\\nThe goal is to simplify the system as much as possible. Complex code is harder to debug, maintain and understand. For example, instead of creating a universal but convoluted data handler, it is better to write several simple, specialised functions.\\n\\nThe DRY principle (Don\'t Repeat Yourself)\\nDuplicated code is a source of errors and of difficulties when changes are made. If a piece of logic is repeated in several places, it should be moved into a separate function, module or constant.\\n\\nThe YAGNI principle (You Ain\'t Gonna Need It)\\nDevelopers are often inclined to add features \\"for the future\\" that may never be needed. That leads to greater complexity, longer development and more bugs. YAGNI urges you to add functionality only when there is an immediate need for it.\\nFor example, there is no point implementing methods for complex analytics in a user class if the current requirements are limited to holding a name and an email.\\nPractical application and balance\\nThese principles are often applied together, but they can sometimes conflict. Blindly following DRY can lead to excessively abstract, complex code (breaking KISS). And over-simplification (KISS) sometimes leads to duplication. The key is finding a balance and using common sense. These principles are at their most effective in iterative development processes such as Agile.\\nConclusion: The KISS, DRY and YAGNI principles are worth applying to create maintainable, flexible, comprehensible code, especially in long-term projects. They help you avoid the typical mistakes — over-complication, duplication and developing unnecessary features.","// Complex and redundant\\nfunction processData(data, format, options = {}) {\\n    // Lots of conditions and branches\\n}\\n\\n// Simple and clear (KISS)\\nfunction processJSON(data) { /* ... */ }\\nfunction processCSV(data) { /* ... */ }\\n\\n// Breaking DRY\\nconst area1 = Math.PI * radius1 * radius1;\\nconst area2 = Math.PI * radius2 * radius2;\\n\\n// Following DRY\\nfunction calculateCircleArea(radius) {\\n    return Math.PI * radius * radius;\\n}\\nconst area1 = calculateCircleArea(radius1);\\nconst area2 = calculateCircleArea(radius2);"],[1753,"The preload and prefetch attributes are hints to the browser about how to load resources in advance. Preload tells the browser to load a critically important resource (for example, a font or a script) immediately, because it will be needed on the current page. Prefetch suggests that the browser load a resource for a future navigation (for example, for the next page) in the background, when there is spare network bandwidth. That lets you speed up the display of the current page and make transitions between pages faster.","The `preload` and `prefetch` attributes are mechanisms provided by modern browsers for optimising the loading of resources. They let a developer tell the browser explicitly which files should be loaded in advance, before they are needed, which can significantly improve a site\'s perceived performance.\\n\\nPreload for critical resources\\n\\nThe `preload` attribute is used for resources that are critically important to the current page and will be used shortly. The browser loads such a resource at high priority without blocking the page\'s rendering. That is especially useful for fonts, scripts or styles that are discovered late (for example, through JavaScript).\\n\\nIn this example we preload a font, stating the resource\'s type through the `as` attribute, which helps the browser set the priorities correctly and apply the security policies (for example, CORS).\\n\\nPrefetch for future navigation\\n\\nThe `prefetch` attribute is intended for resources that will be needed on subsequent pages the user may navigate to. The browser loads such resources at low priority, when the network is idle, so as not to interfere with loading the current page.\\n\\nThis approach is often used in single-page applications (SPAs) to preload the code of sections the user may reach.\\n\\nPractical use and the differences\\n\\n- Preload is for the current page: for fonts, critical CSS/JS, images above the fold.\\n\\n- Prefetch is for future pages or actions: for loading the code of the next route in an SPA, or images in a gallery that may be needed.\\n\\n- The key difference is the priority: `preload` is high, `prefetch` is low.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `preload` to speed up the loading of critical resources on the current page, and `prefetch` to improve the speed of navigation between pages, especially in an SPA. These are simple but powerful tools for optimising performance.","<link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"critical-font.woff2\\" as=\\"font\\" type=\\"font/woff2\\" crossorigin>\\n\\n<link rel=\\"prefetch\\" href=\\"next-page-bundle.js\\" as=\\"script\\">"],[1754,"Webpack optimises the reuse of libraries first and foremost by creating a separate vendor chunk. It moves third-party libraries (for example, React, Lodash) out of the application\'s main code into a separate file. That lets the browser cache that file for a long time, since libraries change rarely. When the application\'s code is updated, the user needs to download only the changed main chunk, while the libraries come from the cache, which speeds up repeat visits.","Webpack uses several strategies to optimise the reuse of libraries, the main one being code splitting. The goal is to separate the rarely changing code of third-party dependencies (libraries) from the frequently changing code of the application itself.\\n\\nCreating a vendor chunk\\n\\nThe most widespread approach is to configure the splitting so that every module from the `node_modules` folder ends up in a separate bundle, often called `vendor.js` or `vendors~main.js`. That is done by configuring the entry point or the `SplitChunksPlugin`.\\n\\nHow it works and where it is used\\n\\nDuring the build Webpack analyses the dependency graph. Every module matching the rule (for example, located in `node_modules`) is placed in a separate chunk. In practice that means libraries such as React, Vue or Lodash are downloaded once on the first visit to the site and stored in the browser\'s cache. On subsequent updates to the application\'s functionality (and changes to the main chunk) the browser does not need to download those libraries again — they come from the cache. That is critically important for large applications and increases loading speed for regular users.\\n\\nAdditional optimisations\\n\\n- Hashing the file names: The output files\' names include a hash of the content (for example, `vendors.abc123.js`). If the libraries have not changed, the hash stays the same and the browser uses the cached version.\\n\\n- Minification and compression: The libraries\' code is minified and compressed to reduce the amount transferred.\\n\\n- Tree shaking: For ES6 modules Webpack can exclude unused code from libraries, if they support it, making the vendor chunk smaller still.\\n\\nConclusion: This approach is worth applying in practically any medium or large project using Webpack. It gives an immediate performance win through long-term caching of the libraries, which improves the user experience, especially for returning users.","// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  optimization: {\\n    splitChunks: {\\n      cacheGroups: {\\n        vendor: {\\n          test: /[\\\\\\\\/]node_modules[\\\\\\\\/]/,\\n          name: \'vendors\',\\n          chunks: \'all\',\\n        },\\n      },\\n    },\\n  },\\n};"],[1755,"Importing a single function can pull in the whole library if the module bundler (for example, Webpack) cannot safely remove the unused code. That happens when a module has side effects on import, for example changing global variables or executing code. Some libraries also do not support tree shaking because of the structure of their exports (for example, exporting through a single object). To avoid this you need to use libraries that support ES modules and to configure `sideEffects` in the bundler\'s configuration.","When you import one function from a library, you expect only its code to end up in the final bundle. Sometimes, however, the whole library is pulled in, which increases the application\'s size. That is to do with how module bundlers such as Webpack or Rollup work and with their ability to perform \\"tree shaking\\" — removing dead code.\\nHow tree shaking works\\nTree shaking is the process of statically analysing the imports and exports in ES modules. The bundler determines which exports are actually used and excludes the unused ones. For that, the code has to be written in the ES module format (import/export), since it is statically analysable.\\nWhy tree shaking may not work\\n\\n- Side effects: If a module performs actions on import (for example, registers polyfills or changes a global object), the bundler cannot remove it, since that might break the application. In Webpack\'s configuration you have to state `sideEffects: false` explicitly for libraries without side effects.\\n- CommonJS modules: Libraries exported through `module.exports` are loaded dynamically, which makes analysis harder. Bundlers often cannot remove unused code from such modules.\\n- The structure of the exports: If a library exports every function through a single object (for example, `import _ from \'lodash\'`), then even when one function is used the whole module may be pulled in. Modern versions of libraries often support modular imports (for example, `import debounce from \'lodash/debounce\'`).\\nA code example and the configuration\\nConsider an example with a library that does not support tree shaking because of side effects:\\n\\nWhen only `add` is imported, the bundler may include the whole module, since there is a side effect. To fix that you have to move the side effects into a separate module, or state in the library\'s package.json:\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Use libraries that support ES modules and tree shaking (for example, the ES version of Lodash).\\n- Configure `sideEffects` in Webpack\'s configuration or in package.json.\\n- Check the bundle\'s size with tools such as Webpack Bundle Analyzer.\\nConclusion: Tree shaking is a powerful optimisation method, but it requires the right code structure and bundler configuration. Apply it to reduce the bundle\'s size in production builds, especially in large applications with many dependencies.","// mathUtils.js\\nexport function add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\nexport function multiply(a, b) {\\n  return a * b;\\n}\\n// A side effect: changing a global variable\\nwindow.myLib = { version: \'1.0\' };\\n\\n{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"my-lib\\",\\n  \\"sideEffects\\": false\\n}"],[1756,"The loss of the `this` context happens when an object\'s method is passed as a callback, for example into `setTimeout`. Inside the callback `this` refers to the global object (or `undefined` in strict mode) rather than to the original object. To fix that you can use an arrow function, which has no `this` of its own and inherits it from the surrounding lexical context. You can also bind the context explicitly with the `bind` method, creating a new function with a fixed `this`. Another option is to save a reference to `this` in a variable (for example, `self` or `that`) before passing the method on.","Losing the context is a frequent problem in JavaScript, arising when an object\'s method is passed as a callback function. The `this` keyword inside a function is determined at the moment it is called, not when it is declared. If a method is called other than as an object\'s method (for example, as an ordinary function), `this` loses its link to the original object.\\n\\nWhy is the context lost?\\n\\nConsider the classic example:\\n\\nWhen `user.greet` is passed into `setTimeout`, we pass only a reference to the function. When the timer fires, the function is called as an ordinary one rather than as a method of the `user` object, so `this` becomes `window` (or `undefined` in strict mode).\\n\\nWays to fix it\\n\\n- Arrow functions: Arrow functions have no `this` of their own and capture it from the surrounding lexical context.\\n\\n- The bind() method: Creates a new function with a firmly bound context.\\n\\n- Saving a reference (self/that): An old but working approach.\\n\\n- Calling it in a wrapper: You can wrap the call in an anonymous function that calls the method with the right context.\\n\\nWhere does this apply?\\n\\nThe problem of losing the context arises everywhere methods are passed as callbacks: DOM event handlers (`addEventListener`), asynchronous operations (`Promise.then`, `setTimeout`), array methods (`map`, `forEach`) if they use `this` from an outer object. Understanding and solving this problem is critical for working with classes and object-oriented patterns in JavaScript.\\n\\nConclusion: To fix a lost context, arrow functions (in modern code) or the `bind` method are used most often. That provides predictable behaviour for `this` and is essential for methods that depend on an object\'s state to work correctly.","const user = {\\n  name: \'Alice\',\\n  greet() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}!`);\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// Works correctly:\\nuser.greet(); // Output: Hello, Alice!\\n\\n// The context is lost:\\nsetTimeout(user.greet, 100); // Output: Hello, undefined!\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => user.greet(), 100); // Now it works\\n\\nconst boundGreet = user.greet.bind(user);\\nsetTimeout(boundGreet, 100);\\n\\nconst self = this; // or that\\nsetTimeout(function() {\\n  self.greet();\\n}, 100);\\n\\nsetTimeout(function() {\\n  user.greet();\\n}, 100);"],[1757,"The Shared layer (or common layer) holds code used by several of the application\'s other layers but belonging to none of them directly. It usually contains shared utilities, constants, base types (DTOs, models), repository and service interfaces, and infrastructure-related code that does not depend on the business logic. The main rule is that the Shared layer must not depend on outer layers such as the UI or the infrastructure, to avoid circular dependencies and to ensure it can be reused.","In a layered architecture such as Clean Architecture or Onion Architecture, the Shared layer (sometimes called Core or Domain) plays a key role in organising dependencies. It serves as a central hub holding the elements common to several layers, which lets you avoid duplicated code and breaches of the Dependency Inversion principle.\\n\\nWhat is usually in the Shared layer?\\n\\n- Shared data models (DTOs, entities, value objects): Base classes or interfaces describing the structure of the data passed between layers.\\n\\n- Repository and service interfaces: Abstractions defining the contracts for working with data or external services. Their implementations will be in the infrastructure layer.\\n\\n- Utilities and helpers: Shared functions such as validators, parsers or mathematical computations that do not depend on specific business logic.\\n\\n- Constants and enums: Values common to the whole application, for example error codes or order statuses.\\n\\n- Cross-cutting concerns: Base implementations for logging, exception handling or caching, if they are abstracted away from specific frameworks.\\n\\nAn example structure and code\\n\\nConsider a simple example in TypeScript for an e-commerce application. In the Shared layer we can define a repository interface and a base product model.\\n\\nThe rules and limitations\\n\\nIt is critically important that the Shared layer does not depend on outer layers such as the UI (for example, React components), the infrastructure (databases, HTTP clients) or frameworks. That prevents circular dependencies and makes the code more testable and reusable. For example, the `IProductRepository` interface is declared in Shared, while its implementation `MongoProductRepository` lives in the infrastructure layer, which depends on Shared.\\n\\nConclusion: The Shared layer should be used to hold code that is common and independent of specific implementation details. That improves consistency, reduces duplication and makes an application easier to maintain, especially in large projects with a clear separation of responsibilities.","// shared/interfaces/IProductRepository.ts\\nexport interface IProductRepository {\\n  findById(id: string): Promise<Product | null>;\\n  findAll(): Promise<Product[]>;\\n  save(product: Product): Promise<void>;\\n}\\n\\n// shared/models/Product.ts\\nexport class Product {\\n  constructor(\\n    public id: string,\\n    public name: string,\\n    public price: number,\\n    public category: string\\n  ) {}\\n\\n  // A shared method that can be used in different layers\\n  applyDiscount(percent: number): void {\\n    if (percent > 0 && percent <= 100) {\\n      this.price = this.price * (1 - percent / 100);\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// shared/utils/validation.ts\\nexport function isValidEmail(email: string): boolean {\\n  const regex = /^[^\\\\\\\\s@]+@[^\\\\\\\\s@]+\\\\\\\\.[^\\\\\\\\s@]+$/;\\n  return regex.test(email);\\n}"],[1758,"The Event Loop is the mechanism that lets JavaScript, a single-threaded language, perform asynchronous operations without blocking the main thread. It constantly checks whether the call stack is empty. If the stack is empty, the Event Loop takes the first task from the callback queue (or from the higher-priority microtask queue) and places it on the call stack to run. That lets events, timers and network requests be handled, creating the illusion of multithreading.","The Event Loop is a fundamental part of the JavaScript runtime (for example, a browser or Node.js) that manages the execution of code, the collection of events and the handling of asynchronous callbacks. Since JavaScript has only one thread of execution (the main thread), the Event Loop provides the non-blocking behaviour modern web applications need.\\n\\nThe key components\\n\\nTo understand the Event Loop you need to know several related structures:\\n\\n- The call stack: Where synchronous functions execute. Every function call is pushed onto the stack and removed once it finishes.\\n\\n- Web APIs: Functionality provided by the environment (the browser or Node.js), such as `setTimeout`, `fetch` or DOM events. When such an asynchronous operation is called, it is handed to the Web API and the main thread carries on working.\\n\\n- The callback queue (task queue): The queue where the Web APIs place callbacks from asynchronous operations that are ready to run (for example, a `setTimeout` callback).\\n\\n- The microtask queue: A separate, higher-priority queue holding callbacks from `Promise.then`, `async/await` and `MutationObserver`.\\n\\nHow the event loop works\\n\\nThe Event Loop works as a continuous cycle. Its main rule: first run all the synchronous code until the call stack is empty. It then checks the microtask queue and runs every task in it until it is empty. Only then does it take one task from the macrotask queue (the callback queue) and place it on the call stack. This process repeats indefinitely.\\n\\nA code example and the order of execution\\n\\nThe output will be: `1, 4, 3, 2`. First all the synchronous calls run (`1` and `4`). Then the Event Loop processes the microtask queue (the promise callback, `3`), and only after that the macrotask queue (the timeout, `2`).\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nUnderstanding the Event Loop is critical for writing efficient, non-blocking code. It helps you avoid the interface hanging during long computations, work correctly with asynchronous operations (API requests, reading files, timers) and debug a complex order of execution.\\n\\nConclusion: The Event Loop is the mechanism that makes asynchronous programming in JavaScript possible. Understanding it deeply is essential for optimising performance, preventing errors related to the order of execution and writing responsive web applications.","console.log(\'1\'); // A synchronous call\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(\'2\'); // A macrotask (the callback queue)\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n    console.log(\'3\'); // A microtask (the microtask queue)\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(\'4\'); // A synchronous call"],[1759,"To reuse a UI component with different data sources you need to separate the display logic from the data-fetching logic. The component should receive its data through props, for example as an array of objects. The data-loading logic can be moved into a parent component, into a custom hook, or you can use the Render Props pattern. That lets the same list or table component work with an API, local state or mock data.","Reusing a UI component with different data sources is a fundamental principle of the component approach that improves the code\'s flexibility and testability. The main idea is a separation of responsibilities: the component is responsible only for presentation, while loading and preparing the data (the business logic) is delegated outwards.\\nThe key approaches\\n\\n- Props and composition: The simplest way is to pass the data into the component through props. The component knows nothing about where the data came from.\\n- Render props: A pattern where a component takes a function (a render prop) that returns a React element. That function receives the data as an argument, which lets a container component manage the data logic.\\n- Custom hooks: Ideally suited to encapsulating data-fetching logic (fetching, the loading state, errors). The UI component uses the hook to obtain the data and then renders it.\\n- Provider components (context): Useful when the data is needed by many components at different levels of nesting, while the UI component itself still has to stay independent of any particular context.\\nA practical example\\nConsider a `UserList` component that has to display a list of users from an API, from local state (Redux) or from a static array.\\n\\nIn this example `UserList` is fully reusable. We can easily create another hook, `useUsersFromRedux`, or pass mock data directly, without changing the list component itself.\\nConclusion: This approach is always worth applying when creating components that display data. It makes the code predictable, makes testing easier (you can test the component with fake data) and lets you adapt the interface quickly to changes in the business logic or the data sources.","// 1. A pure presentational component that receives its data through props\\nfunction UserList({ users, isLoading, error }) {\\n  if (isLoading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n  if (error) return <p>Error: {error.message}</p>;\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {users.map(user => (\\n        <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// 2. A custom hook for loading the data from an API\\nfunction useUsersFromAPI() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setIsLoading(true);\\n    fetch(\'/api/users\')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(setUsers)\\n      .catch(setError)\\n      .finally(() => setIsLoading(false));\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return { users, isLoading, error };\\n}\\n\\n// 3. The parent component, which connects the hook and the UI\\nfunction App() {\\n  const apiData = useUsersFromAPI();\\n  const staticData = { users: [{id: 1, name: \'Alice\'}], isLoading: false, error: null };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h2>Users from API:</h2>\\n      <UserList {...apiData} />\\n      <h2>Static Users:</h2>\\n      <UserList {...staticData} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[1760,"Entities in Feature-Sliced Design are extracted when business logic appears that is common to several features or layers. They are objects describing the domain\'s key concepts, for example User, Order, Product. They are extracted to avoid duplicated code and to provide a single source of truth for the business rules. That usually happens at the design stage, when it becomes clear that the same data and operations are used in different places.","Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for front-end applications that offers clear rules for organising code. The entities layer sits at the very bottom of the hierarchy and contains the business entities that are the foundation of the application\'s domain.\\n\\nWhen should you extract entities?\\n\\nEntities are worth extracting in the following cases:\\n\\n- Repeated business logic: When the same data, and the rules for handling it (validation, computation), are used in several features or widgets. For example, a `User` object with methods for checking access rights.\\n\\n- The domain\'s key concepts: When the application has central objects describing the essence of the business (for example, `Order`, `Product`, `Invoice`). Extracting them makes the code more comprehensible and more resilient to change.\\n\\n- Independence from the UI and from external sources: When the logic has to exist separately from the way it is displayed (the UI) or from a particular API. Entities know nothing about React components or HTTP requests.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nConsider an example of a `Product` entity in an online shop application:\\n\\nThis entity encapsulates the product\'s data and the basic business rules. Various features can use it: \\"the product card\\", \\"the basket\\", \\"the admin panel for managing products\\".\\n\\nWhere are entities used?\\n\\nEntities are used in the layers above:\\n\\n- In features, to build specific user-facing logic.\\n\\n- In widgets, to compose interfaces that work with that data.\\n\\n- In the shared layer there may be utilities for working with entities (for example, specific formatters).\\n\\nIt is important that entities contain no code related to the UI, to the application\'s state (such as Redux) or to network requests. They represent pure business logic.\\n\\nConclusion: Entities in FSD are worth extracting at the early stages of design, once the stable, reusable concepts of the domain have been identified. That creates a solid foundation, reduces duplication and makes the business logic easier to test.","// src/entities/product/model/product.ts\\ntype Currency = \'USD\' | \'EUR\';\\n\\nexport class Product {\\n  constructor(\\n    public readonly id: string,\\n    public name: string,\\n    public price: number,\\n    public currency: Currency,\\n    public stockCount: number\\n  ) {}\\n\\n  // A business rule: the product is available to order\\n  isAvailable(): boolean {\\n    return this.stockCount > 0;\\n  }\\n\\n  // A business rule: the formatted price\\n  getFormattedPrice(): string {\\n    return `${this.price} ${this.currency}`;\\n  }\\n\\n  // A business rule: checking whether a discount can be applied\\n  canApplyDiscount(discountPercent: number): boolean {\\n    return discountPercent > 0 && discountPercent <= 50;\\n  }\\n}"],[1761,"Composition in React is an approach to building user interfaces where complex components are created by combining simpler, more specialised ones. Instead of inheritance, React recommends using composition, passing data and other components through props (including the special `children` prop). That makes the code more predictable, reusable and easy to test. For example, a button with an icon can be composed from Button and Icon components rather than created as one monolithic component.","Composition is a fundamental React principle that means assembling a user interface from independent, reusable components, like building blocks. Instead of creating enormous components that do everything, you break the UI into small, manageable parts and then combine them to get the result you want. That is the opposite of inheritance, where you might extend classes; React encourages composition through props.\\n\\nHow composition works\\n\\nThe main mechanisms of composition in React:\\n\\n- Passing data through props: You pass data (strings, numbers, arrays, objects) from a parent component to a child, configuring its behaviour.\\n\\n- Passing React elements and components through props: You can pass whole components or JSX elements as props. The special `children` prop is especially important, as it lets you nest content between a component\'s opening and closing tags.\\n\\n- Using patterns: Patterns such as container and presentational components, or render props, are also based on composition.\\n\\nExamples of composition\\n\\nThe simplest example is using `children`:\\n\\nThe `Card` component knows nothing about its content — it simply renders the `children` passed to it. That makes it maximally reusable.\\n\\nA more complex example is composition by passing components into props explicitly:\\n\\nWhere and why to apply it\\n\\nComposition is used everywhere in React applications to create a flexible, maintainable architecture. It is useful when you need to:\\n\\n- Create reusable wrapper components (for example, modal windows, cards, panels).\\n\\n- Separate responsibilities between components (for example, a container fetches the data while a presentational component displays it).\\n\\n- Implement complex UI patterns, such as higher-order components (HOCs) or custom hooks, which compose logic.\\n\\nConclusion: Composition is the key approach in React for building interfaces. It makes code modular, easy to test and easy to reuse. Always prefer composition to inheritance when developing in React.","function Card({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"card\\">\\n      {children}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Card>\\n      <h2>Title</h2>\\n      <p>Some text inside the card.</p>\\n    </Card>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction PageLayout({ header, sidebar, content }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"layout\\">\\n      <header>{header}</header>\\n      <aside>{sidebar}</aside>\\n      <main>{content}</main>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <PageLayout\\n      header={<Header />}\\n      sidebar={<Sidebar />}\\n      content={<Content />}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}"],[1762,"The spread operator (`...`) creates a shallow copy. That means only the top-level properties are copied. If a property holds an object, an array or a function, only the reference to that object is copied, not the object itself. So changes inside nested structures are visible in both the original and the copy. For deep copying you need other methods, for example `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj))` or libraries such as Lodash.","The spread operator (three dots: `...`) in JavaScript is convenient syntax for quickly creating a copy of an array or an object. It is important to understand, however, that this copy is shallow. That means only the values of primitive types (strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, symbols) and the references to objects are copied. The object a reference points at is not duplicated.\\n\\nHow shallow copying works\\n\\nLet\'s look at an example of an object with nested data:\\n\\nAs you can see, the `name` property changed only in the copy, because it is a string (a primitive). The `address` property, however, is an object, and spread copied only the reference to it. Now `original.address` and `copy.address` point at the same object in memory.\\n\\nWhy is it done this way?\\n\\n- Performance: Deep copying can be resource-intensive for large, complex structures.\\n\\n- Flexibility: Often it is precisely a shallow copy that is wanted, so that data can be shared between different parts of an application.\\n\\n- Semantics: Spread is intended for quickly creating a new object/array from an existing one, not for cloning it completely.\\n\\nHow do you make a deep copy?\\n\\nSeveral approaches can be used for deep copying:\\n\\n- The JSON methods (they work only with JSON-compatible data; they lose functions, undefined values, symbols and circular references):\\n\\n- A recursive function that walks every level of nesting.\\n\\n- Libraries, for example `_.cloneDeep` from Lodash, or `structuredClone` (a modern native method).\\n\\nAn example with structuredClone\\n\\nModern environments support the global `structuredClone` function for deep copying:\\n\\nConclusion: The spread operator is excellent for creating shallow copies, when you need to create a new object or array quickly while changing a few top-level properties. For a complete, independent copy of nested structures you have to use deep-copying methods such as `structuredClone` or `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())`, bearing their limitations in mind.","const original = {\\n  name: \'Alice\',\\n  address: { city: \'London\', zip: 12345 }\\n};\\n\\nconst copy = { ...original };\\n\\n// Changing a primitive property - safe\\ncopy.name = \'Bob\';\\nconsole.log(original.name); // \'Alice\' (unchanged)\\n\\n// Changing the nested object - a problem!\\ncopy.address.city = \'Paris\';\\nconsole.log(original.address.city); // \'Paris\' (changed!)\\n\\nconst deepCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\\n\\nconst originalArray = [{ a: 1 }, { b: 2 }];\\nconst deepCopyArray = structuredClone(originalArray);\\n\\ndeepCopyArray[0].a = 999;\\nconsole.log(originalArray[0].a); // 1 (unchanged)"],[1763,"Dependency Inversion is a SOLID principle stating that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions. On the front end that means components (for example, React components) should not depend directly on concrete implementations of services (API clients, stores) but on their interfaces (abstractions). That makes it easy to substitute implementations, for example for testing (with mocks) or when the back end changes, making the code more flexible and maintainable.","The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) is one of the five SOLID principles, which helps you create an architecture resilient to change. In front-end development its application is especially important for managing the dependencies between user-interface components and the business logic or external services.\\n\\nThe main idea\\n\\nInstead of a component creating an instance of, or importing, a concrete service directly (for example, `axios` for HTTP requests or `localStorage` for working with data), it should receive that service from outside through parameters (props, constructor arguments, context hooks). The dependency is defined through an abstraction — an interface or a class with a particular contract of methods.\\n\\nHow it is applied\\n\\nConsider a typical example: a component that loads a list of users. Without DIP the component depends directly on a particular HTTP-request library.\\n\\nWith DIP applied, we define an abstraction (for example, a `UserService` interface) and inject its implementation from outside.\\n\\nThe practical advantages\\n\\n- Testability: It is easy to substitute a mock implementation for the real service in unit tests.\\n\\n- Flexibility: Replacing the back end (for example, moving from REST to GraphQL) requires changing only one implementation of the service rather than every component.\\n\\n- Loose coupling: Components know only about the contract, not about the implementation\'s details, which simplifies refactoring and reuse.\\n\\nConclusion: Apply dependency injection on the front end for key services (APIs, state, local storage), especially in large applications where testability and the ability to swap implementations matter. It makes the code cleaner, more modular and ready for change.","// BAD: a direct dependency on axios\\nimport axios from \'axios\';\\n\\nfunction UserList() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    axios.get(\'/api/users\').then(response => setUsers(response.data));\\n  }, []);\\n  // ... the rendering\\n}\\n\\n// GOOD: a dependency on an abstraction\\n// 1. Define the interface (in TypeScript) or the contract\\ninterface UserService {\\n  getUsers(): Promise<User[]>;\\n}\\n\\n// 2. The component receives the service through props (or context)\\nfunction UserList({ userService }: { userService: UserService }) {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    userService.getUsers().then(setUsers);\\n  }, [userService]);\\n  // ... the rendering\\n}\\n\\n// 3. The concrete implementation somewhere at the application\'s top level\\nconst axiosUserService: UserService = {\\n  getUsers: () => axios.get(\'/api/users\').then(res => res.data)\\n};\\n\\n// 4. Inject the implementation where the component is used\\n<UserList userService={axiosUserService} />"],[1764,"In React, JSX can be passed into a component in two main ways. The first is through the special `children` prop, by placing the JSX between the component\'s opening and closing tags. The second is through any other prop, by passing the JSX as its value. The `children` prop is the standard and most intuitive way of passing markup, which makes the component a wrapper. Passing it through a named prop gives you more control, for example to render content in different places within the component.","In React, component composition is a fundamental concept, and passing JSX is a key part of it. JSX is a syntax extension for JavaScript that looks like HTML and compiles into `React.createElement()` calls. Passing JSX into a component lets you create abstractions and reuse logic with different content.\\n\\nThe main ways of passing JSX\\n\\nThere are two main approaches to passing JSX into a component.\\n\\n1. Using the `children` prop\\n\\nThis is the most widespread and idiomatic way. When you place JSX between a component\'s opening and closing tags, React automatically passes that JSX in the `props.children` prop.\\n\\nInside `Card`, everything between the `<Card>` and `</Card>` tags becomes the value of `children`. That can be plain text, an element, an array of elements or even another component.\\n\\n2. Passing JSX through named props\\n\\nSometimes you need to pass several independent fragments of JSX, or to state explicitly where they should be inserted. In that case you can use ordinary props whose value is JSX.\\n\\nThis approach gives more clarity and control over the component\'s structure, especially in complex layouts.\\n\\nPractical aspects and use\\n\\n- Wrapper components: `children` is ideal for creating containers, modal windows, cards or context providers, where the inner content is dynamic.\\n\\n- Render props: This is an advanced pattern where a component takes a function (often in a prop named `render`) that returns JSX. That lets the component share its internal state with the function passed in.\\n\\n- Conditional rendering: The JSX passed in can be rendered conditionally inside the receiving component, using the standard JavaScript constructs (`if`, `&&`, the ternary operator).\\n\\nConclusion: Use the `children` prop for simple cases, where the component acts as a wrapper for arbitrary content. Use named props with JSX when you need to separate several areas of content explicitly or want greater structural clarity. Both methods underpin composition and code reuse in React applications.","// A wrapper component\\nfunction Card({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"card\\">\\n      {children}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Card>\\n      <h2>Title</h2>\\n      <p>This is the content of the card, passed as children.</p>\\n    </Card>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// A component with several slots for content\\nfunction Layout({ header, sidebar, mainContent }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"layout\\">\\n      <header>{header}</header>\\n      <aside>{sidebar}</aside>\\n      <main>{mainContent}</main>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Layout\\n      header={<h1>My site</h1>}\\n      sidebar={<nav>Menu</nav>}\\n      mainContent={<article>The main article...</article>}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}"],[1765,"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) can run into difficulties when modelling complex processes that cut across several features or layers. The main limitations include the difficulty of managing dependencies between isolated slices, the potential for duplicated logic, and more complicated routing for end-to-end processes. The methodology suits clearly separated functional blocks best, rather than tightly interwoven workflows. For complex processes an additional global state-management layer, or an adaptation of FSD\'s rules, may be required.","Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for front-end applications that offers a clear separation of code by business feature and by technical layer. When building complex processes, however, such as multi-stage payment, user onboarding or a workflow with feedback between modules, its limitations can show.\\n\\nFSD\'s key limitations for complex processes\\n\\n- Rigid slice boundaries: Complex processes often require close interaction between several features (for example, \\"the basket\\", \\"payment\\", \\"notifications\\"). In FSD, accessing one slice from another directly is forbidden, which can lead to redundant proxy layers or to a more complicated data flow.\\n\\n- Managing the process\'s state: State belonging to the process (for example, the current step of placing an order) can end up smeared across several slices. Placing it in a global store (Redux) partly breaks FSD\'s principle of isolation.\\n\\n- Routing and navigation: Complex processes often have their own multi-step routing (/checkout/step1, /checkout/step2), which has to be coordinated between the \\"features\\" and \\"pages\\" layers. That can create a pile-up in the pages layer.\\n\\n- The potential for duplicated logic: If different features in a process require similar validations or data transformations, there may be a temptation to duplicate the code so as not to break the slice boundaries.\\n\\nA code example: the problem with the process\'s state\\n\\nConsider the order-placement process, which touches the `cart`, `shipping` and `payment` features. In ideal FSD every feature manages its own state. But the state of the process itself (for example, `currentStep`) does not belong explicitly to any of them.\\n\\nPractical approaches to easing the limitations\\n\\n- Extracting \\"processes\\" as a separate entity: You can create a \\"processes\\" layer or use shared hooks for coordination, while observing FSD\'s rules about the direction of dependencies (higher layers depend on lower ones).\\n\\n- Using a global event bus or state manager: To connect slices you can use carefully designed global events without creating direct imports.\\n\\n- Adapting FSD\'s rules: In large projects the strictness of the rules is sometimes relaxed for cross-cutting concerns, with explicit exceptions introduced for complex business processes.\\n\\nConclusion: Feature-Sliced Design is excellent for structuring applications with clear, independent functional blocks. For complex, multi-step processes requiring intensive interaction between features, however, an additional architectural layer or a compromise on the methodology\'s strictness may be required, to avoid excessive complexity in the code.","// A problematic placement of the process\'s state inside one feature\\n// features/checkout/model/store.ts\\nexport const $checkoutProcess = createStore({\\n  currentStep: \'cart\',\\n  isCompleted: false,\\n  // Does data from other features have to be held here?\\n  shippingData: null,\\n  paymentData: null,\\n});\\n\\n// The shipping feature now has to interact with this store,\\n// which creates a dependency on the checkout slice."],[1766,"Front-end development uses various architectural approaches, such as MVC, MVVM, Flux (and its implementation Redux), as well as modular and component-oriented architectures. Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is a methodology for structuring a project that groups code by business capability (features) rather than by technical layer. Its essence is dividing an application into isolated, loosely coupled modules (layers), such as shared, entities, features, widgets, pages and app. That improves code reuse, simplifies navigating the code base and reduces the risk of unintended dependencies between unrelated parts of the application.","In modern front-end development the choice of architecture is critically important for supporting the growth of the application and the team. Besides the classic patterns such as MVC, component-oriented and state-management approaches such as Flux/Redux have gained popularity. As projects grow more complex, however, the problem of \\"spaghetti code\\" arises, where the dependencies between modules become tangled. Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) offers a solution through a clear separation of responsibilities based on the business logic.\\n\\nThe main principles of FSD\\n\\nFSD organises code as vertical slices (features), each of which represents a complete business capability. The architecture is built on several key layers:\\n\\n- Shared: reusable utilities, UI kits and helpers not tied to the business logic.\\n\\n- Entities: the business entities (for example, User, Product), their models and basic operations.\\n\\n- Features: specific user actions and interactive logic (for example, authentication, adding a product to the basket).\\n\\n- Widgets: composite blocks that assemble several features and entities for display.\\n\\n- Pages: the application\'s entry points, composing widgets for particular routes.\\n\\n- App: the global configuration, providers, styles and initialisation.\\n\\nHow and where FSD is used\\n\\nFSD is especially useful in large projects with long-term maintenance and big teams, where several developers work on different features simultaneously. It is used in the React/Next.js, Vue and Angular ecosystems and in other frameworks. The approach provides a predictable folder structure, which simplifies onboarding new developers and refactoring.\\n\\nA practical example of the structure\\n\\nConsider a simplified structure for an e-commerce project organised along FSD lines:\\n\\nIn this example the `add-to-cart` feature is isolated and contains all the logic and UI needed to add a product to the basket. It can use the `product` entity and the shared components from `shared/ui`, but does not depend directly on other features, which minimises coupling.\\n\\nConclusion: Feature-Sliced Design is worth applying in medium and large front-end projects where scalability, a clear separation of responsibilities and ease of maintenance matter. It helps you avoid chaos in the code base, making the dependencies explicit and manageable.","src/\\n  app/                 # The application\'s initialisation, routing\\n    providers.tsx\\n    router.tsx\\n  pages/              # The pages\\n    catalog/\\n      index.tsx\\n    cart/\\n      index.tsx\\n  widgets/            # The widgets (composite blocks)\\n    product-list/\\n      ui/ProductCard.tsx\\n    header/\\n      ui/Header.tsx\\n  features/           # The features (business actions)\\n    add-to-cart/\\n      api/addToCart.ts\\n      ui/AddToCartButton.tsx\\n    product-search/\\n      lib/searchLogic.ts\\n      ui/SearchBar.tsx\\n  entities/           # The entities\\n    product/\\n      model/types.ts\\n      api/productApi.ts\\n    user/\\n      model/userSlice.ts\\n  shared/             # The shared modules\\n    ui/\\n      Button.tsx\\n      Input.tsx\\n    lib/\\n      axiosInstance.ts\\n      constants.ts"],[1767,"Decomposing components means splitting a large component into several small ones, each responsible for one specific task. That makes the code cleaner, clearer and easier to test. The main principles are: the single-responsibility principle (one component, one responsibility), moving reusable logic into custom hooks, and separating presentational and container components. Always start by extracting repeated parts of the interface and complex logic.","Decomposing components is a fundamental skill in React development that turns monolithic, hard-to-understand components into a tree of small, manageable and often reusable parts. The main goal is to improve readability, simplify testing and make working together as a team easier.\\n\\nThe key principles of decomposition\\n\\n- The single-responsibility principle (SRP): Every component should solve one specific task. If a component starts doing too much (for example, loading data and rendering a complex list and handling a form), it is worth splitting up.\\n\\n- Extracting reusable parts of the UI: Repeated interface elements (buttons, cards, input fields with shared logic) should be moved into separate components.\\n\\n- Separating logic and presentation: Use the \\"containers and presentational components\\" pattern. Containers manage state and logic, while presentational components only render the data received through props.\\n\\n- Using custom hooks: Complex business logic, or logic for working with side effects, can be encapsulated in custom hooks, which keeps components cleaner.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider a monolithic user-profile component that loads data and displays it.\\n\\nNow let\'s decompose it:\\n\\nThe result is several independent modules: a hook for the data, a pure display component, an indicator component and the main component that coordinates them. Each part can now be tested and changed separately.\\n\\nConclusion: Decomposition is always worth applying when a component becomes too large or performs several unrelated tasks. It is especially useful in large applications for maintaining architectural cleanliness and simplifying refactoring.","// Bad: one large component\\nfunction UserProfile() {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetchUser().then(data => {\\n      setUser(data);\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    });\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{user.name}</h1>\\n      <img src={user.avatar} alt={user.name} />\\n      <p>Email: {user.email}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => alert(\'Edited!\')}>Edit Profile</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// 1. A custom hook for the data-loading logic\\nfunction useUser(userId) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetchUser(userId).then(data => {\\n      setUser(data);\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    });\\n  }, [userId]);\\n\\n  return { user, loading };\\n}\\n\\n// 2. A presentational component for displaying the profile\\nfunction UserProfileView({ user, onEdit }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{user.name}</h1>\\n      <img src={user.avatar} alt={user.name} />\\n      <p>Email: {user.email}</p>\\n      <button onClick={onEdit}>Edit Profile</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// 3. A reusable loading component\\nfunction Loader() {\\n  return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n}\\n\\n// 4. The main container component, which ties it all together\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const { user, loading } = useUser(userId);\\n  const handleEdit = () => alert(\'Edited!\');\\n\\n  if (loading) return <Loader />;\\n  return <UserProfileView user={user} onEdit={handleEdit} />;\\n}"],[1768,"A component\'s extensibility means it can easily be modified or supplemented with new logic without breaking the existing code. The main approaches are: using composition (for example, children, render props, slots), passing configuration through props, and applying patterns such as higher-order components or hooks. It is important to design components in line with the open/closed principle: they should be open for extension but closed for modification. That reduces coupling and simplifies maintenance.","A component\'s extensibility is its ability to have new behaviour added, or existing behaviour changed, without changing its source code. This is a key principle for supporting scalability and code reuse in large applications.\\nThe main approaches to achieving it\\n\\n- Composition through children or slots: Lets you nest arbitrary content or other components inside a component, delegating part of its responsibility to external code.\\n- Configuration through props: Passing parameters, callbacks, styles and other data through props makes a component flexible. For example, you can add a new event handler simply by passing it as a prop.\\n- Render props and scoped slots: Patterns where a component receives a function for rendering part of its UI, which gives full control over the content displayed from outside.\\n- Higher-order components (HOCs): Functions that take a component and return a new one enriched with additional logic. They let you add functionality (for example, a data subscription) without changing the original component.\\n- Custom hooks (in React): Let you extract and reuse state and side effects, making components cleaner and more extensible through the composition of logic.\\nA practical example\\nConsider a button component that has to be made extensible to support different icons, themes and sizes.\\n\\nIn this example the `Button` component is extensible thanks to: accepting `children` for any text, the `variant` and `size` parameters for the styles, the `icon` prop for displaying an icon, and the use of `...restProps` to pass on any additional attributes (for example, `disabled`, `aria-*`). To add a new theme or type you do not need to change the component itself — it is enough to pass new props.\\nWhere it is used\\nThe principles of extensibility are critically important when developing design systems, UI component libraries (for example, Material-UI, Ant Design) and in any long-term project where the requirements change often. They also help in team work, letting different developers modify parts of the system independently.\\nConclusion: Create extensible components when you anticipate that the functionality will grow or change over time, especially in public libraries or large applications with many teams. That reduces technical debt and makes adding new features easier.","// An extensible Button component in React\\nconst Button = ({ \\n  children, \\n  onClick, \\n  variant = \'primary\', \\n  size = \'medium\', \\n  icon: Icon, // The icon component can be passed as a prop\\n  ...restProps // All the other props (for example, className, disabled) are passed on\\n}) => {\\n  const baseClasses = \'btn\';\\n  const variantClass = `btn-${variant}`; // primary, secondary, etc.\\n  const sizeClass = `btn-${size}`;       // small, medium, large\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button\\n      className={`${baseClasses} ${variantClass} ${sizeClass}`}\\n      onClick={onClick}\\n      {...restProps}\\n    >\\n      {Icon && <Icon />}\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Use\\nconst MyComponent = () => (\\n  <div>\\n    <Button onClick={() => alert(\'Clicked!\')}>\\n      An ordinary button\\n    </Button>\\n    <Button variant=\\"secondary\\" size=\\"large\\" icon={SendIcon} disabled>\\n      Send\\n    </Button>\\n  </div>\\n);"],[1769,"Feature-Sliced Design divides an application into several horizontal layers, each with a strictly defined area of responsibility. The main layers are: \'app\' (the application\'s initialisation and global settings), \'pages\' (composing entities and features to form pages), \'features\' (interactive logic tied to the domain), \'entities\' (the basic business entities, such as User or Product) and \'shared\' (reusable technical components and utilities). This separation lets you manage dependencies (a layer may depend only on the layers below it) and simplifies navigating the code base.","Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for front-end applications that organises code into horizontal layers and vertical slices. The layers define the level of abstraction and the responsibility of the code, while the slices group code by business domain. The main goal is to create a predictable, scalable, maintainable project structure where the dependencies are strictly regulated: code may depend only on layers at the same or a lower level of abstraction.\\n\\nFSD\'s main layers (from the top down)\\n\\n- app — the very top layer, responsible for initialising the application, the global settings, the providers (for example, routing, styles, the store) and the general start-up logic. It is the application\'s entry point.\\n\\n- pages — the layer that composes entities and features to form the application\'s particular pages. Every page corresponds to a route and usually contains no complex business logic, merely assembling components from the layers below.\\n\\n- features — the layer for interactive logic tied to the domain (for example, authentication, comments, the basket). This is where the user scenarios (features) live, which may include components, hooks and stores specific to that feature.\\n\\n- entities — the layer of basic business entities, such as User, Product, Order. These entities are independent of particular features and represent the domain\'s fundamental concepts. They usually contain display components, types and data models.\\n\\n- shared — the very bottom layer, containing reusable technical components, utilities, helpers, the UI kit (buttons, inputs) and configurations. This layer is not tied to the business logic and is used by every layer above.\\n\\nAn example project structure\\n\\nHere is what a file structure reflecting FSD\'s layers might look like:\\n\\nHow and where FSD is used\\n\\nFSD is especially useful in large front-end projects with long-term maintenance and team development. It helps you:\\n\\n- Separate responsibilities between parts of the application clearly.\\n\\n- Manage dependencies (for example, a feature cannot import code from pages, but can from entities and shared).\\n\\n- Simplify navigating the code thanks to a single structure.\\n\\n- Increase the reusability of components and logic.\\n\\nThe methodology is often used with a React/TypeScript stack, but can be adapted to other frameworks.\\n\\nConclusion: Feature-Sliced Design is worth applying in medium and large front-end applications where scalability, a clear organisation of the code and the management of dependencies between modules matter. For small projects or prototypes, adopting FSD may be excessive.","src/\\n  app/                    # The application\'s initialisation, the providers\\n    providers/\\n    styles/\\n    App.tsx\\n  pages/                  # The application\'s pages\\n    home/\\n      ui/\\n        HomePage.tsx\\n    about/\\n  features/               # The features (user scenarios)\\n    auth-by-phone/\\n      ui/\\n      model/\\n      lib/\\n    toggle-theme/\\n  entities/               # The business entities\\n    user/\\n      ui/\\n        UserCard.tsx\\n      model/\\n        types.ts\\n    product/\\n  shared/                 # The reusable code\\n    ui/\\n      Button.tsx\\n      Input.tsx\\n    lib/\\n      api/\\n      helpers/"],[1770,"Decomposition is the process of dividing a large, complex system into smaller, manageable parts or modules. Composition is the reverse process of assembling a system from those ready-made, independent modules. Decomposition helps you cope with complexity, making a system easier to understand and develop. Composition lets you reuse modules and combine them flexibly to create new features. Together they form a design cycle: first we break the task up (decomposition), then we assemble the solution (composition).","Composition and decomposition are complementary design principles that underpin the creation of quality software. They help you manage complexity, making systems comprehensible, testable and easy to change.\\n\\nDecomposition\\n\\nDecomposition is the \\"divide and conquer\\" strategy. Its goal is to break a monolithic, complex task or system into smaller, independent subtasks or modules. Every module should solve one clearly defined problem (the single-responsibility principle). That simplifies:\\n\\n- Comprehension: It is easier to grasp how a small module works than the whole system at once.\\n\\n- Development: Different teams can work on different modules in parallel.\\n\\n- Testing: Modules can be tested in isolation (unit testing).\\n\\n- Maintenance: Changes in one module affect the others minimally.\\n\\nFor example, when developing an online shop you can identify the modules: \\"user management\\", \\"the product catalogue\\", \\"the shopping basket\\", \\"placing an order\\".\\n\\nComposition\\n\\nComposition is the process of assembling the final system from those ready-made, well-defined modules. It focuses on how the modules interact with each other to achieve the overall goal. The key ideas of composition:\\n\\n- Reuse: A well-designed module (for example, for authentication) can be used in different projects.\\n\\n- Flexibility: By changing the way the modules are connected, you can create new features without changing their internal logic.\\n\\n- Loose coupling: The modules communicate through clear interfaces rather than depending directly on each other\'s internal implementation.\\n\\nIn object-oriented programming, composition is often implemented by including one object in another (a has-a relationship) rather than through inheritance (is-a).\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nConsider a simplified example in Python demonstrating both principles. We decompose the task of sending a notification into independent components and then compose them.\\n\\nIn this example `EmailSender` and `Logger` are the results of decomposition (separate responsibilities). `NotificationService` is the result of composition: it assembles those components into a working service without knowing their implementation details.\\n\\nConclusion: Decomposition is applied at the analysis and design stage to overcome complexity. Composition is applied at the implementation stage to build a system from ready-made blocks. Use decomposition when a task seems too big to start on. Use composition when you have a set of proven, reliable modules and need to assemble a new solution from them quickly.","# Decomposition: we create small, specialised classes (modules).\\n\\nclass EmailSender:\\n    def send(self, to, message):\\n        # The email-sending logic\\n        print(f\\"Email to {to}: {message}\\")\\n\\nclass Logger:\\n    def log(self, event):\\n        # The logging logic\\n        print(f\\"LOG: {event}\\")\\n\\n# Composition: we assemble a service that uses those modules.\\nclass NotificationService:\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        # We include (compose) the dependencies needed\\n        self.email_sender = EmailSender()\\n        self.logger = Logger()\\n\\n    def notify_user(self, user_email, text):\\n        self.logger.log(f\\"Starting notification for {user_email}\\")\\n        self.email_sender.send(user_email, text)\\n        self.logger.log(f\\"Notification sent to {user_email}\\")\\n\\n# Use\\nservice = NotificationService()\\nservice.notify_user(\\"user@example.com\\", \\"Your order is ready!\\")"],[1771,"DRY and SRP are not in direct conflict, but their goals can sometimes create tension in design. DRY demands that duplicated code be avoided, which can lead to the creation of shared components or services. SRP demands that a class or module have one reason to change. The problem arises when shared code created to satisfy DRY starts performing too many unrelated tasks, breaching SRP. The key is finding a balance: extract only the logic that really is the same and has a single responsibility.","DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself) and the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) are two fundamental approaches to writing clean, maintainable code. DRY aims to minimise the duplication of knowledge in a system, which usually means eliminating repeated code. SRP, part of SOLID, states that a class or module should have one and only one reason to change — that is, a single responsibility.\\nWhere does the tension arise?\\nThe conflict is not direct, but it can show up when a developer, following DRY, starts aggressively extracting every repeated fragment of code into shared utilities or base classes. That can lead to the creation of a \\"god object\\" or a service that knows and does too many different things, thereby breaching SRP. Such a component becomes fragile, since changes in one part of the system may require it to be modified, affecting many other, seemingly unrelated places.\\nAn example of the practical contradiction\\nImagine you have two classes: `InvoiceSender` (which sends invoices) and `ReportGenerator` (which generates reports). Both use the same complex date-formatting algorithm.\\n\\nFollowing DRY, we move the `formatDate` method into a separate class.\\n\\nNow `DateFormatter` risks breaching SRP if it starts handling everything to do with time (formatting, parsing, conversion, validation). Its reason to change becomes blurred.\\nHow do you find the balance?\\nThe solution lies in analysing the nature of the duplication carefully:\\n\\n- Incidental duplication: If the code looks similar but represents different concepts (for example, formatting a postcode and a phone number), extracting it into a shared function is a mistake. It will breach SRP.\\n- Essential duplication: If it is the same business logic or rule (for example, calculating tax), then creating a separate module with a single responsibility (for example, `TaxCalculator`) satisfies both DRY and SRP.\\nIn the date example, if the formatting is needed only for display in the UI, it would be more correct to create a separate `DisplayDateFormatter` module with the single responsibility of preparing dates to show to the user. If parsing dates from user input is needed later, that is a different responsibility and deserves its own module.\\nConclusion: The conflict between DRY and SRP is a myth when they are understood properly. DRY fights the duplication of knowledge (the same rule or algorithm), while SRP fights the duplication of reasons to change. Competent design consists in extracting precisely the unified areas of knowledge into separate modules, which will then have a single responsibility. If combining code makes a module\'s responsibility unclear, you are probably combining different pieces of knowledge, and that breaches SRP even if the letter of DRY is observed.","// Breaking DRY - duplicated code\\nclass InvoiceSender {\\n    formatDate(date) {\\n        // Complex formatting logic\\n        return `${date.getDate()}.${date.getMonth()+1}.${date.getFullYear()}`;\\n    }\\n    send() { /* ... uses formatDate ... */ }\\n}\\n\\nclass ReportGenerator {\\n    formatDate(date) {\\n        // An exact copy of the logic from InvoiceSender\\n        return `${date.getDate()}.${date.getMonth()+1}.${date.getFullYear()}`;\\n    }\\n    generate() { /* ... uses formatDate ... */ }\\n}\\n\\n// An attempt to satisfy DRY\\nclass DateFormatter {\\n    static format(date) {\\n        return `${date.getDate()}.${date.getMonth()+1}.${date.getFullYear()}`;\\n    }\\n    // Later we add logic for parsing a date from a string, for another part of the application\\n    static parse(dateString) { /* ... */ }\\n    // Then we add time-zone conversion...\\n}"],[1772,"The open/closed principle states that components should be open for extension but closed for modification. In a UI context that means a base component should not change when new functionality is added. Instead, the new logic is added through composition, inheritance (rarely) or by passing parameters. For example, a button can be extended by passing new props or wrapping it in a HOC rather than changing its source code. That makes the code more predictable and simplifies testing.","The open/closed principle (OCP) is the second SOLID principle, which states that software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be open for extension but closed for modification. In the world of components, especially in React, Vue or Angular, that means a base component should be designed so that its behaviour can be extended without changing its source code.\\n\\nHow does it work in components?\\n\\nThe main idea is to anticipate the points of change and make them configurable. Instead of adding conditions (if/else) or new props for every new case, a component should provide abstractions (for example, slots, render props, HOCs) for customisation.\\n\\n- Composition: The most frequent approach. A base component is created that takes children or functions for rendering parts of the UI. For example, a `Modal` component may take `header`, `body` and `footer` as children.\\n\\n- Props and configuration: The component is configured through props. It is important that adding new functionality does not require changing the existing checks inside the component, but is simply passed in as a new parameter.\\n\\n- Render props / children as a function: Let you determine dynamically what to render inside a component and how, delegating the rendering logic to the calling code.\\n\\n- Higher-order components (HOCs): Wrappers that add new behaviour (logic, props) to an existing component without changing its source.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nConsider a button component that initially supports only a primary colour. If an icon, sizes and loading states have to be added later, the bad approach is to add a pile of props and conditional logic to one component. The good approach is to make the component extensible through composition.\\n\\nIn this example `Button` stays simple and stable. To add an icon we do not reach inside `Button`, but create a new `IconButton` component that composes the original `Button` and adds the markup needed. That is OCP in action.\\n\\nWhere is it used?\\n\\nThe principle is critically important in design systems and component libraries, where the base components (buttons, inputs, cards) have to be resilient to change while allowing customisation for different projects. OCP also helps when developing plugin architectures, where third-party developers can extend the core\'s functionality without rewriting it.\\n\\nConclusion: Apply the open/closed principle in components when you are creating a library or a set of base UI elements that will be used in different parts of an application or by different teams. That reduces the risk of breaking existing code when new functionality is added and makes the system more flexible.","// The base Button component (closed for modification)\\nconst Button = ({ children, onClick, variant = \'primary\', ...props }) => {\\n  const baseStyle = \'px-4 py-2 rounded font-medium\';\\n  const variants = {\\n    primary: \'bg-blue-600 text-white\',\\n    secondary: \'bg-gray-300 text-black\',\\n  };\\n  return (\\n    <button\\n      className={`${baseStyle} ${variants[variant]}`}\\n      onClick={onClick}\\n      {...props}\\n    >\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Extension through composition (open for extension)\\nconst IconButton = ({ icon, label, ...buttonProps }) => (\\n  <Button {...buttonProps}>\\n    <span className=\\"flex items-center gap-2\\">\\n      {icon}\\n      {label}\\n    </span>\\n  </Button>\\n);\\n\\n// Use\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <div>\\n    <Button onClick={() => alert(\'Clicked!\')}>Basic Button</Button>\\n    <IconButton\\n      icon={<StarIcon />}\\n      label=\\"With Icon\\"\\n      variant=\\"secondary\\"\\n      onClick={() => console.log(\'Icon button clicked\')}\\n    />\\n  </div>\\n);"],[1773,"The single-responsibility principle (SRP) in React means that every component should solve one specific task. For example, a component for displaying a list of users should not also manage the state of a form for adding them. That makes components simpler to understand, test and reuse. Observing SRP helps you avoid \\"bloated\\" components that are hard to maintain.","The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) is one of the key SOLID principles, which states that a module (in React\'s context, a component, a hook or a function) should have one and only one reason to change. In React that means designing components responsible for one clear part of the user interface or of the logic.\\n\\nHow to apply SRP in components\\n\\nInstead of creating one large component that displays data and handles events and manages state, you should break it into several small ones:\\n\\n- Presentational (dumb) components: Responsible only for rendering the UI from the props received. For example, `UserList`, which simply maps an array of users into a list of elements.\\n\\n- Container (smart) components: Manage state and business logic and pass the data into the presentational components. For example, `UserListContainer`, which loads the data from an API and passes it into `UserList`.\\n\\n- Custom hooks: Move repeated logic (for example, subscribing to data, managing a form) into separate hooks. That keeps components cleaner.\\n\\nA code example: breaking and observing SRP\\n\\nConsider a component that breaks SRP by combining loading the data, displaying it and handling clicks:\\n\\nNow let\'s separate the responsibilities, using a custom hook for the data logic and extracting a presentational component:\\n\\nWhere and why to apply it\\n\\nApplying SRP matters especially in medium and large applications. It simplifies:\\n\\n- Testing: Small components with a clear task are easy to cover with unit tests.\\n\\n- Reuse: Presentational components such as `UserList` can be used in different parts of an application with different data.\\n\\n- Maintenance: Changing the data-loading logic does not touch the rendering code, and vice versa.\\n\\n- Collaboration: Different developers can work on different responsibilities (logic vs UI) with minimal conflicts.\\n\\nConclusion: Apply the single-responsibility principle in React to create modular, flexible, easily maintainable components. Separate components into ones that manage logic (containers, hooks) and ones responsible for display. That is especially useful in long-term projects and in teams, where the readability and testability of the code matter.","// BAD: a component with many responsibilities\\nfunction BadUserComponent() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    fetch(\'/api/users\')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setUsers(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      });\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const handleDelete = (id) => {\\n    // The deletion logic...\\n  };\\n\\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>User List</h1>\\n      <ul>\\n        {users.map(user => (\\n          <li key={user.id}>\\n            {user.name}\\n            <button onClick={() => handleDelete(user.id)}>Delete</button>\\n          </li>\\n        ))}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// A hook for managing the users\' data (one responsibility)\\nfunction useUsers() {\\n  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    fetch(\'/api/users\')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setUsers(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      });\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const deleteUser = (id) => {\\n    // The deletion logic...\\n  };\\n\\n  return { users, loading, deleteUser };\\n}\\n\\n// A presentational component for the list (one responsibility - rendering)\\nfunction UserList({ users, onDelete }) {\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {users.map(user => (\\n        <li key={user.id}>\\n          {user.name}\\n          <button onClick={() => onDelete(user.id)}>Delete</button>\\n        </li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The main container component, which ties the logic and the presentation together\\nfunction GoodUserComponent() {\\n  const { users, loading, deleteUser } = useUsers();\\n\\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>User List</h1>\\n      <UserList users={users} onDelete={deleteUser} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[1774,"Correct recursion requires two main conditions. First, there must be a base case — a condition under which the function finishes without calling itself, preventing infinite recursion. Second, the recursive call must bring the function\'s arguments closer to the base case, so that it is eventually reached. Without these conditions the function may fall into infinite recursion, which leads to the call stack overflowing and an error.","Recursion is a programming technique where a function calls itself to solve a task, breaking it into smaller subtasks of the same kind. For recursion to work correctly and safely, two fundamental conditions have to be observed.\\n1. The base case\\nThis is the condition under which the function finishes without making any new recursive calls. The base case serves as the stopping point, preventing an infinite chain of calls. Without it the recursion will continue until it exhausts the call stack, which leads to a stack overflow error.\\n2. The recursive case\\nThis is the part of the function where it calls itself. The key requirement: every recursive call must bring the state of the task closer to the base case. That usually means the function\'s arguments change (for example, get smaller) with every step.\\nExample: computing a factorial\\nConsider the classic example of computing the factorial of a number n (n!).\\n\\nIn this example the base case is `n <= 1`. The recursive call `factorial(n - 1)` guarantees that the value of `n` decreases with every step, eventually reaching 1.\\nWhere is recursion used?\\nRecursion is widely used in algorithms and data structures:\\n\\n- Traversing trees and graphs (for example, the DOM tree in a browser).\\n- Divide-and-conquer algorithms (quicksort, merge sort).\\n- Solving combinatorics and dynamic programming problems.\\n- Processing nested data structures (JSON, XML).\\nConclusion: Recursion is worth using when a task naturally breaks into identical subtasks and you can define the stopping condition clearly. It often makes code cleaner and more readable than iterative solutions for certain classes of problem (for example, traversing trees). It is important to remember the potential memory cost of the call stack, however.","function factorial(n) {\\n  // The base case: the factorial of 0 or 1 is 1\\n  if (n <= 1) {\\n    return 1;\\n  }\\n  // The recursive case: n! = n * (n-1)!\\n  // The argument (n-1) moves towards the base case (1)\\n  return n * factorial(n - 1);\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(factorial(5)); // Output: 120"],[1775,"The main hooks for optimisation in React are useMemo, useCallback and React.memo (although memo is a HOC rather than a hook). useMemo caches the result of a computation so it is not recomputed on every render while the dependencies have not changed. useCallback caches the function itself, which is useful when passing it to child components so they do not re-render because a new function was created. React.memo wraps a component and prevents it re-rendering while its props have not changed.","React provides several tools for optimising rendering, among which the `useMemo` and `useCallback` hooks, and the `React.memo` higher-order component, play the key role. Their main purpose is to prevent expensive computations and unnecessary re-renders of child components, which is critical for maintaining high performance in complex interfaces.\\nuseMemo\\nThe `useMemo` hook caches (memoises) the result of a computation. It takes a function that returns a value and an array of dependencies. The value is recomputed only when one of the dependencies changes. That is ideal for heavy computations, for example filtering or sorting large lists.\\n\\nuseCallback\\nThe `useCallback` hook caches the function itself rather than its result. It returns a memoised version of the function that changes only when the dependencies change. That matters especially when the function is passed as a prop into a child component optimised with `React.memo`, to avoid unnecessary re-renders of that child because a new function is created on every render of the parent.\\n\\nReact.memo\\n`React.memo` is a higher-order component (HOC) rather than a hook, but it is closely tied to optimisation. It wraps a functional component and prevents it re-rendering if its props have stayed the same (they are compared shallowly). To control the comparison you can pass a comparison function as the second argument.\\n\\nThese tools should be applied deliberately. Premature optimisation can complicate the code. Start optimising only when real performance problems appear that can be measured with the React DevTools Profiler.\\nConclusion: Use `useMemo` to cache expensive computations, `useCallback` to stabilise the references to functions passed downwards, and `React.memo` to prevent re-renders of pure components. These are the main tools for optimising rendering in React applications.","import { useMemo } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction ExpensiveComponent({ list, filter }) {\\n  const filteredList = useMemo(() => {\\n    console.log(\'Recalculating filtered list...\');\\n    return list.filter(item => item.includes(filter));\\n  }, [list, filter]); // Recomputed only if list or filter changes\\n\\n  return <p>{filteredList.join(\', \')}</p>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useCallback } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    console.log(\'Button clicked, count:\', count);\\n    // Some logic\\n  }, [count]); // The function is updated only when count changes\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n      <ChildComponent onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst ChildComponent = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log(\'Child rendered\');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click me</button>;\\n});\\n\\nimport React from \'react\';\\n\\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(function MyComponent({ text }) {\\n  console.log(\'MyComponent rendered with text:\', text);\\n  return <div>{text}</div>;\\n});\\n\\n// The component re-renders only if the `text` prop changes"],[1776,"SCSS Modules is an approach that combines the capabilities of the SCSS preprocessor with the concept of CSS Modules. CSS Modules is a system that automatically generates unique class names for selectors, isolating styles within a particular component. SCSS adds mixins, nesting, variables and other conveniences on top. The result is that a developer writes SCSS code in separate files (for example, Button.module.scss), and the bundler (for example, Webpack) converts it into CSS with unique class names, guaranteeing that one component\'s styles do not affect another.","SCSS Modules is a technology combining the power of the SCSS preprocessor with CSS Modules\' style-isolation mechanism. It solves CSS\'s classic problem of a global namespace, where styles can unexpectedly conflict in large applications.\\nHow it works\\nYou create a file with the .module.scss extension (for example, `Button.module.scss`). Inside you write ordinary SCSS code, using all its capabilities: variables, nesting, mixins. During the build (usually with Webpack and the `sass-loader` together with `css-loader` in modules mode) the system:\\n\\n- Compiles the SCSS into CSS.\\n- Automatically converts the class names (for example, `.button`) into unique hashed identifiers (for example, `.Button_button__a5bKc`).\\n- Creates a mapping object (a JavaScript object) that maps the original class names to the generated ones.\\nPractical use and an example\\nThis approach is especially popular in React applications. You import the styles object as a module and use the generated class names through dot notation.\\n\\nIn the compiled HTML the button gets a unique class, for example `class=\\"Button_button__a5bKc Button_button--large__d7fGt\\"`. That guarantees that this particular `Button` component\'s styles will not clash with anything.\\nThe key advantages\\n\\n- Complete isolation: The styles are confined to the component, which removes side effects.\\n- Use of SCSS: All the preprocessor\'s advantages (variables, mixins, functions) remain available.\\n- Ease of refactoring: You can safely rename classes inside a module without fear of breaking other parts of the application.\\n- Explicit dependencies: If a component is removed, its styles automatically stop being included.\\nSummary: SCSS Modules is worth applying in component-oriented projects (especially in React, Vue), where modularity and maintainability matter and style conflicts have to be avoided. It is the golden mean between purely global styles and CSS-in-JS solutions, offering predictability together with the preprocessor\'s power.","// Button.module.scss\\n$primary-color: #3498db;\\n\\n.button {\\n  background-color: $primary-color;\\n  padding: 10px 20px;\\n  border: none;\\n  border-radius: 4px;\\n  color: white;\\n  font-size: 16px;\\n\\n  &:hover {\\n    background-color: darken($primary-color, 10%);\\n  }\\n\\n  &--large {\\n    padding: 15px 30px;\\n    font-size: 18px;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Button.jsx (a React component)\\nimport React from \'react\';\\nimport styles from \'./Button.module.scss\'; // Importing the styles object\\n\\nconst Button = ({ children, size }) => {\\n  // Choose the class dynamically\\n  const buttonClass = `${styles.button} ${size === \'large\' ? styles[\'button--large\'] : \'\'}`;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button className={buttonClass}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nexport default Button;"],[1777,"The reduce method applies a function to every element of an array, accumulating the result into a single value. It takes two main arguments: the reducer function and the accumulator\'s initial value. The function receives the current accumulator and the array element and returns the new value of the accumulator. That makes it easy to sum numbers, join strings or group objects.","The `reduce` method is a powerful functional-programming tool in JavaScript, intended for turning an array into a single value. It processes every element in turn, applying a given function to it and to the current intermediate result (the accumulator). That makes reduce universal for aggregation tasks such as summing, finding a maximum or transforming data structures.\\n\\nHow reduce works\\n\\nThe method\'s syntax: `array.reduce(callback(accumulator, currentValue, index, array), initialValue)`. The first argument is a callback function invoked for every element. The second (optional) argument is the accumulator\'s initial value. If it is not supplied, the accumulator becomes the array\'s first element and the processing starts from the second.\\n\\nUsage examples\\n\\nLet\'s look at a few practical examples:\\n\\nWhere reduce is used\\n\\n- Aggregating data: computing sums, averages, concatenating strings.\\n\\n- Transforming structures: turning an array into an object or another collection.\\n\\n- Complex computations: for example, function composition or implementing state management.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `reduce` when you need to obtain a single value from an array through sequential processing of its elements. It is especially useful in a functional style and for complex aggregations, but for simple sums or concatenations simpler methods such as `forEach` or loops are sometimes enough.","// 1. The sum of the numbers in an array\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];\\nconst sum = numbers.reduce((acc, num) => acc + num, 0);\\nconsole.log(sum); // 10\\n\\n// 2. Finding the maximum value\\nconst max = numbers.reduce((acc, num) => Math.max(acc, num));\\nconsole.log(max); // 4\\n\\n// 3. Grouping objects by a property\\nconst items = [\\n  { category: \'fruit\', name: \'apple\' },\\n  { category: \'vegetable\', name: \'carrot\' },\\n  { category: \'fruit\', name: \'banana\' }\\n];\\nconst grouped = items.reduce((acc, item) => {\\n  if (!acc[item.category]) acc[item.category] = [];\\n  acc[item.category].push(item.name);\\n  return acc;\\n}, {});\\nconsole.log(grouped); // { fruit: [\'apple\', \'banana\'], vegetable: [\'carrot\'] }"],[1778,"The map, filter and reduce methods serve different purposes when working with arrays. Map creates a new array by transforming every element of the original. Filter returns a new array containing only the elements that satisfy a condition. Reduce boils the whole array down to a single value, accumulating the result. None of them changes the original array, and they are pure functions, which makes the code predictable.","In JavaScript the `map`, `filter` and `reduce` methods are fundamental functional-programming tools for working with arrays. They let you process data declaratively, without using `for` or `while` loops, which makes the code more readable and expressive.\\n\\nThe map method\\n\\nThe `map` method applies the function passed in to every element of an array and returns a new array of the same length holding the results of calling that function. It is used when you need to transform data, for example to extract particular properties of objects or to apply mathematical operations.\\n\\nThe filter method\\n\\nThe `filter` method creates a new array including only those elements for which the predicate function passed in returns `true`. It is ideally suited to selecting data by a condition.\\n\\nThe reduce method\\n\\nThe `reduce` method boils an array down to a single value by applying an accumulator function in sequence. It takes two arguments: the reducer function and the accumulator\'s initial value. The function receives the current accumulator and the array element and returns the new value of the accumulator.\\n\\nThese methods are often combined into chains to solve complex data-processing tasks. For example, you can filter an array, then transform the elements and finally compute an overall value.\\n\\nUse `map` to transform data, `filter` to filter it and `reduce` to aggregate or fold it into a single value. These methods make code cleaner and easier to test, since they are immutable and follow the principles of functional programming.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst doubled = numbers.map(num => num * 2);\\nconsole.log(doubled); // [2, 4, 6]\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\\nconst evens = numbers.filter(num => num % 2 === 0);\\nconsole.log(evens); // [2, 4]\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];\\nconst sum = numbers.reduce((acc, num) => acc + num, 0);\\nconsole.log(sum); // 10\\n\\nconst transactions = [\\n  { amount: 100, currency: \'USD\' },\\n  { amount: 200, currency: \'EUR\' },\\n  { amount: 50, currency: \'USD\' }\\n];\\nconst totalUSD = transactions\\n  .filter(t => t.currency === \'USD\')\\n  .map(t => t.amount)\\n  .reduce((sum, amount) => sum + amount, 0);\\nconsole.log(totalUSD); // 150"],[1779,"Tokens are safer stored in HTTP-only cookies than in localStorage or sessionStorage. localStorage and sessionStorage are accessible through JavaScript, which makes them vulnerable to XSS attacks. HTTP-only cookies are inaccessible to scripts, which reduces the risk of a token leaking. Cookies do require protection against CSRF, however, through additional measures such as SameSite attributes and CSRF tokens.","The choice of where to store tokens (for example, a JWT) on the client directly affects an application\'s security. The main options are `localStorage`, `sessionStorage` and HTTP-only cookies, each with its own risks and advantages.\\nWhy localStorage and sessionStorage are insecure\\nBoth stores are accessible through JavaScript on the same page. That means if an attacker manages to inject a malicious script (an XSS attack), they can read the token and send it to their own server. `localStorage` keeps data permanently and `sessionStorage` only for the session, but both are equally vulnerable to XSS.\\nThe advantages of HTTP-only cookies\\nCookies with the `HttpOnly` flag are set by the server through the `Set-Cookie` header and are sent automatically by the browser with every request to the domain. The key advantage: JavaScript cannot access such a cookie\'s contents, which protects the token from being stolen through XSS.\\nThe drawbacks of cookies and protection against CSRF\\nThe main risk of using cookies is CSRF (cross-site request forgery) attacks. An attacker can make the user\'s browser send a request with the cookies to your site. To protect against that, use:\\n\\n- The `SameSite=Strict` or `Lax` attribute on the cookies, to limit their being sent in cross-site requests.\\n- CSRF tokens in forms or request headers.\\n- Validation of the requests\' origin (the Origin/Referer headers).\\nAn example of setting the cookie on the server (Node.js/Express)\\n\\nOn the client you do not need to manage that cookie manually. For API requests requiring authorisation, the cookie will be sent automatically.\\nConclusion\\nUse HTTP-only cookies to store tokens where possible, especially for traditional web applications with server-side rendering. For an SPA, where explicit control over the token is required, consider a combined approach: keep the refresh token in an HTTP-only cookie and the short-lived access token in JavaScript\'s memory (but not in localStorage). Always include the `secure` and `sameSite` flags and implement protection against CSRF.","res.cookie(\'access_token\', token, {\\n  httpOnly: true,\\n  secure: true, // Over HTTPS only\\n  sameSite: \'strict\',\\n  maxAge: 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000 // 1 day\\n});"],[1780,"Transpilation is the process of converting code from one version of JavaScript into another, usually from a newer version into an older one. It is needed so that developers can write in modern ES6+ (for example, using arrow functions, classes, async/await) while browsers that do not support those features can still run the code. Tools such as Babel automatically turn the new syntax into equivalent older syntax, providing cross-browser compatibility. That lets teams use the language\'s leading-edge capabilities without losing users on old browsers.","Transpilation is the process of converting source code written in one version of a programming language into code in another version of the same language, preserving its logic. In JavaScript\'s context that most often means converting code that uses the modern capabilities of the ECMAScript 2015 (ES6) standard and later into code compatible with older versions of JavaScript (for example, ES5) that every browser and runtime understands.\\n\\nWhy is this needed?\\n\\nJavaScript as a language is constantly evolving. New syntactic constructs appear (for example, arrow functions, classes, destructuring, the rest/spread operators) and new APIs (for example, Promise, async/await). The speed at which users update their browsers varies, however, and many carry on using old versions (especially in corporate environments or on mobile devices). If a developer writes code using `const` and arrow functions and a user opens it in Internet Explorer 11, the code simply will not run, which leads to errors.\\n\\nHow does transpilation work?\\n\\nThe most popular tool for transpiling JavaScript is Babel. It analyses your modern code (the source), builds its abstract syntax tree (AST) and then transforms that tree, replacing the new constructs with their equivalents in the older syntax. For example:\\n\\nBabel performs that conversion automatically, often as part of the project\'s bundler (for example, Webpack or Vite). The developer configures the list of target browsers (through `.browserslistrc`), and Babel converts only those features that are not supported in the browsers specified.\\n\\nWhere is it used?\\n\\n- Web development: To provide cross-browser compatibility for client-side code.\\n\\n- Server-side development on Node.js: Although Node.js often supports the modern ES6+ capabilities, transpilation can be used to take advantage of the very newest proposals for the language (TC39 stages) or to support old versions of Node.\\n\\n- Developing libraries and frameworks: So that a library written in modern JavaScript can be used in any project, its source code is transpiled and distributed as a compatible bundle.\\n\\n- Using language extensions: Babel can transpile code written in TypeScript, JSX (for React) or experimental syntax proposals into vanilla JavaScript.\\n\\nConclusion: Transpilation is a necessary stage in modern front-end development that lets you use new JavaScript capabilities safely without waiting for every user to update their browser. It is worth applying in practically any project that has to work in different browsers and environments.","// The source code (ES6+)\\nconst greet = (name) => `Hello, ${name}!`;\\n\\nclass User {\\n  constructor(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// After Babel transpiles it to ES5 you may get something like:\\n\\"use strict\\";\\n\\nvar greet = function greet(name) {\\n  return \\"Hello, \\" + name + \\"!\\";\\n};\\n\\nvar User = function User(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n};"],[1781,"A callback is a function passed into another function to be invoked after an asynchronous operation completes, which can lead to \\"callback hell\\". A Promise is an object representing an operation\'s future result, allowing chains of `.then()` and `.catch()` calls for better control of the flow and of errors. Async/await is syntactic sugar over Promises, letting you write asynchronous code in a synchronous style using the `async` and `await` keywords, which makes the code more readable and easier to debug.","Asynchronous programming in JavaScript evolved from simple callbacks through Promises to the async/await syntax. Each approach solves the problem of performing operations that take time (for example, network requests or reading files) without blocking the main thread of execution.\\n\\nCallbacks\\n\\nThis is the basic concept: a function is passed as an argument into another function and is invoked once an asynchronous operation finishes. The main drawback is \\"callback hell\\", where nested callbacks make the code unreadable and hard to maintain. Error handling also becomes complicated, since every operation requires its own check.\\n\\nPromises\\n\\nA Promise is an object representing the result (successful or failed) of an asynchronous operation. It has three states: pending, fulfilled and rejected. A Promise lets you use chains of `.then()` to handle successful results and `.catch()` for errors, which improves readability and control of the flow.\\n\\nAsync/await\\n\\nAsync/await is syntactic sugar introduced in ES2017 that lets you write asynchronous code in a style resembling synchronous code. The `async` keyword defines an asynchronous function, which always returns a Promise. Inside such a function you can use `await` to suspend execution until a Promise settles, which makes the code linear and easy to debug.\\n\\nAsync/await is especially useful for sequential asynchronous operations, where complex chains of `.then()` are to be avoided. It also simplifies error handling with try/catch, familiar from synchronous code.\\n\\nComparison and use\\n\\n- Callbacks: Used in older code, in Node.js APIs (for example, fs.readFile) and in events. Suitable for simple one-off operations, but avoid deep nesting.\\n\\n- Promises: The foundation for modern asynchronous operations, such as the fetch API. Ideal for chains of asynchronous tasks and for parallel execution with Promise.all().\\n\\n- Async/await: Recommended for new code, especially with complex logic involving conditions and loops. It makes code cleaner and reduces the likelihood of errors.\\n\\nConclusion: Use async/await for most new projects, since it improves readability and simplifies debugging. Promises remain the foundation for asynchronous operations and are useful for parallel execution. Callbacks are worth using only when working with legacy libraries or for simple event handlers.","// An example with a callback\\nfunction fetchData(callback) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    const data = { id: 1, name: \'John\' };\\n    callback(null, data); // The first argument is the error, the second the data\\n  }, 1000);\\n}\\n\\nfetchData((err, data) => {\\n  if (err) {\\n    console.error(\'Error:\', err);\\n  } else {\\n    console.log(\'Data:\', data);\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// An example with a Promise\\nfunction fetchDataPromise() {\\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      const success = true;\\n      if (success) {\\n        resolve({ id: 1, name: \'John\' });\\n      } else {\\n        reject(new Error(\'Failed to fetch data\'));\\n      }\\n    }, 1000);\\n  });\\n}\\n\\nfetchDataPromise()\\n  .then(data => console.log(\'Data:\', data))\\n  .catch(err => console.error(\'Error:\', err));\\n\\n// An example with async/await\\nasync function fetchDataAsync() {\\n  try {\\n    const data = await fetchDataPromise(); // Wait for the Promise to settle\\n    console.log(\'Data:\', data);\\n    return data;\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.error(\'Error:\', err);\\n    throw err;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfetchDataAsync();"],[1782,"In C#, value types are passed into methods by value, that is, a copy of the value is created and changes inside the method do not affect the original variable. Reference types are passed by value of the reference, that is, the reference to the object is copied, so changes to the object itself inside the method persist. To change the reference itself, or the original variable\'s value, the `ref` or `out` keywords are used.","In C# how a parameter is passed into a method depends on whether the type is a value type or a reference type. This fundamental difference affects the data\'s behaviour when a method is called.\\nPassing value types\\nValue types (for example, int, double, struct) hold their data directly in the memory allocated for the variable. When such a parameter is passed into a method, a copy of the value is created by default. Any changes made inside the method do not touch the original variable.\\n\\nPassing reference types\\nReference types (for example, class, array, string) hold a reference to an object on the heap. When such a parameter is passed, by default the reference itself is copied (by value of the reference). That means the method gains access to the same object in memory, so changes to the object\'s properties or elements will be visible outside. If the reference is reassigned to a new object inside the method, however, that does not affect the original reference.\\n\\nUsing ref and out\\nTo change the variable itself (for value types) or the reference (for reference types), you can use the `ref` or `out` modifiers. They let a parameter be passed by reference, so the method works directly with the original variable.\\n\\nThis approach is used when a method has to be able to change the original variables, for example in functions that swap values or that initialise with `out`.\\nConclusion: Understanding how types are passed matters for writing correct code, especially when working with mutable data. Use passing by value by default for safety, and ref/out when variables explicitly have to be changed.","void ModifyValue(int number) {\\n    number = 10; // The copy is changed\\n}\\n\\nint original = 5;\\nModifyValue(original);\\nConsole.WriteLine(original); // Output: 5 (unchanged)\\n\\nvoid ModifyArray(int[] arr) {\\n    arr[0] = 99; // Changes the object the copied reference points at\\n}\\n\\nint[] myArray = {1, 2, 3};\\nModifyArray(myArray);\\nConsole.WriteLine(myArray[0]); // Output: 99 (changed)\\n\\nvoid Swap(ref int a, ref int b) {\\n    int temp = a;\\n    a = b;\\n    b = temp;\\n}\\n\\nint x = 1, y = 2;\\nSwap(ref x, ref y);\\nConsole.WriteLine($\\"x={x}, y={y}\\"); // Output: x=2, y=1"],[1783,"Access to a dictionary in Python is O(1) on average, but not always. That is guaranteed only under ideal hashing conditions. When collisions occur (when different keys have the same hash), access can degrade to O(n) in the worst case. Modern Python implementations minimise such situations, but the theoretical possibility remains. It also matters that the key has to be a hashable object.","A dictionary (dict) in Python is implemented as a hash table, which provides an average time complexity of O(1) for inserting, retrieving and deleting elements. That \\"average case\\", however, depends on several factors.\\n\\nHow a hash table works\\n\\nWhen a key-value pair is added, Python computes the key\'s hash using its `__hash__()` method. That hash determines the bucket the entry will be placed in. When a value is looked up by key, the hash is computed again, the right bucket is found and the value is retrieved.\\n\\nWhen the complexity becomes O(n)\\n\\nThe worst case of O(n) arises with:\\n\\n- Hash collisions: If many keys produce the same hash, they all land in the same bucket. Searching such an overfull bucket becomes linear.\\n\\n- An attack on the hash function: An attacker can deliberately choose keys that cause collisions, to slow the application down (a hash DoS attack).\\n\\nPython 3.3+ includes a hash randomisation mechanism (PYTHONHASHSEED), which makes such attacks harder but does not remove the theoretical possibility of collisions.\\n\\nAn example of collisions\\n\\nPractical aspects\\n\\nIn reality, for the standard types (strings, numbers, tuples) the hash functions are well distributed and collisions are rare. When using custom classes with a poor hash function, however, you can run into degraded performance.\\n\\nConclusion: Although a Python dictionary is optimised for O(1) access, a developer should understand that the guarantee is for the average case. When designing systems where performance is critical, it is worth avoiding keys with potential collisions and remembering the theoretical worst case of O(n).","# Two different objects can have the same hash\\nclass BadKey:\\n    def __hash__(self):\\n        return 1  # Every instance returns the same hash\\n\\n    def __eq__(self, other):\\n        return self is other  # But these are different objects\\n\\nd = {}\\nfor i in range(1000):\\n    d[BadKey()] = i  # Every key lands in the same bucket\\n# A lookup by any key is now O(n) in this table"],[1784,"FIFO stands for First In, First Out. It is the principle whereby the element added to a collection first is the first to be taken out of it. It underlies the queue data structure. FIFO is used wherever the order of arrival matters: for example, in a document print queue, in handling requests to a server or in a message buffer. It guarantees fair, predictable processing.","The FIFO (First In, First Out) principle describes an order of processing in which the element that entered the system earliest is served first. It is a fundamental concept in computer science and systems design, analogous to an ordinary queue of people in a shop.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nFIFO is implemented with the queue data structure. A queue has two main operations:\\n\\n- Enqueue — adding an element at the end.\\n\\n- Dequeue — removing an element from the front.\\n\\nThe order of removal therefore corresponds strictly to the order of addition.\\n\\nWhere FIFO is used\\n\\nThis principle is used in a wide range of areas:\\n\\n- Task queues: operating system schedulers process processes in the order they arrive (in the simplest case).\\n\\n- Buffering data: network buffers (for example, in routers) often use FIFO to hold packets temporarily.\\n\\n- Messaging systems: message brokers (RabbitMQ, Kafka) can use FIFO queues to guarantee the order of delivery.\\n\\n- Resource management: database connection pools may hand out connections on a FIFO basis.\\n\\nA code example (a queue in Python)\\n\\nConclusion: The FIFO principle is worth applying when the order of arrival is critically important and fair service without priorities is required. It is ideal for modelling queues, buffers and any system where the original sequence has to be preserved.","from collections import deque\\n\\n# Create the queue\\nqueue = deque()\\n\\n# Add elements (enqueue)\\nqueue.append(\\"Task A\\")\\nqueue.append(\\"Task B\\")\\nqueue.append(\\"Task C\\")\\nprint(\\"The queue after the additions:\\", list(queue))\\n# Output: The queue after the additions: [\'Task A\', \'Task B\', \'Task C\']\\n\\n# Remove elements (dequeue) - Task A leaves first\\nfirst_out = queue.popleft()\\nprint(\\"The first element removed:\\", first_out)\\nprint(\\"The queue after the removal:\\", list(queue))\\n# Output: The first element removed: Task A\\n#        The queue after the removal: [\'Task B\', \'Task C\']"],[1785,"A value type holds its data directly in the variable, while a reference type holds a reference to data in memory. On assignment a value type creates a copy of the value, whereas on assignment of a reference type only the reference is copied and both variables start pointing at the same data. Value types are usually allocated on the stack, which makes operations on them fast, while reference types are allocated on the heap, which lets you work with large, dynamic data structures.","In programming languages such as C#, Java or TypeScript, all data types divide into two main categories: value types and reference types. That division determines how data is stored in memory and how it is passed between variables and methods.\\n\\nStorage in memory\\n\\nValue types hold their data directly in the region of memory allocated for the variable. That is usually the stack — a fast, ordered region of memory managed automatically. Examples are the primitive types: int, bool, float, and also structs (struct in C#).\\n\\nReference types hold in the variable not the data itself but the address (the reference) of a region of memory on the heap where that data lives. The heap is a dynamic region of memory managed by the garbage collector. Examples are classes, arrays and strings (in some languages).\\n\\nBehaviour on assignment and when passed\\n\\nThe key difference shows up when copying:\\n\\n- When a value-type variable is assigned to another variable, the value itself is copied. The two variables then become completely independent.\\n\\n- When a reference-type variable is assigned, only the reference (the address) is copied. Both variables start pointing at the same object on the heap. Changes through one variable will be visible through the other.\\n\\nAn example in C#\\n\\nWhere they are used\\n\\nValue types are ideal for small, frequently used data, where performance and copy semantics matter (for example, a point\'s coordinates, sums of money). Reference types are used for large objects that may change dynamically and have to be passed by reference for efficiency (for example, lists of users, an application\'s configuration).\\n\\nConclusion: Use value types for simple, indivisible data where independent copying is needed. Use reference types for complex objects where shared state and efficient memory management are necessary.","// A value-type example (a struct)\\nint a = 10;\\nint b = a; // The value 10 is copied\\nb = 20;\\nConsole.WriteLine(a); // Output: 10 (a has not changed)\\n\\n// A reference-type example (a class)\\nclass Person { public string Name; }\\nPerson p1 = new Person { Name = \\"Alice\\" };\\nPerson p2 = p1; // The reference is copied; p2 points at the same object\\np2.Name = \\"Bob\\";\\nConsole.WriteLine(p1.Name); // Output: Bob (the object was changed through p2)"],[1786,"The complexity of searching for an element in an array depends on the conditions. If the array is not sorted, the search takes linear time, O(n), since every element has to be checked. If the array is sorted, you can use a binary search with a complexity of O(log n), which is significantly faster for large arrays. Understanding this difference helps you write efficient code.","Searching for an element in an array is one of the basic operations in programming, and its efficiency directly affects an application\'s performance. Time complexity describes how an algorithm\'s execution time grows as the size of the input grows (in this case, as the number of elements in the array grows).\\nLinear search (O(n))\\nIf the array is not sorted, the only reliable way to find an element is to check each of them in turn, starting from the first. That is called a linear search. In the worst case, when the element sought is absent or is at the end, the algorithm makes n comparisons, where n is the size of the array. A complexity of O(n) means a linear relationship: if the array grows tenfold, the search time in the worst case also grows roughly tenfold.\\n\\nBinary search (O(log n))\\nIf the array is sorted, you can use a far more efficient algorithm — a binary search. It works on the divide-and-conquer principle: it compares the element sought with the array\'s middle element and, depending on the result, discards half of the remaining elements. The search space is therefore halved at every step.\\n\\nA complexity of O(log n) is logarithmic. That means an array of 1,000,000 elements requires no more than 20 comparisons (log₂(1,000,000) ≈ 20), which is incomparably faster than a linear search.\\nWhere they are used\\n\\n- A linear search is used when the array is small, is unsorted, or sorting is impossible/not worthwhile.\\n- A binary search is used in sorted data structures (arrays, trees) for fast lookups, and is built into many standard libraries (for example, `Array.prototype.indexOf` for the linear case, `Array.prototype.binarySearch` in some languages).\\nConclusion: For unsorted data use a linear search (O(n)). If the data can be sorted once and then searched many times, sorting in advance and then using a binary search (O(log n)) gives a huge performance win on large volumes.","function linearSearch(arr, target) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n    if (arr[i] === target) {\\n      return i; // Return the index of the element found\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return -1; // The element was not found\\n}\\n\\nfunction binarySearch(sortedArr, target) {\\n  let left = 0;\\n  let right = sortedArr.length - 1;\\n  while (left <= right) {\\n    const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);\\n    if (sortedArr[mid] === target) {\\n      return mid;\\n    }\\n    if (sortedArr[mid] < target) {\\n      left = mid + 1; // Search in the right half\\n    } else {\\n      right = mid - 1; // Search in the left half\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return -1;\\n}"],[1787,"An immutable string means that once it has been created its contents cannot be changed. Any operation that apparently changes the string (for example, concatenation or replacing a character) in fact creates a new string in memory. This property provides data safety, simplifies multithreading and lets memory be optimised through string interning. In languages such as JavaScript and Python, strings are immutable by default.","The concept of immutability for strings means that once a string object has been created its contents cannot be changed. Any operation that seemingly modifies a string in fact returns a completely new string object, leaving the original untouched. This is a fundamental property that affects performance, security and a program\'s logic.\\n\\nHow it works in practice\\n\\nConsider an example in Python. When you try to \\"change\\" a string, for example by appending text to it, a new string is created.\\n\\nAn attempt to change a character by index raises an error, because strings in Python do not support assignment by index.\\n\\nWhere and why string immutability is used\\n\\n- Safety and predictability: Since a string cannot be changed once created, it can be passed safely between functions or threads without fear of accidental modification.\\n\\n- Memory optimisation (string interning): Many languages (for example, Java, Python) hold only one copy of identical string literals in a special pool, which saves memory.\\n\\n- Caching hashes: Immutability lets a string\'s hash code be computed once at creation and then reused, which speeds up hash tables (dictionaries, sets).\\n\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nIn JavaScript strings are likewise immutable. Methods such as `toUpperCase()` or `replace()` return new strings.\\n\\nConclusion: String immutability is a key principle that provides reliability and efficiency. It is worth bearing in mind when working with large volumes of text data, to avoid unexpected memory consumption from creating many intermediate strings, and worth taking advantage of for safe shared access to data.","original = \\"Hello\\"\\nnew_string = original + \\" World\\"\\n# original stays \\"Hello\\"\\n# new_string is a new object, \\"Hello World\\"\\nprint(original)  # Output: Hello\\nprint(new_string) # Output: Hello World\\n\\nmy_str = \\"abc\\"\\n# my_str[0] = \'z\'  # This raises TypeError: \'str\' object does not support item assignment\\n\\nlet str = \\"hello\\";\\nlet upperStr = str.toUpperCase();\\nconsole.log(str);      // \\"hello\\" - the original has not changed\\nconsole.log(upperStr); // \\"HELLO\\" - this is a new string\\n\\n// Even \\"changing\\" it by index does not work\\nstr[0] = \'H\';          // Raises no error, but does not change the string either\\nconsole.log(str);      // Still \\"hello\\""],[1788,"A queue is a data structure that works on the FIFO (First-In, First-Out) principle — first in, first out. Elements are added (enqueued) at the end of the queue and removed (dequeued) from its front. That makes it ideal for modelling processes where the order of arrival matters, for example a print job queue or messages in data-exchange systems. The main operations are adding, removing and inspecting the first element (peek).","A queue is an abstract data type representing an ordered collection of elements, where additions happen at one end (the tail) and removals at the other (the head). That principle is known as FIFO (First-In, First-Out) and is fundamental to many computational processes.\\n\\nThe main operations and the principle\\n\\nA queue supports several key operations:\\n\\n- Enqueue: adding a new element at the end of the queue.\\n\\n- Dequeue: retrieving and removing the element at the front of the queue.\\n\\n- Peek / front: obtaining the element at the front of the queue without removing it.\\n\\n- isEmpty: checking whether the queue is empty.\\n\\nThis order guarantees that elements are processed in the sequence in which they arrived.\\n\\nWhere are queues used?\\n\\nQueues are widely used in programming and computer systems:\\n\\n- Task management: the schedulers of operating systems and web servers use queues to handle requests.\\n\\n- Asynchronous interaction: messaging systems (RabbitMQ, Kafka) are built on queues to buffer data between producers and consumers.\\n\\n- Graphical interfaces: events (for example, mouse clicks) are often handled through an event queue.\\n\\n- Algorithms: breadth-first search (BFS) in graphs uses a queue to hold the vertices still to be visited.\\n\\nAn implementation example in Python\\n\\nIn Python a queue can easily be implemented with a list, but for efficiency it is better to use the `deque` collection from the `collections` module.\\n\\nConclusion: A queue is worth using when the order of processing has to follow the \\"first in, first out\\" principle strictly. It is indispensable for buffering, managing streams of tasks and implementing algorithms such as BFS.","from collections import deque\\n\\n# Creating the queue\\nqueue = deque()\\n\\n# Adding elements (enqueue)\\nqueue.append(\\"Task 1\\")\\nqueue.append(\\"Task 2\\")\\nqueue.append(\\"Task 3\\")\\nprint(\\"The queue after the additions:\\", list(queue))  # [\'Task 1\', \'Task 2\', \'Task 3\']\\n\\n# Removing an element (dequeue) - the one added first leaves\\ncompleted_task = queue.popleft()\\nprint(\\"The task completed:\\", completed_task)  # Task 1\\nprint(\\"The queue after the removal:\\", list(queue))  # [\'Task 2\', \'Task 3\']\\n\\n# Inspecting the first element (peek)\\nnext_task = queue[0]\\nprint(\\"The next task:\\", next_task)  # Task 2"],[1789,"When the int range overflows in hashes, the result of the hash function\'s computation falls outside the permitted values of the int type. That can lead to unexpected negative numbers or to zero. As a result, keys that should have different hashes may get the same value, increasing the number of collisions. That degrades hash tables\' performance, since the chains in the buckets grow longer.","Integer overflow during the computation of a hash function happens when an intermediate or final result of an operation (for example, a multiplication or an addition) exceeds the maximum value the int data type can hold (for example, 2^31-1 for a 32-bit signed int). In programming languages where integer overflow raises no exception (as in C++, Java, Go), the result \\"wraps\\" modulo 2^n, which may give a negative number or an unexpectedly small positive one.\\n\\nHow this affects hashing\\n\\nA hash function converts a key (a string, an object) into an integer for fast access in a hash table. If overflow occurs during the computation, the resulting hash may become unpredictable:\\n\\n- Collisions: Different keys may get the same hash because the values \\"collapse\\" after overflow.\\n\\n- Distribution: The uniformity of the hashes\' distribution is broken, which leads to data clustering in particular buckets of the table.\\n\\n- Performance: In the worst case, searching, inserting and deleting in the hash table degrade to O(n) instead of the expected O(1).\\n\\nAn example in Java\\n\\nConsider a simplified hash function for a string that is prone to overflow:\\n\\nHow to avoid the problem\\n\\nModern languages and libraries use methods that minimise the risk:\\n\\n- Using unsigned types (uint in C#) or types with greater capacity (long).\\n\\n- Applying modular arithmetic with a large prime to limit the range.\\n\\n- Built-in hash functions (for example, Object.hashCode() in Java) already account for overflow, but it still happens — what matters is that the resulting distribution stays uniform.\\n\\nConclusion: Int overflow in hashes is not an error but a characteristic that has to be accounted for when designing hash functions. It is acceptable if the resulting distribution of hashes stays uniform and collisions are rare. For performance-critical applications it is worth using proven library implementations of hashing.","public int badHash(String s) {\\n    int hash = 0;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n        // Multiplying by 31 can cause overflow on long strings\\n        hash = hash * 31 + s.charAt(i);\\n    }\\n    return hash;\\n}\\n\\n// For a very long string, hash may overflow and become negative\\nString longString = \\"This is a very long string...\\";\\nSystem.out.println(badHash(longString)); // May print a negative number"],[1790,"A stack is a data structure that works on the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle, that is, the element added last is the first to be taken out. The main operations are push (adding an element to the top of the stack) and pop (removing the element from the top). A stack is often used to manage function calls, to implement undo, and for parsing (for example, checking brackets). Its implementation is simple and efficient.","A stack is a fundamental abstract data structure that organises elements in a vertical order, like a stack of plates. The key principle of its operation is LIFO (Last In, First Out), meaning the element added last will be the first removed. That distinguishes a stack from a queue (FIFO).\\nThe main operations\\nA stack supports two basic operations:\\n\\n- Push — adding a new element to the top of the stack.\\n- Pop — removing and returning the element from the top of the stack.\\nHelper operations are often available too, such as peek (looking at the top element without removing it) and isEmpty (checking whether the stack is empty).\\nWhere is a stack used?\\nA stack is not merely an academic concept; it is widely used in real systems:\\n\\n- Managing function calls: when a function is called, its context (arguments, local variables, the return address) is placed on the call stack. When the function finishes, its context is removed and control returns to the point of the call.\\n- Implementing undo: every user action (for example, in a text editor) is saved on a stack. The undo command takes the last action from the stack and reverses it.\\n- Parsing: checking the correctness of nested structures, such as brackets in an expression. Opening brackets are placed on the stack, and when a closing one is encountered it is checked against the top of the stack.\\n- Navigating history: browsers use a stack for the \\"Back\\" and \\"Forward\\" buttons.\\nAn implementation example in JavaScript\\nThe simplest implementation of a stack can be built on an array:\\n\\nIn this example the array\'s `push` and `pop` methods correspond perfectly to the stack\'s operations, since they work from the end of the array.\\nConclusion\\nA stack is a simple but powerful data structure worth applying in tasks that require elements to be processed in the reverse order of their arrival, or when you need a temporary buffer with fast access to the last element. Its efficiency and predictability make it indispensable in systems programming, compilers and algorithms.","class Stack {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.items = [];\\n  }\\n\\n  push(element) {\\n    this.items.push(element);\\n  }\\n\\n  pop() {\\n    if (this.isEmpty()) {\\n      return \'Stack is empty\';\\n    }\\n    return this.items.pop();\\n  }\\n\\n  peek() {\\n    return this.items[this.items.length - 1];\\n  }\\n\\n  isEmpty() {\\n    return this.items.length === 0;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst stack = new Stack();\\nstack.push(10);\\nstack.push(20);\\nconsole.log(stack.peek()); // 20\\nconsole.log(stack.pop());  // 20\\nconsole.log(stack.pop());  // 10"],[1791,"LIFO stands for Last In, First Out. It is a principle of organising data whereby the element added last is the first to be taken out. The classic example is a stack of plates: a new plate is put on top, and plates are taken from the top too. In programming, a stack working on LIFO is used to manage function calls, track operations and parse expressions.","The LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle describes an order of processing in which the element added last becomes the first to be removed or processed. It is the key concept behind the stack data structure.\\n\\nHow a stack works on the LIFO principle\\n\\nA stack can be pictured as a vertical collection of elements with two main operations:\\n\\n- Push — adding an element to the top of the stack.\\n\\n- Pop — removing the element from the top of the stack.\\n\\nThe element added last (the one at the top) will always be removed first. Accessing elements in the middle of the stack directly is impossible without removing the ones above.\\n\\nWhere LIFO is used\\n\\nThis principle is widely used in computer science:\\n\\n- The call stack: When a function is called, information about it (the return address, the local variables) is placed on the stack. When the function finishes, its context is removed and control returns to the previous function.\\n\\n- Memory management: Some memory-allocation models use a stack to hold local variables.\\n\\n- Parsing expressions: Stacks are used to convert infix expressions into postfix (reverse Polish) notation and to evaluate them.\\n\\n- Action history (undo/redo): In many applications undo operations are often implemented with a stack: the last action is the first undone.\\n\\nA code example: implementing a stack\\n\\nConsider a simple implementation of a stack in Python, demonstrating the LIFO principle:\\n\\nConclusion: The LIFO principle and the stack data structure are essential in situations requiring the reverse order of processing or the temporary storage of elements with priority to the most recently added, for example to manage function calls, to parse, or to implement an undo mechanism.","class Stack:\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.items = []\\n\\n    def push(self, item):\\n        # Add the element to the top (the end of the list)\\n        self.items.append(item)\\n\\n    def pop(self):\\n        # Remove and return the element from the top\\n        if not self.is_empty():\\n            return self.items.pop()\\n        return None\\n\\n    def peek(self):\\n        # Look at the element on top without removing it\\n        if not self.is_empty():\\n            return self.items[-1]\\n        return None\\n\\n    def is_empty(self):\\n        return len(self.items) == 0\\n\\n# Use\\nstack = Stack()\\nstack.push(\'A\')  # The first element\\nstack.push(\'B\')  # The second element\\nstack.push(\'C\')  # The third element (now on top)\\n\\nprint(stack.pop())  # Prints \'C\' (last in, first out)\\nprint(stack.pop())  # Prints \'B\'\\nprint(stack.pop())  # Prints \'A\'"],[1792,"An array is stored in memory as a contiguous sequence of elements of the same type. That means all the array\'s elements lie in memory one after another. Knowing the address of the first element (the pointer) and the size of each element, you can compute the address of any element from its index. This organisation provides fast random access (O(1)), but requires the whole block of memory to be allocated in advance.","An array is a fundamental data structure that in most programming languages is stored as a contiguous block of memory. That means all the array\'s elements lie in memory sequentially, one after another, at adjacent addresses.\\n\\nThe principle and computing an address\\n\\nThe key idea is that to access an element by index you do not have to walk through all the previous elements. It is enough to know:\\n\\n- The base address: the address in memory where the array\'s first element is located (usually the value of the pointer to the array).\\n\\n- The size of one element: the number of bytes each element occupies (for example, 4 bytes for a 32-bit integer).\\n\\n- The index: the position of the element required, starting from 0.\\n\\nAn element\'s address is computed by the formula: `Address = Base address + (Index * Element size)`. That operation is performed very quickly and is the basis of random access with a time complexity of O(1).\\n\\nAn example in C\\n\\nConsider a simple example demonstrating the layout of the elements in memory:\\n\\nIn the program\'s output you will see that the elements\' addresses increase by the same amount (usually 4 bytes for `int` on 32-bit systems), which confirms the contiguous storage.\\n\\nWhere this applies and the important consequences\\n\\nContiguous storage of arrays is widely used and has several important practical consequences:\\n\\n- Cache efficiency: thanks to the locality of the data, the processor loads several adjacent array elements into the cache at once, which speeds up iteration.\\n\\n- Limitations: the array\'s size has to be known in advance (a static array) or allocated dynamically, but in a single block. Inserting or removing an element in the middle of an array requires shifting all the subsequent elements, which is expensive (O(n)).\\n\\n- The link with pointers: in languages like C an array\'s name is often \\"decayed\\" into a pointer to its first element, and pointer arithmetic uses the address formula directly.\\n\\nConclusion: Storing an array as a contiguous block of memory is the classic approach, providing maximum speed of access by index and high performance when processing data sequentially. It is worth using when the size of the collection is known or changes rarely and the main operations are reading and writing by index rather than frequent insertions/removals.","#include <stdio.h>\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\\n    \\n    // Print the elements\' addresses\\n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\\n        printf(\\"arr[%d] = %d, address: %p\\\\n\\", i, arr[i], (void*)&arr[i]);\\n    }\\n    \\n    // The size of one element (in bytes)\\n    printf(\\"The size of int: %zu bytes\\\\n\\", sizeof(int));\\n    \\n    return 0;\\n}"],[1793,"A collision in a hash table arises when two different key-value pairs receive the same index after the hash function is applied. That happens because the hash function maps a set of possible keys onto a limited number of buckets in the table. To resolve collisions, methods such as chaining (separate lists in each bucket) or open addressing (looking for another free bucket) are used. Understanding collisions matters for working efficiently with hash tables, since they affect the performance of insertion and lookup.","A collision in a hash table is a situation where two different key-value pairs receive the same index (hash) after the hash function is applied. Since a hash function converts keys of arbitrary length into a fixed range of indices (for example, from 0 to the table\'s size), it is inevitable that different keys may produce the same result. This is a fundamental problem of hashing, and resolving it is critical for the data structure to work correctly.\\n\\nWhy do collisions arise?\\n\\nHash functions aim to distribute keys evenly across the table\'s buckets, but because the table\'s size is limited and the set of keys potentially infinite, avoiding collisions entirely is impossible. For example, if a hash function for a string returns the sum of the character codes modulo 10, then the strings \\"ab\\" and \\"ba\\" may give the same hash, which leads to a collision.\\n\\nMethods of resolving collisions\\n\\nThere are two main approaches to handling collisions:\\n\\n- Separate chaining: Every bucket in the table holds a linked list (or another structure) of all the elements that landed in that bucket. On a collision the new element is added to the list. A lookup requires walking the list in the bucket.\\n\\n- Open addressing: All the elements are stored directly in the table\'s array. On a collision the algorithm looks for the next free bucket according to a particular probe sequence (for example, linear probing, quadratic probing, double hashing).\\n\\nA code example with chaining\\n\\nWhere are hash tables with collision handling used?\\n\\nHash tables are widely used in programming to implement associative arrays, sets, caches and databases. For example, in Python dictionaries (dict) use open addressing to resolve collisions, while in Java HashMap uses chaining. Understanding collisions helps you choose or tune hash functions and the table\'s size to optimise performance.\\n\\nConclusion: Collisions are an inevitable part of hashing, and knowing the methods of resolving them (chaining or open addressing) lets you design and use hash tables effectively in real applications, especially where the speed of access to data matters.","class HashTable:\\n    def __init__(self, size):\\n        self.size = size\\n        self.table = [[] for _ in range(size)]  # A list of lists for the chains\\n\\n    def hash_function(self, key):\\n        return hash(key) % self.size  # A simple hash function\\n\\n    def insert(self, key, value):\\n        index = self.hash_function(key)\\n        # Check whether such a key is already in the chain\\n        for pair in self.table[index]:\\n            if pair[0] == key:\\n                pair[1] = value  # Update the value\\n                return\\n        # If the key is absent, add a new pair\\n        self.table[index].append([key, value])\\n\\n    def get(self, key):\\n        index = self.hash_function(key)\\n        for pair in self.table[index]:\\n            if pair[0] == key:\\n                return pair[1]\\n        return None  # The key was not found\\n\\n# Use\\nht = HashTable(10)\\nht.insert(\\"apple\\", 5)\\nht.insert(\\"banana\\", 7)\\n# Suppose \\"apple\\" and \\"orange\\" collided (the same index)\\nht.insert(\\"orange\\", 9)  # It is added to the same list as \\"apple\\"\\nprint(ht.get(\\"orange\\"))  # Prints 9"],[1794,"In most languages the int type has a limited size, for example 32 or 64 bits. That means a hash function returning an int can generate only a finite number of unique values (2^32 or 2^64). If the number of inputs exceeds that, collisions are inevitable — different data will receive the same hash. Overflow may also occur during the computations inside the hash function, distorting the result. It is therefore important either to use a type with sufficient capacity or to apply methods that minimise the risk of overflow.","Hashing is the process of converting data of arbitrary size into a value of fixed length, called a hash. When implementing hash functions an integer data type such as `int` is often used to hold the intermediate results and the final hash. The size of the `int` type, however, imposes key limitations that affect the correctness and security of the hashing.\\n\\nThe limit on the number of unique hashes\\n\\nThe `int` type usually has a fixed size, for example 32 bits (which gives a range from roughly -2.1 billion to +2.1 billion) or 64 bits. That means a hash function can generate at most 2^32 or 2^64 different values. If the number of possible inputs (for example, strings) exceeds that, then by the pigeonhole principle collisions will inevitably arise — situations where different inputs correspond to the same hash. For cryptographic hashes that is critical, since it reduces security. For ordinary hash tables it increases the number of collisions and degrades performance.\\n\\nThe risk of integer overflow\\n\\nComputing a hash often involves arithmetic operations (multiplication, addition, shifts). If an intermediate result exceeds the maximum value for the `int` type, overflow occurs. In languages like C/C++ that leads to undefined behaviour (UB); in Java to a wrap-around; in Python the type automatically widens to a long. Overflow can distort the hash, making it unpredictable or increasing the likelihood of collisions.\\n\\nA code example with potential overflow\\n\\nIn this example the operation `hash * prime` can easily exceed the bounds of a 32-bit `int` for long strings, causing overflow. To avoid that you can use an unsigned type of greater capacity (for example, `uint64_t`) or apply modular arithmetic with a large prime modulus, bearing the limitations in mind.\\n\\nPractical consequences and uses\\n\\n- Hash tables: The hash\'s size determines the number of buckets. The hash is usually reduced modulo the table\'s size, so even a 32-bit hash is enough for most applications, but overflow during the computation has to be controlled.\\n\\n- Cryptography: Cryptographic hash functions (SHA-256) return a hash of greater size (256 bits), to make collisions practically improbable. Using a standard `int` is inapplicable here.\\n\\n- Language specifics: In Python integers are unbounded, so there is no overflow. In Java you can use `long` (64 bits) to reduce the risk. In C++ it is worth using `uint64_t` and checking the bounds explicitly.\\n\\nConclusion: The limitations on `int`\'s size affect the uniqueness of hashes and the safety of the operations. When designing hash functions you have to choose a data type with enough bits for the expected volume of data and prevent overflow, or use ready-made library functions that account for these subtleties.","// A simplified hash function for a string (a 32-bit int)\\nint hashString(const char* str) {\\n    int hash = 0;\\n    int prime = 31;\\n    while (*str) {\\n        // Potential overflow with large strings!\\n        hash = hash * prime + (*str);\\n        str++;\\n    }\\n    return hash;\\n}"],[1795,"An array in the classic sense (for example, in C) is a contiguous block of memory of fixed size, allocated when it is created. You cannot simply \\"extend\\" that block, because the memory addresses following it may already be occupied by other data. To change the size you have to allocate a new, larger block of memory, copy the old elements into it and free the old block. That is exactly how dynamic arrays work (for example, ArrayList in Java or vector in C++).","A classic array is a fundamental data structure representing a contiguous sequence of memory cells of the same type. When a program allocates memory for an array (for example, `int arr[10];`), the operating system reserves one solid block of a particular size for it. That block has a fixed starting address (a pointer) and a length.\\n\\nWhy is the size fixed?\\n\\n- The contiguity of the memory: An array\'s elements lie in memory one after another. That provides fast access by index in O(1), since any element\'s address is computed by the formula: `start_address + index * element_size`.\\n\\n- The limitations of memory allocation: The memory after the end of an allocated block may be occupied by other variables, data structures or even another program. The operating system cannot \\"push apart\\" a block that has already been allocated without affecting the neighbouring data.\\n\\n- Static allocation: At compile time, or at the moment of the declaration, the size is often fixed. Even with dynamic allocation through `malloc` or `new`, the size is specified once and cannot be changed for that particular block.\\n\\nHow are \\"growable\\" arrays implemented then?\\n\\nDynamic arrays (for example, `std::vector` in C++, `ArrayList` in Java, lists in Python) solve this problem using a reallocation strategy. Internally they hold an ordinary array (a pointer to a block of memory) and track two quantities: the capacity (the size of the allocated block) and the size (the number of elements actually stored).\\n\\nWhen an element is added and the current array is full, the following happens:\\n\\n- A new, larger block of memory is allocated (often 1.5 to 2 times larger).\\n\\n- All the existing elements are copied into the new block.\\n\\n- The old block of memory is freed.\\n\\n- The structure\'s internal pointer starts referring to the new block.\\n\\nWhere does this apply?\\n\\nUnderstanding this mechanism is critical when working with systems sensitive to performance and memory. For example:\\n\\n- When developing games or embedded systems, where frequent reallocations can cause memory fragmentation or FPS drops.\\n\\n- When optimising algorithms, so as to reserve enough capacity in advance and avoid expensive copying.\\n\\n- When writing low-level code in C/C++, where memory management lies entirely with the developer.\\n\\nSummary: You cannot dynamically increase the size of an array\'s original block of memory because of the requirement for contiguity and the limitations of the memory manager. The problem is solved, however, by the dynamic-array abstractions, which under the hood allocate a new block and copy the data, providing the illusion of growability. Use the dynamic arrays from the standard libraries for most tasks, but remember the cost of the insertion operation, which in the worst case becomes O(n) because of the copying.","// A simplified illustration of reallocation\\nclass DynamicArray {\\n    private int[] data;\\n    private int size;\\n    private int capacity;\\n\\n    public void add(int element) {\\n        if (size == capacity) { // There is no room\\n            int newCapacity = capacity * 2; // Increase the capacity (usually twofold)\\n            int[] newData = new int[newCapacity]; // Allocate a new, larger block\\n            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) { // Copy the old elements\\n                newData[i] = data[i];\\n            }\\n            data = newData; // Point the pointer at the new block\\n            capacity = newCapacity;\\n            // The old block will be removed by the garbage collector (or manually in C++)\\n        }\\n        data[size] = element;\\n        size++;\\n    }\\n}"],[1796,"The garbage collector (GC) runs automatically when the system needs to free memory on the heap. The exact moment depends on the language\'s implementation and on the algorithm used. The trigger is often a shortage of free memory or the reaching of a certain threshold of allocated objects. A programmer usually does not control this process directly but can influence it by clearing references to objects that are no longer needed.","Garbage collection (GC) is an automatic memory-management process that frees the memory occupied by objects the program no longer uses. The GC is usually started by the runtime (for example, the Java virtual machine, or the V8 JavaScript engine) and depends on the particular implementation and algorithm.\\n\\nThe main triggers for the GC running\\n\\n- Memory shortage (heap pressure): When the application requests the allocation of a new object and there is not enough free space on the heap.\\n\\n- Reaching a threshold: Some collectors (for example, in .NET) run when a particular generation of objects (Gen 0, Gen 1, Gen 2) reaches a certain size.\\n\\n- An explicit call: In some languages (for example, Java, C#) you can explicitly request that the collector run, through `System.gc()` or `GC.Collect()`, but that is considered bad practice and is usually ignored or not performed immediately.\\n\\n- Pauses in the application\'s work: In some environments a collection may run at predefined idle moments.\\n\\nAn example in JavaScript (the V8 engine)\\n\\nIn JavaScript a developer does not manage memory directly. The V8 engine uses a combined algorithm (generational and incremental mark-sweep). A collection runs when the heap is nearly full.\\n\\nPractical aspects\\n\\nUnderstanding the GC\'s triggers helps you write code that minimises pauses (stop-the-world) and memory leaks. For example, in high-load services it is important to avoid creating a large number of short-lived objects, so as not to provoke frequent collections in the younger generation (minor GC).\\n\\nConclusion: The garbage collector is a background service of the runtime that runs automatically as the need arises. Knowing how it works lets you write more efficient, responsive applications, especially in systems with strict requirements for performance and memory consumption.","function createLargeObjects() {\\n  let arr = [];\\n  // Create many objects, filling the heap\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {\\n    arr.push({ index: i });\\n  }\\n  // When the function finishes, arr goes out of scope.\\n  // The objects become unreachable and can be collected by the GC.\\n}\\ncreateLargeObjects();\\n// At some point after this function runs\\n// the engine may start a garbage collection to free the memory."],[1797,"When strings are concatenated in a loop, in most languages (Java, C#, Python before 3.11) many intermediate string objects are created, since strings are immutable. Every iteration creates a new string by copying the contents of the previous one and appending the new part. That leads to quadratic time complexity, O(n²), and excessive memory use. For efficiency you should use special classes, such as StringBuilder in Java or join() in Python.","Concatenating strings in a loop is the classic example of a potential performance problem, especially in languages where strings are immutable objects. Understanding this mechanism matters for writing efficient code.\\n\\nWhy is it inefficient?\\n\\nIn languages such as Java, C# and older versions of Python, strings are immutable. That means a string cannot be changed once created. The operation `str1 + str2` does not modify the existing strings but creates a completely new object in memory holding the result of joining them. In a loop that process repeats on every iteration.\\n\\nImagine a loop assembling a string from 1000 parts:\\n\\nOn the first iteration a string of about 5 characters is created. On the second, those 5 characters are copied and new ones appended, creating a string of about 10 characters, and so on. The total amount of data copied grows quadratically (O(n²)), which is extremely inefficient for large n.\\n\\nHow should strings be assembled?\\n\\nSpecial mutable structures exist to solve this problem:\\n\\n- Java, C#: Use `StringBuilder` (or `StringBuffer` for multithreading). That object holds the characters in a mutable buffer and performs the final assembly of the string in a single `toString()` call.\\n\\n- Python: Use the `join()` method on a list of strings, or, in modern versions (3.11+), concatenation in a loop is optimised by the interpreter.\\n\\n- JavaScript: Use an array and the `join()` method, or template strings in the loop (modern engines optimise concatenation well).\\n\\nAn example in Java with `StringBuilder`:\\n\\nThis approach has linear complexity, O(n), and saves a great deal of memory.\\n\\nWhere does this apply?\\n\\nUnderstanding this problem is critical when generating large texts: HTML/XML, SQL queries, logs, JSON or any dynamic messages in high-load applications. Inefficient concatenation can become a bottleneck.\\n\\nConclusion: Avoid simple string concatenation in loops, especially with a large number of iterations. Use specialised tools instead, such as `StringBuilder`, `join()` or arrays, to ensure linear performance and reduce the load on the garbage collector.","String result = \\"\\";\\nfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n    result = result + \\"part\\" + i; // A new string is created on every iteration!\\n}\\n\\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\\nfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n    sb.append(\\"part\\").append(i);\\n}\\nString result = sb.toString(); // The string is created only once!"],[1798,"Null represents the absence of a value, but using it often leads to runtime errors such as a NullPointerException. It breaks the principles of type safety, since a variable can be either an object or null. The code becomes overloaded with null checks, which makes it harder to read and maintain. Some languages, for example Kotlin, have introduced nullable types to solve this problem.","The concept of null was introduced in programming to denote the absence of a value for a reference or pointer. Although that seems convenient, its widespread use gives rise to a number of serious problems that can cause applications to crash and make debugging difficult.\\nThe main problems with null\\n\\n- Runtime errors (NullPointerException/NPE): The most frequent problem is an attempt to call a method on, or access a property of, a null reference, which makes the program crash immediately.\\n- Broken type safety: If a language lets any reference be null, then the type system cannot guarantee that a variable contains a valid object, which weakens static analysis.\\n- Code polluted with checks: Developers are forced to add checks constantly (if (obj != null)), which increases the amount of code, makes it less readable and can mask logical errors.\\n- Ambiguity of meaning: Null can mean different things: \\"the value was not found\\", \\"the value is not initialised yet\\" or \\"the value is deliberately absent\\", which makes the code harder to understand.\\nA code example in Java\\n\\nApproaches to the solution\\nModern languages and paradigms offer alternatives. For example, using the Optional pattern (Java, TypeScript) or built-in nullable types (Kotlin, TypeScript with strictNullChecks). These approaches force the developer to handle the case of an absent value explicitly at the level of the types, which prevents many errors.\\nConclusion: Problems with null are a classic source of errors. Using language facilities (Optional, nullable types) or strict coding conventions helps make a program more predictable and resilient. Knowing about these problems matters especially when designing APIs and libraries\' public methods.","public class UserService {\\n    public String getUserName(User user) {\\n        // A potential problem: if user == null, the call to getProfile() will crash.\\n        Profile profile = user.getProfile();\\n        return profile.getName(); // And here too, if profile == null.\\n    }\\n    // Safer but more cluttered code:\\n    public String getUserNameSafe(User user) {\\n        if (user != null) {\\n            Profile profile = user.getProfile();\\n            if (profile != null) {\\n                return profile.getName();\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return \\"Unknown\\";\\n    }\\n}"],[1799,"Yes, null can be passed into a method if the method\'s parameter has a reference type. In Java, C#, Kotlin and similar languages that is permitted by default. If the method does not expect null, however, it can cause a NullPointerException (or an equivalent error). To handle null you need to use checks (if (param == null)), annotations (@Nullable, @NotNull), use Optional (in Java) or use languages with null safety (Kotlin, TypeScript in strict mode).","Passing `null` into a method is a widespread operation in languages with reference types, but it requires careful handling to avoid failures.\\nHow null is passed\\nIn languages such as Java, C#, JavaScript (for objects) or Python (where `None` is the analogue of null) you can pass null as an argument if the method\'s parameter is a reference type (not a primitive). For example, in Java `String` or `Object` can accept null.\\nHandling null in a method\\nIf a method is not intended to work with null, then accessing the properties or methods of the object passed in will raise an exception. The standard approach is defensive programming:\\n\\n- Check the argument at the start of the method: `if (param == null) { ... }`\\n- Return a default value or throw an exception (for example, `IllegalArgumentException`).\\n- Use annotations (for example, `@Nullable` and `@NotNull` in IntelliJ or Lombok) to document the contract.\\n- In modern languages, built-in null-safety facilities are used.\\nAn example in Java\\n\\nModern approaches\\nIn Kotlin types do not permit null by default, and `?` is used for nullable types (for example, `String?`), which forces the developer to handle a possible null explicitly. In Java 8+ you can use `Optional` to denote a possibly absent value explicitly. In TypeScript with `strictNullChecks` enabled, the compiler checks for null/undefined being passed.\\nConclusion: You can pass null, but it requires explicit handling inside the method, or the use of the language\'s null-safety facilities, for the code to be reliable and comprehensible. Apply defensive checks or modern typed approaches, especially in public APIs.","public class Example {\\n    // A method that handles null\\n    public String processName(String name) {\\n        if (name == null) {\\n            return \\"Guest\\"; // The default value\\n        }\\n        return \\"Hello, \\" + name.toUpperCase();\\n    }\\n\\n    // A method that forbids null (it throws an exception)\\n    public int calculateLength(String text) {\\n        if (text == null) {\\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\\"Text cannot be null\\");\\n        }\\n        return text.length();\\n    }\\n}"],[1800,"When an empty array is passed into a method that iterates (for example, forEach, map, filter), the method simply performs no iterations, since the array has no elements. The method returns its default value: forEach returns undefined, map returns a new empty array, and filter likewise returns a new empty array. This is safe behaviour that raises no errors, but it is important to account for when processing the result further.","Passing an empty array into a method intended to work with collections is a widespread scenario that tests how resilient the code is to edge cases. The behaviour depends on the particular method, but the general principle for most iterative methods in JavaScript is that there are no errors and a predictable result is returned.\\n\\nThe behaviour of the standard array methods\\n\\nLet\'s look at several key methods:\\n\\n- forEach: Does not run the callback function at all and returns `undefined`.\\n\\n- map: Returns a new empty array of the same length (0).\\n\\n- filter: Returns a new empty array, since no element satisfies the condition (the condition is not even checked).\\n\\n- reduce: Raises a `TypeError` if no initialValue is supplied. With an initialValue it returns that initialValue.\\n\\n- some / every: Return `false` (some — there are no truthy elements; every — every element is truthy for an empty array).\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nThe following code demonstrates the behaviour:\\n\\nPractical use and its importance\\n\\nUnderstanding this behaviour is critical when chaining method calls. For example, after filtering an empty array you get an empty array, and a subsequent `map` over it also safely returns an empty array without raising errors. That lets you write declarative, safe code for processing data that may be absent.\\n\\nConclusion: Passing an empty array into iterative methods (except `reduce` without an initial value) is safe and predictable. This behaviour should be used to handle optional data, avoiding unnecessary emptiness checks before calling the method, but always bearing the return value in mind (especially for `reduce`).","const emptyArr = [];\\n\\n// forEach\\nconsole.log(emptyArr.forEach(x => console.log(x))); // undefined, nothing was printed\\n\\n// map\\nconst mapped = emptyArr.map(x => x * 2);\\nconsole.log(mapped); // []\\nconsole.log(mapped.length); // 0\\n\\n// filter\\nconst filtered = emptyArr.filter(x => x > 5);\\nconsole.log(filtered); // []\\n\\n// reduce with an initialValue\\nconst sumWithInit = emptyArr.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val, 0);\\nconsole.log(sumWithInit); // 0\\n\\n// reduce without an initialValue (AN ERROR)\\n// const sumNoInit = emptyArr.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val); // TypeError"]]');export{e as default};